{"parentUuid":"85b9e968-7023-4f05-b7c2-0581c42f8368","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"cd7a4cde-2781-465b-b264-9d057dc89d63","agentId":"a943b4c4c92ae7c73","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0175QL5ZewU1JTyPbBZGVbHg","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Review: Supertraining Ch 5a (5.1 to 5.2.3), independent falsification pass\n2\t\n3\tReviewer: Claude Opus (independent of the writer). Date: 2026-10-03.\n4\tScope: the 19 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch5a.txt`, folder `staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`.\n5\tSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx 266-278 (printed 255-267). Every page in range was rendered to `staging/_renders/r5a_p266.png` ... `r5a_p278.png` and opened as an image. Every equation, table, figure and number cited below was read from the image, not the text layer.\n6\tBaseline: every \"Eric's baseline:\" quote checked against `docs/method-baseline.md` at the cited line (lines 61, 67, 73, 76, 78, 79, 96, 97, 101, 103, 104, 105, 107, 130, 134, 141, 181, 182, 188, 260). All quotes and line numbers match. Tension 16 is correctly named at line 260.\n7\tLinks: every `[[wikilink]]` in the 19 files resolves to a file in the vault library or the staging library. Claims the entries make about linked entries were spot-checked (Bischke & Morehouse in the PAP entry; breath-holding in the isometric drawbacks entry; Moyer wearable-resistance entry \"wrong in three separate ways\"; Bondarchuk leg-to-leg 50 m \"all levels\"; Vasiliev uniform-gain in the threshold entry; APRE named in the isokinetic entry). All hold.\n8\tDuplicates: searched the vault library and all staging libraries (including the 5b and 5c files in the same folder) for impulse-momentum, DeLorme, APRE/DAPRE, Berger, Ivanov 1966, Lelikov, analytical-synthetic, cross training, multi-faceted, Rogener, Egstrom, Zubov, thigh cuffs, Table 5.3, Krusen, Narikashvili, 6SM, \"20% of 1RM\", \"independent qualities\", \"literally copying\". No entry in scope restates an existing entry without a new angle. The Newton entry reuses Fig 5.1 (already atomized from Ch 2.2.2) but says so and adds the impulse-momentum angle.\n9\t\n10\tCounts: **GO 11, FIX 8, NO-GO 0.**\n11\t\n12\t## Per-entry verdicts\n13\t\n14\t| file | verdict | problem (entry vs source, with page) | exact correction |\n15\t|---|---|---|---|\n16\t| claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md | FIX | `proven` is correctly scoped to the impulse-momentum identity, and (a), (b), (c) are correct physics. Three physics sentences are imprecise. (1) The entry quotes the book's \"I = F.ΔT = m.ΔV\" and \"the impulse may be calculated from the change in momentum, m.ΔV\" without flagging that the book defines ΔV as \"The final velocity reached at the end of this phase\" with the integral running from 0 (PDF 267 / p.256, image). m times a final velocity equals the change in momentum only if the body starts from rest. (2) When to apply: \"a driver metric such as propulsive impulse to the output metric (take-off velocity, hence jump height)\". Only the net impulse (vertical GRF minus body weight) fixes take-off velocity; GRF impulse alone does not. (3) When NOT (d): \"constant-speed circular motion has a constant-size force that only turns\". A force that turns is a changing force vector, so this does not counter the book's \"associated with a change in force\"; it confirms it. (c) also says \"net force on a free body\", which is loose. | (1) Add to When NOT as (e): \"Inference: the book defines ΔV as the final velocity of the phase and integrates from 0, so m.ΔV equals the change in momentum only when the body starts from rest; in general impulse = m(v_final - v_initial).\" (2) Replace \"propulsive impulse\" with \"net propulsive impulse (vertical ground reaction force minus body weight, integrated over the propulsive phase)\". (3) In (d), replace the circular-motion example with: \"a projectile under gravity changes direction continuously while the force on it stays constant, so a change in velocity direction does not require a change in force, only a net force.\" In (c), replace \"net force on a free body\" with \"net external force on the body\". Keep `proven` with its existing scope. |\n17\t| principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities.md | GO | none | checked: \"totally independent qualities\", \"does not necessarily enhance\", \"(Verkhoshansky, 1963)\", \"inversely proportional to one another (see Ch 4)\" at PDF 268 / p.257 (image); Example labelled Inference; baseline line 101 quote and Explicit Q1 tag; `supported` appropriate. Suggest reciprocal link to `framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation` (5.4, transfer weakens with qualification). |\n18\t| heuristic--analytical-synthetic-and-multifaceted-preparation-suit-only-early-training.md | GO | none | checked: analytical-synthetic definition, gymnasts/weightlifters sprints and barbell for track (Verkhoshansky 1977), \"appropriate only for the initial stages ... serious error for highly-qualified athletes to use them casually\", \"partly true and only under certain circumstances\", Yakovlev 1968 / Zimkin 1965 / Zatsiorsky 1965, \"inevitably opposes the law of gradual development\", cross training \"(see 5.5)\", \"sequentiality, simultaneity or scheme of superposition\" all at PDF 268 / p.257 (image); baseline line 182; Ch 2 counter-quote \"positively influences the development of the others\" present in the linked entry. Suggest reciprocal link to the 5.5 entry `heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label`. |\n19\t| heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement.md | FIX | (1) Location wrong. Entry: \"The paragraph before 5.1's dynamics discussion sets this up\". The neuromuscular-system paragraph (\"with the speed necessary to produce the required motor effect ... requires specialised means of training\") closes the dynamics discussion: it follows Fig 5.1 at the foot of PDF 267 / p.256, and \"All subsequent training and performance errors are caused by inappropriate neuromuscular programming\" opens PDF 268 / p.257 (image). (2) Unlabelled writer judgment and application. When to apply: \"This passage is the authors' own statement of that 'not surface resemblance' rule. Use it when a coach asks for a loaded version of a sport skill (a weighted throwing motion, a banded route stem, ...)\" has no \"Inference:\" in that paragraph. | (1) Replace \"The paragraph before 5.1's dynamics discussion sets this up\" with \"The paragraph that closes 5.1's dynamics discussion, after Fig 5.1, sets this up\". (2) Prefix \"This passage is the authors' own statement ...\" with \"Inference:\". All quotes otherwise verified at PDF 268 / p.257 and PDF 277-278 / p.266-267 (image). |\n20\t| framework--special-strength-methods-classified-by-sport-group-and-strength-type.md | GO | none | checked: four sport categories (Diachkov 1961), strength types with Diachkov / Kuznetsov / Chudinov, three principles incl. \"(Verkhoshansky, 1970)\", maximal tension / speed-strength / strength-endurance loads, \"repetitive, maximal and dynamic effort (Zatsiorsky, 1966)\", five method groups, \"(see 3.1)\" at PDF 269 / p.258 (image); section headings 5.2.1 at PDF 269, 5.2.3 at PDF 275, 5.2.4 at PDF 278, 5.2.5 at PDF 296 (text-layer search); baseline line 188. Football-as-group-4 is inside an Inference sentence. |\n21\t| framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md | FIX | `contested` is justified and all four contradiction pairs are on the cited pages (image): sets \"more than three (Berger, 1962)\" PDF 271 / p.260 vs \"reducing the number of sets and repetitions\" PDF 272 / p.261; \"larger training effect\" PDF 271 / p.260 vs \"Similar significant increases\" PDF 272 / p.261; Salter 1955 PDF 271 / p.260 and Rasch & Morehouse PDF 272 / p.261 vs the same \"similar\" sentence; 5RM \"about 89%\" PDF 269 / p.258, \"90-95% of 1RM ... (3-6)\" PDF 272 / p.261, \"85-95% of 1RM, i.e. 3-5RM\" PDF 271 / p.260. Two problems. (1) Item (4) overstates: \"These three are not consistent\". The clear clash is 90-95% with up to 6 reps against 5RM = 89% (6 reps must sit below 89%). \"85-95% i.e. 3-5RM\" puts the 5RM near 85% against 89%, a loose mismatch only. (2) When to apply has a sentence fragment carrying a baseline quote: \"The baseline's off-season rule (\"relative strength and technique first; ...\", line 105, course material, Inferred).\" | (1) Replace \"These three are not consistent; do not use any one as a rep-to-load conversion.\" with \"The 90-95% with 3-6 reps pairing clearly clashes with 5RM = 89% (six reps cannot be done at 90% or more if five reps is 89%); the 85-95% = 3-5RM pairing is only loosely off (it implies a 5RM near 85%). Do not use any of them as a rep-to-load conversion.\" (2) Rewrite the fragment as: \"Eric's baseline: off-season strength work prioritizes 'relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work' (line 105, Inferred, transcript and slides).\" Also verified: Kabat 1947 / Hellebrant & Houtz 1956 at PDF 269 (image; text layer reads \"Rabat\"); Medvedev 1967 / Vorobyev 1971 \"5-6 exercises for 6-10 sets of 1-3\" at PDF 271; baseline lines 61, 105, 260. |\n22\t| claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared.md | FIX | Unlabelled claim not on the page. When NOT: \"All the studies are 1945-1960 work, mostly in rehabilitation or untrained subjects.\" PDF 269-270 / p.258-259 gives no subject populations for Faulkner, McMorris & Elks or Krusen; only McMorris & Elks is qualified (\"particularly with advanced athletes\"). | Replace with: \"All the studies are 1945-1960 work; the book gives no subject populations for them. Inference: the DeLorme lineage began in rehabilitation, so untrained or patient samples are likely, but the page does not say so.\" Otherwise verified (image): 89% / 74% and the DeLorme 50%-75%-100% of 10RM (Hettinger 1961) at PDF 269 / p.258; Oxford/Zinovieff and McCloy sequences, \"add nothing new\", 154% vs 142%, 212.8% vs 186% (Faulkner 1950), 5.5%, McMorris & Elks 1954, Krusen 1949 25/50/75% vs 5RM/125%/150% at PDF 270 / p.259; Narikashvili 1960 at PDF 271 / p.260; baseline line 107. |\n23\t| claim--berger-sets-reps-and-frequency-studies-in-novices.md | FIX | When NOT misstates traceability. Entry: \"conclusion 1 speaks of 'maximal weights three times per week,' which is none of the five listed programmes, and conclusions 3, 5 and 6 refer to data the page does not describe.\" Source PDF 270 / p.259 (image), Berger 1963 paragraph: \"with maximal weights (10RM for one set three times per week)\" and \"There was also no major difference between the effectiveness of 2 or 3 training sessions per week.\" So conclusion 1's comparator and conclusion 5 trace to the Berger 1963 sentences, and conclusion 3 (3RM to 10RM) is loosely backed by the 2RM/5RM/10RM and 3 x 6RM results. Only conclusion 6 has no data on the page. Minor: the separate p.259 sentence that \"most weightlifting training is done with 1-3 repetitions for advanced weightlifters ... especially where strength-skill is involved\" is missing from the advanced-lifter contrast. | Replace the sentence with: \"The six conclusions are drawn partly from data outside the five-programme list: conclusion 1's 'maximal weights three times per week' and conclusion 5 trace to the Berger (1963) sentences ('10RM for one set three times per week'; 'no major difference between ... 2 or 3 training sessions per week', PDF 270 / p.259), conclusion 3 loosely to the 2RM/5RM/10RM and 3 x 6RM results, and conclusion 6 to no data on the page.\" Add after the Vorobyev (1971) clause: \"and 'other studies' show most weightlifting training for advanced lifters is done with 1-3 repetitions, 'emphasizing the importance of 1RM, 2RM and 3RM loads ... especially where strength-skill is involved' (PDF 270 / p.259).\" Otherwise verified (image): 3 x 6RM (Berger 1962), Keipen 1956, Vasiliev 1954 rest, five programmes and \"only ... the fifth one\", conclusions 1-6, italic \"these conclusions were based on research with untrained subjects\", Vorobyev 1978 \"1-6\" at PDF 270-271 / p.259-260. |\n24\t| claim--moderate-and-mixed-movement-tempo-for-repetition-strength-gains.md | GO | none | checked (image, PDF 271 / p.260): Vorobyev 1978 tempo sentence; 30 vs 15 sessions; \"22.6 ± 0.6 kg; and 16.3 ± 0.5 kg\" (Lelikov 1975); Vasiliev 1954 equal work at 20/40/60/80% and 45 lifts/min; \"maximal tempo became less effective as the load was increased\"; 20% exception. Overlap with `claim--strength-training-threshold-stimulus` correctly declared (that entry holds the uniform-gain point; this one adds tempo). Baseline line 181. |\n25\t| claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves.md | FIX | Title contradicts the data and the entry's own body. Title: \"Concentric, Eccentric and Isometric Training Each Improve Their Own Test Most\". Source PDF 272 / p.261 (image): there is no eccentric test; eccentric work gave the largest squat 1RM gain (15 kg), isometric the largest isometric gain (30.2 kg), and only concentric raised the vertical jump (3.7 cm). The entry's When NOT already notes \"a squat 1RM is decided by its concentric phase, yet eccentric training raised it most\". Minor: Why it works glosses the book's phrase with \"each mode trains the nervous system for that mode\", which the book does not say. | Retitle: \"Ivanov (1966): Isometric Training Raised Isometric Strength Most, Eccentric Training Raised the Squat 1RM Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump\" (rename file to match, e.g. `claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves.md`, and update the one inbound link, in the three-methods entry). In Why it works, prefix the gloss \"each mode trains the nervous system for that mode\" with \"Inference:\". Numbers verified (image): 15, 9.2, 30.2 (text layer \"iJO.2\"), 14.6, 3.7, -1.6, -5.4 cm; baseline line 76 and its contested tag. |\n26\t| framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise.md | GO | none | checked: DAPRE (Knight 1979) sets at PDF 272 / p.261; APRE 3RM/6RM/10RM labels, 2-4 min rest, Table 5.1 every cell at PDF 273 / p.262 (image); Table 5.2 every cell at PDF 274 / p.263 (image): 6RM 0-2 decr 2.5-5, 3-4 decr 0-2.5, 5-7 same, 8-12 incr 2.5-5, 13+ incr 5-7.5; 10RM 4-6 decr 2.5-5, 7-8 decr 0-2.5, 9-11 same, 12-16 incr 2.5-5, 17+ incr 5-7.5; 3RM 1-2 decr 2.5-5, 3-4 same, 5-6 incr 2.5-5, 7+ incr 5-10. \"6-8 weeks\" and ballistic-end quote at PDF 274. `contested` stated reason holds: \"more than 6 ... must be increased\" PDF 273 / p.262 vs 5-7 \"leave same\" PDF 274 / p.263, both on the cited pages. Example labelled Inference and consistent with the table. Baseline lines 61, 130, 67. |\n27\t| heuristic--apre-skill-learning-6rm-pyramid-test-and-rm-muscle-testing.md | GO | none | checked (image): \"skills/learning phase of about two weeks\", 20%/10% bodymass, doubled in week 2, pyramid 40%/20%, 10-8-6, 3-4 s per phase, separate and together limbs, prolonged eccentric \"about double\", at PDF 273 / p.262; isokinetic alternative, PNF, 6RM average person / 3RM serious athletes / 10RM hypertrophy, girth and skinfold at PDF 275 / p.264. `untested` fits a protocol with no data. Baseline lines 61, 141. |\n28\t| heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol.md | GO | none | checked (image, PDF 274 / p.263): \"6 repetitions of 100% of one's 1RM held for 6-8 seconds\", 6SM, 5-10 s rest, 50%/75%/to-failure sets, 6RM column, angle-series advice; Fig 5.2 axes (kgf 4-14, time 2-14 s), 100% curve peaking about 13.8 kgf near 6 s, 80% near 12, caption \"Ivenov\". Fig 5.3 (PDF 275 / p.264): 1-rep curve near 8 kgf, top curves about 14-16 kgf; the entry is right that the 4/5/6-rep curves cannot be ranked with confidence. Caption \"percentages of maximal contraction\" vs text \"1RM\" mismatch correctly flagged. Baseline lines 78, 134. |\n29\t| framework--two-groups-of-speed-strength-movements-and-their-tension-types.md | GO | none | checked (image, PDF 275 / p.264): two groups, \"generally unimportant ... decisive role in the second\", group-1 rapid-reaction movements, three tension types with definitions, deferral to 5.2.4, \"transference ... relatively poor\", \"far from being thoroughly understood\". The explosive-ballistic placement oddity is real and correctly flagged. Baseline line 76. Nit (optional): the quote \"plays a decisive role in the second.\" cuts \"type of movement\" inside the quote marks. |\n30\t| heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix.md | GO | none | checked (image): Yakovlev 1960, \"approximately 20% of 1RM\", citation list at PDF 275 / p.264; 146%, \"up to 40% of 1RM\", 60-80% fast isometrics, \"5:1\", \"ranked athlete\" at PDF 276 / p.265; inertia \"very seldom used\" at PDF 277 / p.266; summary \"20% of 1RM ... up to 40% ... 5 to 1\" and starting conditions at PDF 278 / p.267. Baseline lines 104, 105, 61. Nit (optional): the When NOT reference \"(line 61, Explicit, Q10)\" could carry the \"Eric's baseline:\" prefix. |\n31\t| claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass.md | FIX | Title, filename and When to apply state a rule the page's own data do not support. Entry: \"Small Deviations From the Competition Mass Work Better\" and \"argue for overweight and underweight implements kept close to the competition mass\". That is Bondarchuk's thesis. The page's examples (PDF 276-277 / p.265-266, image) include a 3 kg javelin training weight (Matveyev 1967), 2 kg vs 4 kg medicine balls for the water polo throw (Rogener 1961), a 0.6-0.8 kg heavy puck (Savin 1974) and 2 kg table-tennis bats; only the shot (250 g vs 500 g difference) and baseball (57 g / 184 g vs 148 g) are close to competition mass. The page's stated rule is relevance to the sport, a mass that \"does not disrupt technique\", and per-athlete selection of the heavy implement. All individual numbers in the body are correct. | Retitle: \"Lighter and Heavier Implements for Speed-Strength: Choose a Mass That Keeps Technique, Size the Heavy One per Athlete, and Expect One-Way Transfer\" and rename the file to match (e.g. `claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md`; update the one inbound link, in the speed-strength loading entry). In When to apply replace \"kept close to the competition mass\" with \"chosen so technique holds, alternated with the normal implement, with the heavy option sized per athlete\". Keep the Bondarchuk quote but introduce it as \"Inference: Bondarchuk goes further and argues for small deviations from competition weight; ...\". Add to When NOT: \"Inference: several of the book's own examples are far from competition mass (a 3 kg javelin training weight; a 0.6-0.8 kg puck; 2 kg bats), so the page does not support a 'stay close to competition mass' rule; only the shot and baseball data fit it.\" (Competition masses are outside-book knowledge, hence the label.) |\n32\t| case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work.md | GO | none | checked (image): \"38-40%\", 3 x 3 at 80% 1RM, 10 min rest, Fig 5.4 caption \"(Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)\", first effort about 42-49 cm, second about 50 to 67-69 cm, levelling after about movement 8 at PDF 276 / p.265; Table 5.3 every cell (44.0/61.0/+38.8; 0.266/0.250/-6.8; 62.0/65.0/+4.8; 2.330/2.600/+11.7; 5.550/7.430/+33.8; 1.310/1.930/+47.3) and \"(Verkhoshansky, 1977)\" at PDF 277 / p.266. Recomputations confirmed: +38.6%, -6.0%, 0.62/0.266 = 2.33, 0.65/0.250 = 2.60, F x v = 12.9 and 19.3. Bischke & Morehouse 1950 present in linked PAP entry. `untested` acceptable for a single unreported-n experiment. |\n33\t| heuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-strength-tempo-rest-and-fatigue.md | GO | none | checked (image): rest-interval determinants, \"0.5-1 minute\", \"favourable emotional state\", cyclic relaxation phases, \"inversely proportional\", Korobkov 1953 / Monogarov 1958, Diachkov 1961 \"approach the tempo ... and even exceed it\" at PDF 277 / p.266; \"more demanding\" water resistance and limb loads at PDF 277-278; fatigue rule and \"empirical methods\" at PDF 278 / p.267. Baseline lines 96, 97. |\n34\t| case-study--alternate-leg-bounding-with-thigh-cuffs-for-sprinters.md | GO | none | checked (image, PDF 278 / p.267): power greater than running, hip-flexion gather cue, 50-100 m, 100-150 g thigh cuffs, \"experienced athletes\", Zubov; third-person Verkhoshansky correctly attributed to Siff; `untested` right for a coach's anecdote. Bondarchuk and Moyer cross-references match the linked entries. Baseline line 97. |\n35\t\n36\t## Missing (important ideas in PDF 266-278 no entry captures)\n37\t\n38\tCoverage is near complete. Two small gaps, neither needs a new entry:\n39\t\n40\t1. PDF 270 / p.259: \"most weightlifting training is done with 1-3 repetitions for advanced weightlifters, thereby emphasizing the importance of 1RM, 2RM and 3RM loads ... especially where strength-skill is involved.\" Distinct from Vorobyev (1978) \"1-6\". Fold into the Berger entry (see its FIX).\n41\t2. PDF 278 / p.267: the section's organising conclusion that perfecting speed-strength means combining two levers, \"the positive after-effect of preceding work\" and \"exercises in which muscular force opposes the inertia of the load and not its weight\", plus \"Thus, to develop speed-strength...\" summary. The pieces are in the loading entry and the arm-throw case, but neither states the two-lever summary. Optional one-sentence addition to `heuristic--speed-strength-loading-...` What it is.\n42\t\n43\t## Reciprocal-link suggestions\n44\t\n45\t- `heuristic--analytical-synthetic-and-multifaceted-preparation-suit-only-early-training` and `heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label` (5.5).\n46\t- `principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities` and `framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation` (5.4).\n47\t- `framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric` and `heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size` (5.2.4).\n48\t\n49\t## Overall verdict\n50\t\n51\t**GO AFTER FIXES.** Required fixes:\n52\t\n53\t1. Newton II: flag ΔV as final velocity (equals change only from rest); \"net propulsive impulse\"; replace circular-motion example in (d) with constant-gravity projectile; \"net external force on the body\" in (c).\n54\t2. Dynamic correspondence: fix \"paragraph before\" to \"paragraph that closes\" the dynamics discussion; label \"This passage is the authors' own statement ... Use it when\" as Inference.\n55\t3. Three methods: say which load-rep pairing clashes (90-95%/3-6 clearly; 85-95%/3-5RM loosely); rewrite the line-105 fragment with an \"Eric's baseline:\" prefix.\n56\t4. DeLorme: remove or label \"mostly in rehabilitation or untrained subjects\".\n57\t5. Berger: correct the traceability sentence (conclusions 1 and 5 trace to Berger 1963 on p.259; only 6 is orphaned); add the 1-3 rep advanced-lifter sentence.\n58\t6. Ivanov: retitle and rename (eccentric raised squat 1RM most; no \"own test\" for eccentric); label the \"trains the nervous system for that mode\" gloss.\n59\t7. Implement mass: retitle and rename away from \"stay close to competition mass\"; fix When to apply; add the far-from-competition counterexamples (labelled Inference).\n60\t8. After renames in 6 and 7, update inbound wikilinks in this batch.\n61\t\n62\tNo NO-GO. Both `contested` entries keep both sides on the cited pages. The single `proven` entry keeps its scope.\n63\t-----FIXLOG-----\n64\t# Fixes applied: Supertraining 5.1-5.2.3 (Ch5a), 19 entries\n65\t\n66\tFixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-10-03. Review: `staging/review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md` (round 1, GO AFTER FIXES). The review's header counts \"FIX 8\", but its table has 7 FIX rows. Item 8 of its fix list is the inbound-link update after the two renames, not an eighth entry. All 7 rows, item 8, and the two \"Missing\" gaps were handled.\n67\tSource checked: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`. I extracted the text of PDF idx 266-278 with PyMuPDF and rendered them to `staging/_renders/f5a_p<N>.png`. I opened the images for PDF 267 (Newton II, ΔV definition, Fig 5.1, neuromuscular paragraph), 268 (dynamic correspondence), 269 (5RM 89%, DeLorme), 270 (variants, Berger, 1-3 reps sentence), 271 (Berger conclusions 4-6, 85-95% = 3-5RM), 272 (Ivanov, 90-95% / 3-6), 276-277 (implements, Table 5.3) and 278 (two-lever summary). Page key: PDF idx = printed + 11.\n68\tScope: the 19 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch5a.txt`. Only the 7 flagged entries were edited, plus the speed-strength loading entry (inbound link and gap 2). `_book.md` was not touched. No vault file was edited.\n69\t\n70\t| file | what changed |\n71\t|---|---|\n72\t| claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md | **What it is:** the `proven` scope sentence now reads \"(∫F_net dt = mΔv, with Δv the change in velocity, not the book's ΔV)\". Scope unchanged: the identity only.<br>**Why it works:** \"the take-off velocity of a jump fixes the net impulse\" is now \"for a jump from a still start, the take-off velocity fixes the net impulse\".<br>**When to apply:** \"propulsive impulse\" is now \"net propulsive impulse (vertical ground reaction force minus body weight, integrated over the propulsive phase, which starts at zero vertical velocity at the bottom of the countermovement)\".<br>**When NOT:** \"Four sentences\" is now \"Five sentences\". In (c), \"net force on a free body\" is now \"net external force on the body\". In (d), the circular-motion example is replaced: \"any change in the velocity vector, direction alone included, requires a net force, but the force itself need not change. A projectile under gravity (ignoring air resistance) changes direction continuously while the force on it stays constant, so a change in velocity direction does not require a change in force, only a net force.\" New (e) quotes the book's ΔV definition, \"The final velocity reached at the end of this phase\", and the 0-to-ΔV integral (PDF 267 / p.256, image). Then: \"Inference: so m.ΔV equals the change in momentum only when the body starts from rest; in general the impulse of the net external force equals m(v_final - v_initial).\" The closing line is now \"Do not use (c), (d) or (e) as stated.\" `confidence: proven` kept. |\n73\t| heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement.md | **What it is:** \"The paragraph before 5.1's dynamics discussion\" is now \"The paragraph that closes 5.1's dynamics discussion, after Fig 5.1\". The image confirms it: the neuromuscular paragraph follows Fig 5.1 at the foot of PDF 267, and \"All subsequent training ...\" opens PDF 268.<br>**When to apply:** \"This passage is the authors' own statement ...\" is now \"Inference: this passage is the authors' own statement ...\". |\n74\t| framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md | **When NOT (4):** \"These three are not consistent; do not use any one as a rep-to-load conversion.\" is replaced. New text: \"Inference: the 90-95% with 3-6 reps pairing clearly clashes with 5RM = 89% (six reps cannot be done at 90% or more if five reps is 89%); the 85-95% = 3-5RM pairing is only loosely off (it implies a 5RM near 85%). Do not use any of them as a rep-to-load conversion.\"<br>**When to apply:** the sentence fragment is rewritten: \"Eric's baseline: off-season strength work prioritizes \"relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work\" (line 105, Inferred, transcript and shared slides).\" The baseline's own tag is \"Inferred (transcript + shared slides)\".<br>**Related:** the Ivanov link now points to the new slug. |\n75\t| claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared.md | **When NOT:** \"All the studies are 1945-1960 work, mostly in rehabilitation or untrained subjects.\" is now \"All the studies are 1945-1960 work; the book gives no subject populations for them. Inference: the DeLorme lineage began in rehabilitation, so untrained or patient samples are likely, but the page does not say so.\" |\n76\t| claim--berger-sets-reps-and-frequency-studies-in-novices.md | **When NOT:** the traceability sentence is replaced. New text: \"The six conclusions are drawn partly from data outside the five-programme list: conclusion 1's \"maximal weights three times per week\" and conclusion 5 trace to the Berger (1963) sentences (...PDF p. 270 / printed p. 259), though conclusion 5 speaks of \"3 sets of the 10RM\" where that sentence describes one set; conclusion 3 traces loosely to the 2RM/5RM/10RM and 3 x 6RM results; and conclusion 6 traces to no data on the page. Treat conclusion 6 as reported without its data.\"<br>**What it is (gap 1):** added after the Vorobyev (1971) clause: \"and \"Other studies\" show that \"most weightlifting training is done with 1-3 repetitions for advanced weightlifters, thereby emphasizing the importance of 1RM, 2RM and 3RM loads for enhancing competitive strength, especially where strength-skill is involved\" (PDF p. 270 / printed p. 259, verified against the page image; Vorobyev 1978 at PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260)\". The paragraph's closing locator, which used to read \"PDF p. 270-271 / printed p. 259-260\", now gives a page for each quote it covers. The opening locator of What it is (\"PDF p. 270-271 / printed p. 259-260\") is unchanged. |\n77\t| claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves.md (was `claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves.md`) | **Renamed** with `mv`.<br>**Title** is now \"Ivanov (1966): Isometric Training Raised Isometric Strength Most, Eccentric Training Raised the Squat 1RM Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump\". Checked against PDF 272 / p.261 (image): squat 1RM eccentric 15 kg (largest), isometric strength isometric 30.2 kg (largest), vertical jump +3.7 cm with concentric training only. There is no eccentric test.<br>**Why it works:** the gloss is moved out of the authors' voice. New text: \"\"the neuromuscular specificity of strength\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). They give no further mechanism. Inference: read as each mode training the nervous system for that mode; the book does not say this.\" |\n78\t| claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md (was `claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass.md`) | **Renamed** with `mv`.<br>**Title** is now \"Lighter and Heavier Implements for Speed-Strength: Choose a Mass That Keeps Technique, Size the Heavy One per Athlete, and Transfer Can Run One Way\" (see Disagreements 1).<br>**When to apply:** \"kept close to the competition mass, alternated with the normal implement, with the heavy option sized per athlete by whether technique holds\" is now \"chosen so technique holds, alternated with the normal implement, with the heavy option sized per athlete\". The Bondarchuk quote is now introduced as \"Inference: Bondarchuk goes further and argues for small deviations from competition weight; his correlation data: ...\".<br>**When NOT:** added \"Inference: several of the book's own examples are far from competition mass (a 3 kg javelin training weight; a 0.6-0.8 kg puck; 2 kg bats), so the page does not support a \"stay close to competition mass\" rule; only the shot and baseball data fit it, and the water polo result fits only in rank order (both medicine balls are far heavier than a match ball, and the lighter one did better).\"<br>**Related:** the Bondarchuk annotation \"(agrees: close to competition weight transfers best)\" is now \"(Bondarchuk's position: close to competition weight transfers best; this page does not test that)\". The old annotation contradicted the corrected body. |\n79\t| heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix.md | **What it is (gap 2):** added the section's closing summary: \"The section closes on two levers: \"Perfecting the methods of developing speed-strength is a result of the search for a specific combination of means that exploit\" \"the positive after-effect of preceding work on the subsequent activity\" and \"the use of exercises in which muscular force opposes the inertia of the load and not its weight\" (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267, verified against the page image). The after-effect lever is the arm-throw experiment in [[case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work]].\" The arm-throw case was not linked from this entry before.<br>**Related:** the implement-mass link now points to the new slug. |\n80\t| _scope_supertraining_ch5a.txt | Lines 10 and 16 now list the two new filenames. Still 19 entries. |\n81\t\n82\t## Renames and inbound links\n83\t\n84\t| old slug | new slug | inbound links updated |\n85\t|---|---|---|\n86\t| claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves | claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves | `framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md` (Related) |\n87\t| claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass | claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete | `heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix.md` (Related) |\n88\t\n89\tBefore the renames I grepped all of `staging/library`, `/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library` and the 5a/5b/5c scope files for both old slugs. The only hits were the two links above and the 5a scope file. No vault file links either old slug, and no 5b or 5c file does either. The review file and this log still mention the old slugs by design.\n90\t\n91\t## Disagreements with the review\n92\t\n93\t1. **Implement-mass title: I kept \"Transfer Can Run One Way\" instead of the review's \"Expect One-Way Transfer\".** The page supports one-way transfer only in two places. Egstrom is about precision, not speed (\"throwing the light ball improves precision for throwing the heavier ball, but the reverse effect does not occur\"). The shot transfer is \"apparently ... possible only by alternating\" (PDF 276-277 / p.265-266, image). \"Expect\" would overstate both, so the existing, weaker clause stays. The rest of the review's title is applied.\n94\t2. **Implement mass: \"only the shot and baseball data fit it\" is refined, not copied.** Water polo also fits the closer-is-better direction by rank: the 2 kg ball beat the 4 kg ball, and 4 kg harmed technique (PDF 276 / p.265, image). It does not fit a \"stay close\" rule, because both balls are far heavier than a match ball. The added Inference says so. The match-ball comparison is outside-book knowledge, so it sits inside that Inference.\n95\t3. **Newton II: the review's wording is tightened in three places.** The physics agrees with the review; the changes make it exact.\n96\t   - Its (e) reads \"in general impulse = m(v_final - v_initial)\". Bare \"impulse\" repeats the looseness the review flagged in (c), so I wrote \"the impulse of the net external force equals m(v_final - v_initial)\".\n97\t   - Its \"net propulsive impulse\" fixes take-off velocity only if the propulsive phase starts at zero vertical velocity, so I added \"which starts at zero vertical velocity at the bottom of the countermovement\".\n98\t   - Its projectile example now carries \"(ignoring air resistance)\".\n99\t   I also made two small changes the review did not list. The scope sentence now says its Δv is the change in velocity, not the book's ΔV, so it does not appear to contradict (e). The Why it works jump sentence is limited to \"a jump from a still start\".\n100\t4. **Berger: one qualifier added.** Conclusion 5 (\"Training twice a week with 3 sets of the 10RM is as effective as training with 3 sets of 10RM three times a week\", PDF 271 / p.260) traces to the Berger 1963 frequency sentence. That sentence's regime is \"10RM for one set three times per week\" (PDF 270 / p.259). The entry now says that conclusion 5 speaks of 3 sets where the page describes one.\n101\t5. **Three methods: the clash explanation carries \"Inference:\".** The review's replacement sentence (\"six reps cannot be done at 90% or more if five reps is 89%\") is reasoning, not the authors' statement, so it is labelled.\n102\t\n103\tNo review claim was found wrong on the page. Every flagged problem was confirmed on the rendered image before it was changed.\n104\t\n105\t## Proof\n106\t\n107\tGrep for the new wording, the old wording and the old slugs, run from `staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky`:\n108\t\n109\t```\n110\t== Newton II\n111\twith Δv the change in velocity, not the book's ΔV\n112\tfor a jump from a still start\n113\tnet propulsive impulse (vertical ground reaction force minus body weight, integrated over the propulsive phase, which starts at zero vertical velocity at the bottom of the countermovement)\n114\tFive sentences in the passage\n115\tnet external force on the body.\n116\tA projectile under gravity (ignoring air resistance) changes direction continuously while the force on it stays constant, so a change in velocity direction does not require a change in force, only a net force.\n117\tin general the impulse of the net external force equals m(v_final - v_initial)\n118\tDo not use (c), (d) or (e) as stated\n119\t== Newton old wording (expect none)\n120\t0\n121\t== Dynamic correspondence\n122\tThe paragraph that closes 5.1's dynamics discussion, after Fig 5.1\n123\tInference: this passage is the authors' own statement\n124\t== Three methods\n125\tEric's baseline: off-season strength work prioritizes\n126\tline 105, Inferred, transcript and shared slides\n127\tInference: the 90-95% with 3-6 reps pairing clearly clashes with 5RM = 89% (six reps cannot be done at 90% or more if five reps is 89%)\n128\t[[claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves]]\n129\t== DeLorme\n130\tthe book gives no subject populations for them. Inference: the DeLorme lineage began in rehabilitation, so untrained or patient samples are likely, but the page does not say so.\n131\t== Berger\n132\tmost weightlifting training is done with 1-3 repetitions for advanced weightlifters\n133\tdrawn partly from data outside the five-programme list\n134\twhere that sentence describes one set\n135\tTreat conclusion 6 as reported without its data\n136\t== Ivanov\n137\t# Ivanov (1966): Isometric Training Raised Isometric Strength Most, Eccentric Training Raised the Squat 1RM Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump\n138\tInference: read as each mode training the nervous system for that mode; the book does not say this.\n139\t== Implement mass\n140\t# Lighter and Heavier Implements for Speed-Strength: Choose a Mass That Keeps Technique, Size the Heavy One per Athlete, and Transfer Can Run One Way\n141\tchosen so technique holds, alternated with the normal implement, with the heavy option sized per athlete\n142\tInference: Bondarchuk goes further and argues for small deviations from competition weight\n143\tso the page does not support a \"stay close to competition mass\" rule\n144\tBondarchuk's position: close to competition weight transfers best; this page does not test that\n145\t== Loading\n146\tThe section closes on two levers\n147\t[[case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work]]\n148\t[[claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete]]\n149\t== Old slugs in staging/library, scope files, vault (expect nothing)\n150\t(exit 1)\n151\t== New files on disk\n152\tclaim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md\n153\tclaim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves.md\n154\t== em dashes outside section headers in edited files (expect none)\n155\t(end)\n156\t== wrapped wikilinks (expect none)\n157\t(end)\n158\t```\n159\t\n160\tThe old-slug grep covered `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library`, the 5a, 5b and 5c scope files, and `/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library`. Exit 1 means no match.\n161\t\n162\tLint (Mac-path copy), run from the Flint root:\n163\t\n164\t```\n165\t$ ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky\n166\tsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 114, 115]\n167\tTOTAL ISSUES: 1\n168\t```\n169\t\n170\tThe one issue is the known `_book.md` false positive (title and table dashes). It is outside this fix scope; `_book.md` was not touched.\n171\t\n172\tFor the promoter: confidence tallies are unchanged. The Newton entry stays `proven`, scoped to the identity. The two renamed entries keep `supported`. The review's optional reciprocal-link suggestions were not applied, because they touch GO entries and 5b/5c files outside this fix scope.\n173\t"}]},"uuid":"6dc2f23d-468f-44e5-9bd8-f37388a15fb3","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:20:37.511Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/projects/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c/tool-results/bcjsoiq8t.txt","content":"# Review: Supertraining Ch 5a (5.1 to 5.2.3), independent falsification pass\n\nReviewer: Claude Opus (independent of the writer). Date: 2026-10-03.\nScope: the 19 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch5a.txt`, folder `staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`.\nSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx 266-278 (printed 255-267). Every page in range was rendered to `staging/_renders/r5a_p266.png` ... `r5a_p278.png` and opened as an image. Every equation, table, figure and number cited below was read from the image, not the text layer.\nBaseline: every \"Eric's baseline:\" quote checked against `docs/method-baseline.md` at the cited line (lines 61, 67, 73, 76, 78, 79, 96, 97, 101, 103, 104, 105, 107, 130, 134, 141, 181, 182, 188, 260). All quotes and line numbers match. Tension 16 is correctly named at line 260.\nLinks: every `[[wikilink]]` in the 19 files resolves to a file in the vault library or the staging library. Claims the entries make about linked entries were spot-checked (Bischke & Morehouse in the PAP entry; breath-holding in the isometric drawbacks entry; Moyer wearable-resistance entry \"wrong in three separate ways\"; Bondarchuk leg-to-leg 50 m \"all levels\"; Vasiliev uniform-gain in the threshold entry; APRE named in the isokinetic entry). All hold.\nDuplicates: searched the vault library and all staging libraries (including the 5b and 5c files in the same folder) for impulse-momentum, DeLorme, APRE/DAPRE, Berger, Ivanov 1966, Lelikov, analytical-synthetic, cross training, multi-faceted, Rogener, Egstrom, Zubov, thigh cuffs, Table 5.3, Krusen, Narikashvili, 6SM, \"20% of 1RM\", \"independent qualities\", \"literally copying\". No entry in scope restates an existing entry without a new angle. The Newton entry reuses Fig 5.1 (already atomized from Ch 2.2.2) but says so and adds the impulse-momentum angle.\n\nCounts: **GO 11, FIX 8, NO-GO 0.**\n\n## Per-entry verdicts\n\n| file | verdict | problem (entry vs source, with page) | exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md | FIX | `proven` is correctly scoped to the impulse-momentum identity, and (a), (b), (c) are correct physics. Three physics sentences are imprecise. (1) The entry quotes the book's \"I = F.ΔT = m.ΔV\" and \"the impulse may be calculated from the change in momentum, m.ΔV\" without flagging that the book defines ΔV as \"The final velocity reached at the end of this phase\" with the integral running from 0 (PDF 267 / p.256, image). m times a final velocity equals the change in momentum only if the body starts from rest. (2) When to apply: \"a driver metric such as propulsive impulse to the output metric (take-off velocity, hence jump height)\". Only the net impulse (vertical GRF minus body weight) fixes take-off velocity; GRF impulse alone does not. (3) When NOT (d): \"constant-speed circular motion has a constant-size force that only turns\". A force that turns is a changing force vector, so this does not counter the book's \"associated with a change in force\"; it confirms it. (c) also says \"net force on a free body\", which is loose. | (1) Add to When NOT as (e): \"Inference: the book defines ΔV as the final velocity of the phase and integrates from 0, so m.ΔV equals the change in momentum only when the body starts from rest; in general impulse = m(v_final - v_initial).\" (2) Replace \"propulsive impulse\" with \"net propulsive impulse (vertical ground reaction force minus body weight, integrated over the propulsive phase)\". (3) In (d), replace the circular-motion example with: \"a projectile under gravity changes direction continuously while the force on it stays constant, so a change in velocity direction does not require a change in force, only a net force.\" In (c), replace \"net force on a free body\" with \"net external force on the body\". Keep `proven` with its existing scope. |\n| principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities.md | GO | none | checked: \"totally independent qualities\", \"does not necessarily enhance\", \"(Verkhoshansky, 1963)\", \"inversely proportional to one another (see Ch 4)\" at PDF 268 / p.257 (image); Example labelled Inference; baseline line 101 quote and Explicit Q1 tag; `supported` appropriate. Suggest reciprocal link to `framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation` (5.4, transfer weakens with qualification). |\n| heuristic--analytical-synthetic-and-multifaceted-preparation-suit-only-early-training.md | GO | none | checked: analytical-synthetic definition, gymnasts/weightlifters sprints and barbell for track (Verkhoshansky 1977), \"appropriate only for the initial stages ... serious error for highly-qualified athletes to use them casually\", \"partly true and only under certain circumstances\", Yakovlev 1968 / Zimkin 1965 / Zatsiorsky 1965, \"inevitably opposes the law of gradual development\", cross training \"(see 5.5)\", \"sequentiality, simultaneity or scheme of superposition\" all at PDF 268 / p.257 (image); baseline line 182; Ch 2 counter-quote \"positively influences the development of the others\" present in the linked entry. Suggest reciprocal link to the 5.5 entry `heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label`. |\n| heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement.md | FIX | (1) Location wrong. Entry: \"The paragraph before 5.1's dynamics discussion sets this up\". The neuromuscular-system paragraph (\"with the speed necessary to produce the required motor effect ... requires specialised means of training\") closes the dynamics discussion: it follows Fig 5.1 at the foot of PDF 267 / p.256, and \"All subsequent training and performance errors are caused by inappropriate neuromuscular programming\" opens PDF 268 / p.257 (image). (2) Unlabelled writer judgment and application. When to apply: \"This passage is the authors' own statement of that 'not surface resemblance' rule. Use it when a coach asks for a loaded version of a sport skill (a weighted throwing motion, a banded route stem, ...)\" has no \"Inference:\" in that paragraph. | (1) Replace \"The paragraph before 5.1's dynamics discussion sets this up\" with \"The paragraph that closes 5.1's dynamics discussion, after Fig 5.1, sets this up\". (2) Prefix \"This passage is the authors' own statement ...\" with \"Inference:\". All quotes otherwise verified at PDF 268 / p.257 and PDF 277-278 / p.266-267 (image). |\n| framework--special-strength-methods-classified-by-sport-group-and-strength-type.md | GO | none | checked: four sport categories (Diachkov 1961), strength types with Diachkov / Kuznetsov / Chudinov, three principles incl. \"(Verkhoshansky, 1970)\", maximal tension / speed-strength / strength-endurance loads, \"repetitive, maximal and dynamic effort (Zatsiorsky, 1966)\", five method groups, \"(see 3.1)\" at PDF 269 / p.258 (image); section headings 5.2.1 at PDF 269, 5.2.3 at PDF 275, 5.2.4 at PDF 278, 5.2.5 at PDF 296 (text-layer search); baseline line 188. Football-as-group-4 is inside an Inference sentence. |\n| framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md | FIX | `contested` is justified and all four contradiction pairs are on the cited pages (image): sets \"more than three (Berger, 1962)\" PDF 271 / p.260 vs \"reducing the number of sets and repetitions\" PDF 272 / p.261; \"larger training effect\" PDF 271 / p.260 vs \"Similar significant increases\" PDF 272 / p.261; Salter 1955 PDF 271 / p.260 and Rasch & Morehouse PDF 272 / p.261 vs the same \"similar\" sentence; 5RM \"about 89%\" PDF 269 / p.258, \"90-95% of 1RM ... (3-6)\" PDF 272 / p.261, \"85-95% of 1RM, i.e. 3-5RM\" PDF 271 / p.260. Two problems. (1) Item (4) overstates: \"These three are not consistent\". The clear clash is 90-95% with up to 6 reps against 5RM = 89% (6 reps must sit below 89%). \"85-95% i.e. 3-5RM\" puts the 5RM near 85% against 89%, a loose mismatch only. (2) When to apply has a sentence fragment carrying a baseline quote: \"The baseline's off-season rule (\"relative strength and technique first; ...\", line 105, course material, Inferred).\" | (1) Replace \"These three are not consistent; do not use any one as a rep-to-load conversion.\" with \"The 90-95% with 3-6 reps pairing clearly clashes with 5RM = 89% (six reps cannot be done at 90% or more if five reps is 89%); the 85-95% = 3-5RM pairing is only loosely off (it implies a 5RM near 85%). Do not use any of them as a rep-to-load conversion.\" (2) Rewrite the fragment as: \"Eric's baseline: off-season strength work prioritizes 'relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work' (line 105, Inferred, transcript and slides).\" Also verified: Kabat 1947 / Hellebrant & Houtz 1956 at PDF 269 (image; text layer reads \"Rabat\"); Medvedev 1967 / Vorobyev 1971 \"5-6 exercises for 6-10 sets of 1-3\" at PDF 271; baseline lines 61, 105, 260. |\n| claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared.md | FIX | Unlabelled claim not on the page. When NOT: \"All the studies are 1945-1960 work, mostly in rehabilitation or untrained subjects.\" PDF 269-270 / p.258-259 gives no subject populations for Faulkner, McMorris & Elks or Krusen; only McMorris & Elks is qualified (\"particularly with advanced athletes\"). | Replace with: \"All the studies are 1945-1960 work; the book gives no subject populations for them. Inference: the DeLorme lineage began in rehabilitation, so untrained or patient samples are likely, but the page does not say so.\" Otherwise verified (image): 89% / 74% and the DeLorme 50%-75%-100% of 10RM (Hettinger 1961) at PDF 269 / p.258; Oxford/Zinovieff and McCloy sequences, \"add nothing new\", 154% vs 142%, 212.8% vs 186% (Faulkner 1950), 5.5%, McMorris & Elks 1954, Krusen 1949 25/50/75% vs 5RM/125%/150% at PDF 270 / p.259; Narikashvili 1960 at PDF 271 / p.260; baseline line 107. |\n| claim--berger-sets-reps-and-frequency-studies-in-novices.md | FIX | When NOT misstates traceability. Entry: \"conclusion 1 speaks of 'maximal weights three times per week,' which is none of the five listed programmes, and conclusions 3, 5 and 6 refer to data the page does not describe.\" Source PDF 270 / p.259 (image), Berger 1963 paragraph: \"with maximal weights (10RM for one set three times per week)\" and \"There was also no major difference between the effectiveness of 2 or 3 training sessions per week.\" So conclusion 1's comparator and conclusion 5 trace to the Berger 1963 sentences, and conclusion 3 (3RM to 10RM) is loosely backed by the 2RM/5RM/10RM and 3 x 6RM results. Only conclusion 6 has no data on the page. Minor: the separate p.259 sentence that \"most weightlifting training is done with 1-3 repetitions for advanced weightlifters ... especially where strength-skill is involved\" is missing from the advanced-lifter contrast. | Replace the sentence with: \"The six conclusions are drawn partly from data outside the five-programme list: conclusion 1's 'maximal weights three times per week' and conclusion 5 trace to the Berger (1963) sentences ('10RM for one set three times per week'; 'no major difference between ... 2 or 3 training sessions per week', PDF 270 / p.259), conclusion 3 loosely to the 2RM/5RM/10RM and 3 x 6RM results, and conclusion 6 to no data on the page.\" Add after the Vorobyev (1971) clause: \"and 'other studies' show most weightlifting training for advanced lifters is done with 1-3 repetitions, 'emphasizing the importance of 1RM, 2RM and 3RM loads ... especially where strength-skill is involved' (PDF 270 / p.259).\" Otherwise verified (image): 3 x 6RM (Berger 1962), Keipen 1956, Vasiliev 1954 rest, five programmes and \"only ... the fifth one\", conclusions 1-6, italic \"these conclusions were based on research with untrained subjects\", Vorobyev 1978 \"1-6\" at PDF 270-271 / p.259-260. |\n| claim--moderate-and-mixed-movement-tempo-for-repetition-strength-gains.md | GO | none | checked (image, PDF 271 / p.260): Vorobyev 1978 tempo sentence; 30 vs 15 sessions; \"22.6 ± 0.6 kg; and 16.3 ± 0.5 kg\" (Lelikov 1975); Vasiliev 1954 equal work at 20/40/60/80% and 45 lifts/min; \"maximal tempo became less effective as the load was increased\"; 20% exception. Overlap with `claim--strength-training-threshold-stimulus` correctly declared (that entry holds the uniform-gain point; this one adds tempo). Baseline line 181. |\n| claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves.md | FIX | Title contradicts the data and the entry's own body. Title: \"Concentric, Eccentric and Isometric Training Each Improve Their Own Test Most\". Source PDF 272 / p.261 (image): there is no eccentric test; eccentric work gave the largest squat 1RM gain (15 kg), isometric the largest isometric gain (30.2 kg), and only concentric raised the vertical jump (3.7 cm). The entry's When NOT already notes \"a squat 1RM is decided by its concentric phase, yet eccentric training raised it most\". Minor: Why it works glosses the book's phrase with \"each mode trains the nervous system for that mode\", which the book does not say. | Retitle: \"Ivanov (1966): Isometric Training Raised Isometric Strength Most, Eccentric Training Raised the Squat 1RM Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump\" (rename file to match, e.g. `claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves.md`, and update the one inbound link, in the three-methods entry). In Why it works, prefix the gloss \"each mode trains the nervous system for that mode\" with \"Inference:\". Numbers verified (image): 15, 9.2, 30.2 (text layer \"iJO.2\"), 14.6, 3.7, -1.6, -5.4 cm; baseline line 76 and its contested tag. |\n| framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise.md | GO | none | checked: DAPRE (Knight 1979) sets at PDF 272 / p.261; APRE 3RM/6RM/10RM labels, 2-4 min rest, Table 5.1 every cell at PDF 273 / p.262 (image); Table 5.2 every cell at PDF 274 / p.263 (image): 6RM 0-2 decr 2.5-5, 3-4 decr 0-2.5, 5-7 same, 8-12 incr 2.5-5, 13+ incr 5-7.5; 10RM 4-6 decr 2.5-5, 7-8 decr 0-2.5, 9-11 same, 12-16 incr 2.5-5, 17+ incr 5-7.5; 3RM 1-2 decr 2.5-5, 3-4 same, 5-6 incr 2.5-5, 7+ incr 5-10. \"6-8 weeks\" and ballistic-end quote at PDF 274. `contested` stated reason holds: \"more than 6 ... must be increased\" PDF 273 / p.262 vs 5-7 \"leave same\" PDF 274 / p.263, both on the cited pages. Example labelled Inference and consistent with the table. Baseline lines 61, 130, 67. |\n| heuristic--apre-skill-learning-6rm-pyramid-test-and-rm-muscle-testing.md | GO | none | checked (image): \"skills/learning phase of about two weeks\", 20%/10% bodymass, doubled in week 2, pyramid 40%/20%, 10-8-6, 3-4 s per phase, separate and together limbs, prolonged eccentric \"about double\", at PDF 273 / p.262; isokinetic alternative, PNF, 6RM average person / 3RM serious athletes / 10RM hypertrophy, girth and skinfold at PDF 275 / p.264. `untested` fits a protocol with no data. Baseline lines 61, 141. |\n| heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol.md | GO | none | checked (image, PDF 274 / p.263): \"6 repetitions of 100% of one's 1RM held for 6-8 seconds\", 6SM, 5-10 s rest, 50%/75%/to-failure sets, 6RM column, angle-series advice; Fig 5.2 axes (kgf 4-14, time 2-14 s), 100% curve peaking about 13.8 kgf near 6 s, 80% near 12, caption \"Ivenov\". Fig 5.3 (PDF 275 / p.264): 1-rep curve near 8 kgf, top curves about 14-16 kgf; the entry is right that the 4/5/6-rep curves cannot be ranked with confidence. Caption \"percentages of maximal contraction\" vs text \"1RM\" mismatch correctly flagged. Baseline lines 78, 134. |\n| framework--two-groups-of-speed-strength-movements-and-their-tension-types.md | GO | none | checked (image, PDF 275 / p.264): two groups, \"generally unimportant ... decisive role in the second\", group-1 rapid-reaction movements, three tension types with definitions, deferral to 5.2.4, \"transference ... relatively poor\", \"far from being thoroughly understood\". The explosive-ballistic placement oddity is real and correctly flagged. Baseline line 76. Nit (optional): the quote \"plays a decisive role in the second.\" cuts \"type of movement\" inside the quote marks. |\n| heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix.md | GO | none | checked (image): Yakovlev 1960, \"approximately 20% of 1RM\", citation list at PDF 275 / p.264; 146%, \"up to 40% of 1RM\", 60-80% fast isometrics, \"5:1\", \"ranked athlete\" at PDF 276 / p.265; inertia \"very seldom used\" at PDF 277 / p.266; summary \"20% of 1RM ... up to 40% ... 5 to 1\" and starting conditions at PDF 278 / p.267. Baseline lines 104, 105, 61. Nit (optional): the When NOT reference \"(line 61, Explicit, Q10)\" could carry the \"Eric's baseline:\" prefix. |\n| claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass.md | FIX | Title, filename and When to apply state a rule the page's own data do not support. Entry: \"Small Deviations From the Competition Mass Work Better\" and \"argue for overweight and underweight implements kept close to the competition mass\". That is Bondarchuk's thesis. The page's examples (PDF 276-277 / p.265-266, image) include a 3 kg javelin training weight (Matveyev 1967), 2 kg vs 4 kg medicine balls for the water polo throw (Rogener 1961), a 0.6-0.8 kg heavy puck (Savin 1974) and 2 kg table-tennis bats; only the shot (250 g vs 500 g difference) and baseball (57 g / 184 g vs 148 g) are close to competition mass. The page's stated rule is relevance to the sport, a mass that \"does not disrupt technique\", and per-athlete selection of the heavy implement. All individual numbers in the body are correct. | Retitle: \"Lighter and Heavier Implements for Speed-Strength: Choose a Mass That Keeps Technique, Size the Heavy One per Athlete, and Expect One-Way Transfer\" and rename the file to match (e.g. `claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md`; update the one inbound link, in the speed-strength loading entry). In When to apply replace \"kept close to the competition mass\" with \"chosen so technique holds, alternated with the normal implement, with the heavy option sized per athlete\". Keep the Bondarchuk quote but introduce it as \"Inference: Bondarchuk goes further and argues for small deviations from competition weight; ...\". Add to When NOT: \"Inference: several of the book's own examples are far from competition mass (a 3 kg javelin training weight; a 0.6-0.8 kg puck; 2 kg bats), so the page does not support a 'stay close to competition mass' rule; only the shot and baseball data fit it.\" (Competition masses are outside-book knowledge, hence the label.) |\n| case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work.md | GO | none | checked (image): \"38-40%\", 3 x 3 at 80% 1RM, 10 min rest, Fig 5.4 caption \"(Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)\", first effort about 42-49 cm, second about 50 to 67-69 cm, levelling after about movement 8 at PDF 276 / p.265; Table 5.3 every cell (44.0/61.0/+38.8; 0.266/0.250/-6.8; 62.0/65.0/+4.8; 2.330/2.600/+11.7; 5.550/7.430/+33.8; 1.310/1.930/+47.3) and \"(Verkhoshansky, 1977)\" at PDF 277 / p.266. Recomputations confirmed: +38.6%, -6.0%, 0.62/0.266 = 2.33, 0.65/0.250 = 2.60, F x v = 12.9 and 19.3. Bischke & Morehouse 1950 present in linked PAP entry. `untested` acceptable for a single unreported-n experiment. |\n| heuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-strength-tempo-rest-and-fatigue.md | GO | none | checked (image): rest-interval determinants, \"0.5-1 minute\", \"favourable emotional state\", cyclic relaxation phases, \"inversely proportional\", Korobkov 1953 / Monogarov 1958, Diachkov 1961 \"approach the tempo ... and even exceed it\" at PDF 277 / p.266; \"more demanding\" water resistance and limb loads at PDF 277-278; fatigue rule and \"empirical methods\" at PDF 278 / p.267. Baseline lines 96, 97. |\n| case-study--alternate-leg-bounding-with-thigh-cuffs-for-sprinters.md | GO | none | checked (image, PDF 278 / p.267): power greater than running, hip-flexion gather cue, 50-100 m, 100-150 g thigh cuffs, \"experienced athletes\", Zubov; third-person Verkhoshansky correctly attributed to Siff; `untested` right for a coach's anecdote. Bondarchuk and Moyer cross-references match the linked entries. Baseline line 97. |\n\n## Missing (important ideas in PDF 266-278 no entry captures)\n\nCoverage is near complete. Two small gaps, neither needs a new entry:\n\n1. PDF 270 / p.259: \"most weightlifting training is done with 1-3 repetitions for advanced weightlifters, thereby emphasizing the importance of 1RM, 2RM and 3RM loads ... especially where strength-skill is involved.\" Distinct from Vorobyev (1978) \"1-6\". Fold into the Berger entry (see its FIX).\n2. PDF 278 / p.267: the section's organising conclusion that perfecting speed-strength means combining two levers, \"the positive after-effect of preceding work\" and \"exercises in which muscular force opposes the inertia of the load and not its weight\", plus \"Thus, to develop speed-strength...\" summary. The pieces are in the loading entry and the arm-throw case, but neither states the two-lever summary. Optional one-sentence addition to `heuristic--speed-strength-loading-...` What it is.\n\n## Reciprocal-link suggestions\n\n- `heuristic--analytical-synthetic-and-multifaceted-preparation-suit-only-early-training` and `heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label` (5.5).\n- `principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities` and `framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation` (5.4).\n- `framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric` and `heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size` (5.2.4).\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** Required fixes:\n\n1. Newton II: flag ΔV as final velocity (equals change only from rest); \"net propulsive impulse\"; replace circular-motion example in (d) with constant-gravity projectile; \"net external force on the body\" in (c).\n2. Dynamic correspondence: fix \"paragraph before\" to \"paragraph that closes\" the dynamics discussion; label \"This passage is the authors' own statement ... Use it when\" as Inference.\n3. Three methods: say which load-rep pairing clashes (90-95%/3-6 clearly; 85-95%/3-5RM loosely); rewrite the line-105 fragment with an \"Eric's baseline:\" prefix.\n4. DeLorme: remove or label \"mostly in rehabilitation or untrained subjects\".\n5. Berger: correct the traceability sentence (conclusions 1 and 5 trace to Berger 1963 on p.259; only 6 is orphaned); add the 1-3 rep advanced-lifter sentence.\n6. Ivanov: retitle and rename (eccentric raised squat 1RM most; no \"own test\" for eccentric); label the \"trains the nervous system for that mode\" gloss.\n7. Implement mass: retitle and rename away from \"stay close to competition mass\"; fix When to apply; add the far-from-competition counterexamples (labelled Inference).\n8. After renames in 6 and 7, update inbound wikilinks in this batch.\n\nNo NO-GO. Both `contested` entries keep both sides on the cited pages. The single `proven` entry keeps its scope.\n-----FIXLOG-----\n# Fixes applied: Supertraining 5.1-5.2.3 (Ch5a), 19 entries\n\nFixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-10-03. Review: `staging/review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md` (round 1, GO AFTER FIXES). The review's header counts \"FIX 8\", but its table has 7 FIX rows. Item 8 of its fix list is the inbound-link update after the two renames, not an eighth entry. All 7 rows, item 8, and the two \"Missing\" gaps were handled.\nSource checked: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`. I extracted the text of PDF idx 266-278 with PyMuPDF and rendered them to `staging/_renders/f5a_p<N>.png`. I opened the images for PDF 267 (Newton II, ΔV definition, Fig 5.1, neuromuscular paragraph), 268 (dynamic correspondence), 269 (5RM 89%, DeLorme), 270 (variants, Berger, 1-3 reps sentence), 271 (Berger conclusions 4-6, 85-95% = 3-5RM), 272 (Ivanov, 90-95% / 3-6), 276-277 (implements, Table 5.3) and 278 (two-lever summary). Page key: PDF idx = printed + 11.\nScope: the 19 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch5a.txt`. Only the 7 flagged entries were edited, plus the speed-strength loading entry (inbound link and gap 2). `_book.md` was not touched. No vault file was edited.\n\n| file | what changed |\n|---|---|\n| claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md | **What it is:** the `proven` scope sentence now reads \"(∫F_net dt = mΔv, with Δv the change in velocity, not the book's ΔV)\". Scope unchanged: the identity only.<br>**Why it works:** \"the take-off velocity of a jump fixes the net impulse\" is now \"for a jump from a still start, the take-off velocity fixes the net impulse\".<br>**When to apply:** \"propulsive impulse\" is now \"net propulsive impulse (vertical ground reaction force minus body weight, integrated over the propulsive phase, which starts at zero vertical velocity at the bottom of the countermovement)\".<br>**When NOT:** \"Four sentences\" is now \"Five sentences\". In (c), \"net force on a free body\" is now \"net external force on the body\". In (d), the circular-motion example is replaced: \"any change in the velocity vector, direction alone included, requires a net force, but the force itself need not change. A projectile under gravity (ignoring air resistance) changes direction continuously while the force on it stays constant, so a change in velocity direction does not require a change in force, only a net force.\" New (e) quotes the book's ΔV definition, \"The final velocity reached at the end of this phase\", and the 0-to-ΔV integral (PDF 267 / p.256, image). Then: \"Inference: so m.ΔV equals the change in momentum only when the body starts from rest; in general the impulse of the net external force equals m(v_final - v_initial).\" The closing line is now \"Do not use (c), (d) or (e) as stated.\" `confidence: proven` kept. |\n| heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement.md | **What it is:** \"The paragraph before 5.1's dynamics discussion\" is now \"The paragraph that closes 5.1's dynamics discussion, after Fig 5.1\". The image confirms it: the neuromuscular paragraph follows Fig 5.1 at the foot of PDF 267, and \"All subsequent training ...\" opens PDF 268.<br>**When to apply:** \"This passage is the authors' own statement ...\" is now \"Inference: this passage is the authors' own statement ...\". |\n| framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md | **When NOT (4):** \"These three are not consistent; do not use any one as a rep-to-load conversion.\" is replaced. New text: \"Inference: the 90-95% with 3-6 reps pairing clearly clashes with 5RM = 89% (six reps cannot be done at 90% or more if five reps is 89%); the 85-95% = 3-5RM pairing is only loosely off (it implies a 5RM near 85%). Do not use any of them as a rep-to-load conversion.\"<br>**When to apply:** the sentence fragment is rewritten: \"Eric's baseline: off-season strength work prioritizes \"relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work\" (line 105, Inferred, transcript and shared slides).\" The baseline's own tag is \"Inferred (transcript + shared slides)\".<br>**Related:** the Ivanov link now points to the new slug. |\n| claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared.md | **When NOT:** \"All the studies are 1945-1960 work, mostly in rehabilitation or untrained subjects.\" is now \"All the studies are 1945-1960 work; the book gives no subject populations for them. Inference: the DeLorme lineage began in rehabilitation, so untrained or patient samples are likely, but the page does not say so.\" |\n| claim--berger-sets-reps-and-frequency-studies-in-novices.md | **When NOT:** the traceability sentence is replaced. New text: \"The six conclusions are drawn partly from data outside the five-programme list: conclusion 1's \"maximal weights three times per week\" and conclusion 5 trace to the Berger (1963) sentences (...PDF p. 270 / printed p. 259), though conclusion 5 speaks of \"3 sets of the 10RM\" where that sentence describes one set; conclusion 3 traces loosely to the 2RM/5RM/10RM and 3 x 6RM results; and conclusion 6 traces to no data on the page. Treat conclusion 6 as reported without its data.\"<br>**What it is (gap 1):** added after the Vorobyev (1971) clause: \"and \"Other studies\" show that \"most weightlifting training is done with 1-3 repetitions for advanced weightlifters, thereby emphasizing the importance of 1RM, 2RM and 3RM loads for enhancing competitive strength, especially where strength-skill is involved\" (PDF p. 270 / printed p. 259, verified against the page image; Vorobyev 1978 at PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260)\". The paragraph's closing locator, which used to read \"PDF p. 270-271 / printed p. 259-260\", now gives a page for each quote it covers. The opening locator of What it is (\"PDF p. 270-271 / printed p. 259-260\") is unchanged. |\n| claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves.md (was `claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves.md`) | **Renamed** with `mv`.<br>**Title** is now \"Ivanov (1966): Isometric Training Raised Isometric Strength Most, Eccentric Training Raised the Squat 1RM Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump\". Checked against PDF 272 / p.261 (image): squat 1RM eccentric 15 kg (largest), isometric strength isometric 30.2 kg (largest), vertical jump +3.7 cm with concentric training only. There is no eccentric test.<br>**Why it works:** the gloss is moved out of the authors' voice. New text: \"\"the neuromuscular specificity of strength\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). They give no further mechanism. Inference: read as each mode training the nervous system for that mode; the book does not say this.\" |\n| claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md (was `claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass.md`) | **Renamed** with `mv`.<br>**Title** is now \"Lighter and Heavier Implements for Speed-Strength: Choose a Mass That Keeps Technique, Size the Heavy One per Athlete, and Transfer Can Run One Way\" (see Disagreements 1).<br>**When to apply:** \"kept close to the competition mass, alternated with the normal implement, with the heavy option sized per athlete by whether technique holds\" is now \"chosen so technique holds, alternated with the normal implement, with the heavy option sized per athlete\". The Bondarchuk quote is now introduced as \"Inference: Bondarchuk goes further and argues for small deviations from competition weight; his correlation data: ...\".<br>**When NOT:** added \"Inference: several of the book's own examples are far from competition mass (a 3 kg javelin training weight; a 0.6-0.8 kg puck; 2 kg bats), so the page does not support a \"stay close to competition mass\" rule; only the shot and baseball data fit it, and the water polo result fits only in rank order (both medicine balls are far heavier than a match ball, and the lighter one did better).\"<br>**Related:** the Bondarchuk annotation \"(agrees: close to competition weight transfers best)\" is now \"(Bondarchuk's position: close to competition weight transfers best; this page does not test that)\". The old annotation contradicted the corrected body. |\n| heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix.md | **What it is (gap 2):** added the section's closing summary: \"The section closes on two levers: \"Perfecting the methods of developing speed-strength is a result of the search for a specific combination of means that exploit\" \"the positive after-effect of preceding work on the subsequent activity\" and \"the use of exercises in which muscular force opposes the inertia of the load and not its weight\" (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267, verified against the page image). The after-effect lever is the arm-throw experiment in [[case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work]].\" The arm-throw case was not linked from this entry before.<br>**Related:** the implement-mass link now points to the new slug. |\n| _scope_supertraining_ch5a.txt | Lines 10 and 16 now list the two new filenames. Still 19 entries. |\n\n## Renames and inbound links\n\n| old slug | new slug | inbound links updated |\n|---|---|---|\n| claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves | claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves | `framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md` (Related) |\n| claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass | claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete | `heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix.md` (Related) |\n\nBefore the renames I grepped all of `staging/library`, `/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library` and the 5a/5b/5c scope files for both old slugs. The only hits were the two links above and the 5a scope file. No vault file links either old slug, and no 5b or 5c file does either. The review file and this log still mention the old slugs by design.\n\n## Disagreements with the review\n\n1. **Implement-mass title: I kept \"Transfer Can Run One Way\" instead of the review's \"Expect One-Way Transfer\".** The page supports one-way transfer only in two places. Egstrom is about precision, not speed (\"throwing the light ball improves precision for throwing the heavier ball, but the reverse effect does not occur\"). The shot transfer is \"apparently ... possible only by alternating\" (PDF 276-277 / p.265-266, image). \"Expect\" would overstate both, so the existing, weaker clause stays. The rest of the review's title is applied.\n2. **Implement mass: \"only the shot and baseball data fit it\" is refined, not copied.** Water polo also fits the closer-is-better direction by rank: the 2 kg ball beat the 4 kg ball, and 4 kg harmed technique (PDF 276 / p.265, image). It does not fit a \"stay close\" rule, because both balls are far heavier than a match ball. The added Inference says so. The match-ball comparison is outside-book knowledge, so it sits inside that Inference.\n3. **Newton II: the review's wording is tightened in three places.** The physics agrees with the review; the changes make it exact.\n   - Its (e) reads \"in general impulse = m(v_final - v_initial)\". Bare \"impulse\" repeats the looseness the review flagged in (c), so I wrote \"the impulse of the net external force equals m(v_final - v_initial)\".\n   - Its \"net propulsive impulse\" fixes take-off velocity only if the propulsive phase starts at zero vertical velocity, so I added \"which starts at zero vertical velocity at the bottom of the countermovement\".\n   - Its projectile example now carries \"(ignoring air resistance)\".\n   I also made two small changes the review did not list. The scope sentence now says its Δv is the change in velocity, not the book's ΔV, so it does not appear to contradict (e). The Why it works jump sentence is limited to \"a jump from a still start\".\n4. **Berger: one qualifier added.** Conclusion 5 (\"Training twice a week with 3 sets of the 10RM is as effective as training with 3 sets of 10RM three times a week\", PDF 271 / p.260) traces to the Berger 1963 frequency sentence. That sentence's regime is \"10RM for one set three times per week\" (PDF 270 / p.259). The entry now says that conclusion 5 speaks of 3 sets where the page describes one.\n5. **Three methods: the clash explanation carries \"Inference:\".** The review's replacement sentence (\"six reps cannot be done at 90% or more if five reps is 89%\") is reasoning, not the authors' statement, so it is labelled.\n\nNo review claim was found wrong on the page. Every flagged problem was confirmed on the rendered image before it was changed.\n\n## Proof\n\nGrep for the new wording, the old wording and the old slugs, run from `staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky`:\n\n```\n== Newton II\nwith Δv the change in velocity, not the book's ΔV\nfor a jump from a still start\nnet propulsive impulse (vertical ground reaction force minus body weight, integrated over the propulsive phase, which starts at zero vertical velocity at the bottom of the countermovement)\nFive sentences in the passage\nnet external force on the body.\nA projectile under gravity (ignoring air resistance) changes direction continuously while the force on it stays constant, so a change in velocity direction does not require a change in force, only a net force.\nin general the impulse of the net external force equals m(v_final - v_initial)\nDo not use (c), (d) or (e) as stated\n== Newton old wording (expect none)\n0\n== Dynamic correspondence\nThe paragraph that closes 5.1's dynamics discussion, after Fig 5.1\nInference: this passage is the authors' own statement\n== Three methods\nEric's baseline: off-season strength work prioritizes\nline 105, Inferred, transcript and shared slides\nInference: the 90-95% with 3-6 reps pairing clearly clashes with 5RM = 89% (six reps cannot be done at 90% or more if five reps is 89%)\n[[claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves]]\n== DeLorme\nthe book gives no subject populations for them. Inference: the DeLorme lineage began in rehabilitation, so untrained or patient samples are likely, but the page does not say so.\n== Berger\nmost weightlifting training is done with 1-3 repetitions for advanced weightlifters\ndrawn partly from data outside the five-programme list\nwhere that sentence describes one set\nTreat conclusion 6 as reported without its data\n== Ivanov\n# Ivanov (1966): Isometric Training Raised Isometric Strength Most, Eccentric Training Raised the Squat 1RM Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump\nInference: read as each mode training the nervous system for that mode; the book does not say this.\n== Implement mass\n# Lighter and Heavier Implements for Speed-Strength: Choose a Mass That Keeps Technique, Size the Heavy One per Athlete, and Transfer Can Run One Way\nchosen so technique holds, alternated with the normal implement, with the heavy option sized per athlete\nInference: Bondarchuk goes further and argues for small deviations from competition weight\nso the page does not support a \"stay close to competition mass\" rule\nBondarchuk's position: close to competition weight transfers best; this page does not test that\n== Loading\nThe section closes on two levers\n[[case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work]]\n[[claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete]]\n== Old slugs in staging/library, scope files, vault (expect nothing)\n(exit 1)\n== New files on disk\nclaim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md\nclaim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves.md\n== em dashes outside section headers in edited files (expect none)\n(end)\n== wrapped wikilinks (expect none)\n(end)\n```\n\nThe old-slug grep covered `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library`, the 5a, 5b and 5c scope files, and `/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library`. Exit 1 means no match.\n\nLint (Mac-path copy), run from the Flint root:\n\n```\n$ ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 114, 115]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1\n```\n\nThe one issue is the known `_book.md` false positive (title and table dashes). It is outside this fix scope; `_book.md` was not touched.\n\nFor the promoter: confidence tallies are unchanged. The Newton entry stays `proven`, scoped to the identity. The two renamed entries keep `supported`. The review's optional reciprocal-link suggestions were not applied, because they touch GO entries and 5b/5c files outside this fix scope.\n","numLines":173,"startLine":1,"totalLines":173}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"85b9e968-7023-4f05-b7c2-0581c42f8368","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
