{"parentUuid":"18f9b039-4dad-4841-ba43-885b5808bbc1","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a943b4c4c92ae7c73","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffcHQ4rnbmowfY5wsiLb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MzKyCuUK3ZiAtWWPNrThpG","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging; mkdir -p /tmp/r2_5a; ~/.local/bin/uv run --with pymupdf python3 -c \"\nimport pymupdf; d=pymupdf.open('books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf')\nfor n in range(266,279):\n    open(f'/tmp/r2_5a/p{n}.txt','w').write(d[n].get_text())\n    d[n].get_pixmap(dpi=110).save(f'_renders/r2_5a_p{n}.png')\nprint('ok')\n\"; cat /tmp/r2_5a/p267.txt","description":"Extract text and render pages 266-278"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3428,"cache_read_input_tokens":74160,"output_tokens":1374,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1080},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3428},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1374,"cache_read_input_tokens":74160,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3428,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3428,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01MzKyCuUK3ZiAtWWPNrThpG":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging; mkdir -p /tmp/r2_5a; ~/.local/bin/uv run --with pymupdf python3 -c \"\nimport pymupdf; d=pymupdf.open('books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf')\nfor n in range(266,279):\n    open(f'/tmp/r2_5a/p{n}.txt','w').write(d[n].get_text())\n    d[n].get_pixmap(dpi=110).save(f'_renders/r2_5a_p{n}.png')\nprint('ok')\n\"; cat /tmp/r2_5a/p267.txt","description":"Extract text and render pages 266-278"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffcHPojYUmpsGxmh8rXj","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"493e40be-2b69-4af7-a529-109004c78de3","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:20:53.075Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"493e40be-2b69-4af7-a529-109004c78de3","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"cd7a4cde-2781-465b-b264-9d057dc89d63","agentId":"a943b4c4c92ae7c73","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MzKyCuUK3ZiAtWWPNrThpG","type":"tool_result","content":"ok\nSt/PERTRA/NJNG\nIn other words, force F equals mass m times acceleration a. This is the familiar basic form o f Newton II that is \napplied in most sporting situations, since ongoing loss of mass (like the expulsion of exhaust gases derived from a \ngiven mass m of fuel used to propel jet aircraft) does not occur in sport. It should be noted that acceleration and \nvelocity are vector quantities, so that any change in direction, and not only magnitude, will also be associated with \na change in force.\nThe manner in which the force is generated was discussed earlier (2.2.1), by examining the rate of change of \nforce. The maximum value of this term, (dF/dt) or Rate of Force Development (RFD), was used to define an \nindex of explosive strength IES.\nIt is essential now to distinguish between force that is generated ballistically or impulsively versus force that \nis produced more slowly. In the latter case, it is useful to refer to the momentum m .v o f the body, but with \nrespect to brief transient force development, it is conventional to talk about the impulse I of the force. In this case, \nequation (5.1) is used to define the concept o f impulse. The impulse I is obtained by taking the integral of both \nsides of this equation. Here the brief-acting force is very large and the time interval during the impulsive phase is \nAT. The final velocity reached at the end of this phase is AV.\nEven if neither F nor AT is known, the impulse may be calculated from the change in momentum, m.AV.\nIn Ch 2.2.2 we used this information to compare the performances of two different athletes in executing the \nsame exercise, but it is relevant to repeat it here. Both athletes were instructed to perform a single maximal \nrepetition of this exercise as rapidly as possible and to hold the load until fatigue forced them to stop. Their \nresulting force-time curves (Fig 5.1) show that athlete B exerts a greater maximal force and continues to produce \nforce for longer than athlete A. However, at any instant Tj between 0 and time T2 , athlete A is able to exert \ngreater force than athlete B. If the sport concerned requires rapid RFD, then athlete A will have the advantage.\nThis quality is essential in any sports which involve jumping, striking or throwing, such as basketball, martial \narts and track-and-field. In this case, any training aimed at increasing B's maximal strength or bulk will be \nmisdirected, bccause he needs to concentrate on explosive strength (RFD) training. If the sport requires a high \nmaximal force or a large amount of momentum to be exerted irrespective of time, then athlete B will prove to be \nsuperior. Athlete A will not improve unless he trains to increase maximal strength.\nThe area under the curve (i.e. the momentum) describing athlete B's performance is greater than the \ncorresponding area for athlete A, like the total duration of his curve (i.e. reflecting muscle endurance), so that B \nhas a distinct advantage in any activity that requires great momentum or great muscle endurance during a single \nheavy effort. This situation occurs in events such as wrestling, powerlifting and scrummaging in rugby.\nFigure 5.1 Force curves FI and F2, produced by different athletes in reaching and attempting to maintain their respective maximum \nforces for as long as possible in a given exercise.\nMechanical position during movement is preserved only within a known range, since the shape of the force-time \ncurve is determined by the characteristics o f the neuromuscular system, imparting to it the ability to develop \nmuscular force with the speed necessary to produce the required motor effect. This ability to control muscular \nactivity and movement in space and time is a specific property of the neuromuscular system and requires \nspecialised means of training. A lack of effective neuromuscular training leads to errors and can cost the athlete \nyears of hard and fruitless work.\n254\n256","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"71454107-2202-41a0-9840-5c70419aaac8","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:20:56.890Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"ok\nSt/PERTRA/NJNG\nIn other words, force F equals mass m times acceleration a. This is the familiar basic form o f Newton II that is \napplied in most sporting situations, since ongoing loss of mass (like the expulsion of exhaust gases derived from a \ngiven mass m of fuel used to propel jet aircraft) does not occur in sport. It should be noted that acceleration and \nvelocity are vector quantities, so that any change in direction, and not only magnitude, will also be associated with \na change in force.\nThe manner in which the force is generated was discussed earlier (2.2.1), by examining the rate of change of \nforce. The maximum value of this term, (dF/dt) or Rate of Force Development (RFD), was used to define an \nindex of explosive strength IES.\nIt is essential now to distinguish between force that is generated ballistically or impulsively versus force that \nis produced more slowly. In the latter case, it is useful to refer to the momentum m .v o f the body, but with \nrespect to brief transient force development, it is conventional to talk about the impulse I of the force. In this case, \nequation (5.1) is used to define the concept o f impulse. The impulse I is obtained by taking the integral of both \nsides of this equation. Here the brief-acting force is very large and the time interval during the impulsive phase is \nAT. The final velocity reached at the end of this phase is AV.\nEven if neither F nor AT is known, the impulse may be calculated from the change in momentum, m.AV.\nIn Ch 2.2.2 we used this information to compare the performances of two different athletes in executing the \nsame exercise, but it is relevant to repeat it here. Both athletes were instructed to perform a single maximal \nrepetition of this exercise as rapidly as possible and to hold the load until fatigue forced them to stop. Their \nresulting force-time curves (Fig 5.1) show that athlete B exerts a greater maximal force and continues to produce \nforce for longer than athlete A. However, at any instant Tj between 0 and time T2 , athlete A is able to exert \ngreater force than athlete B. If the sport concerned requires rapid RFD, then athlete A will have the advantage.\nThis quality is essential in any sports which involve jumping, striking or throwing, such as basketball, martial \narts and track-and-field. In this case, any training aimed at increasing B's maximal strength or bulk will be \nmisdirected, bccause he needs to concentrate on explosive strength (RFD) training. If the sport requires a high \nmaximal force or a large amount of momentum to be exerted irrespective of time, then athlete B will prove to be \nsuperior. Athlete A will not improve unless he trains to increase maximal strength.\nThe area under the curve (i.e. the momentum) describing athlete B's performance is greater than the \ncorresponding area for athlete A, like the total duration of his curve (i.e. reflecting muscle endurance), so that B \nhas a distinct advantage in any activity that requires great momentum or great muscle endurance during a single \nheavy effort. This situation occurs in events such as wrestling, powerlifting and scrummaging in rugby.\nFigure 5.1 Force curves FI and F2, produced by different athletes in reaching and attempting to maintain their respective maximum \nforces for as long as possible in a given exercise.\nMechanical position during movement is preserved only within a known range, since the shape of the force-time \ncurve is determined by the characteristics o f the neuromuscular system, imparting to it the ability to develop \nmuscular force with the speed necessary to produce the required motor effect. This ability to control muscular \nactivity and movement in space and time is a specific property of the neuromuscular system and requires \nspecialised means of training. A lack of effective neuromuscular training leads to errors and can cost the athlete \nyears of hard and fruitless work.\n254\n256","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":13,"oldLines":15,"newStart":13,"newLines":17,"lines":[" "," # Grade Plyometric Intensity by Momentum at Contact (Set by Drop Height), Since a 1RM Does Not Exist; Prefer Height Over Added Weight"," ","-**What it is** — The authors propose \"a *powermetrics intensity scale* similar to that used in weightlifting, where exercises are ranked in terms of percentages of one's 1RM.\" Because \"the concept of 1RMs is meaningless in plyometric training\", one must use estimates of muscle tension or of \"the impulse ... produced during the contact phase\". \"Impulse is equivalent to the change of momentum (m.ΔV) during the amortisation phase, so that its magnitude for a falling body depends on the height from which the body drops. Thus, the momentum just before contact (estimated from height of the drop jump or distance fallen by the object) may serve as a measure of intensity.\" At the top of the scale is maximal plyometrics, \"the equivalent of the emotionally demanding competitive maximum, CFmax\"; at the bottom, preparatory non-impact plyometrics (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272, verified against the page image).","+**What it is** — The text (Siff, using his term \"powermetrics\", PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268) proposes \"a *powermetrics intensity scale* similar to that used in weightlifting, where exercises are ranked in terms of percentages of one's 1RM.\" Because \"the concept of 1RMs is meaningless in plyometric training\", one must use estimates of muscle tension or of \"the impulse ... produced during the contact phase\". \"Impulse is equivalent to the change of momentum (m.ΔV) during the amortisation phase, so that its magnitude for a falling body depends on the height from which the body drops. Thus, the momentum just before contact (estimated from height of the drop jump or distance fallen by the object) may serve as a measure of intensity.\" At the top of the scale is maximal plyometrics, \"the equivalent of the emotionally demanding competitive maximum, CFmax\"; at the bottom, preparatory non-impact plyometrics (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272, verified against the page image)."," ","-The prescription list adds: \"The magnitude of the impulsive load is determined by the weight and the height of its free-fall. The optimal combination is determined empirically in each specific case; however, preference should be given to a large height over a heavy weight. Since the resulting force is governed by Newton's Second Law (F = m.a), a force plate may be used to determine the height of drop to produce the required force\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, item 1, verified against the page image). Item 4 says rep dosage can be set more precisely \"to calculate the force involved (from bodymass and the drop height) and the athlete's level of special speed-strength\" (same page).","+The authors' prescription list adds: \"The magnitude of the impulsive load is determined by the weight and the height of its free-fall. The optimal combination is determined empirically in each specific case; however, preference should be given to a large height over a heavy weight. Since the resulting force is governed by Newton's Second Law (F = m.a), a force plate may be used to determine the height of drop to produce the required force\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, item 1, verified against the page image). Item 4 says rep dosage can be set more precisely \"to calculate the force involved (from bodymass and the drop height) and the athlete's level of special speed-strength\" (same page)."," ","-**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning is the impulse-momentum statement quoted. Inference (physics): for a body falling from rest through height h, speed at contact is √(2gh), so momentum at contact is m·√(2gh). Momentum therefore rises with the square root of drop height, not in proportion to it: doubling the drop height raises contact momentum by a factor of √2, about 41%, and quadrupling it doubles momentum. Inference (physics): the peak force on landing is not fixed by drop height alone; for the same momentum, a shorter, stiffer arrest gives a higher average force (average net force = change in momentum divided by contact time). That is why the authors' force plate step is needed to find the height that gives a target force.","+The prescription list is introduced by Fig 5.9, \"Plyometric exercises for strength development, using resistance and other apparatus (Verkhoshansky, 1977)\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, verified against the page image). The text says it shows \"Practical application of the plyometric method to different muscle groups\" and that, with these examples \"as a guideline, an athlete of any specialisation requiring explosive strength can select the necessary complex of exercises personally\" (same page). As drawn (small figures, so descriptions are approximate): drop push-ups between raised blocks, feet on the floor; a pulley-and-weight-stack series of arm pushes and pulls, a squat pull, leg kicks and swings, an overhead pull and a seated row; incline-board exercises with an arm push and a sliding seat; and a seated kick against a suspended or wall-mounted apparatus. Inference: in the pulley drills the falling weight stack supplies the \"weight and the height of its free-fall\" of item 1, so the same height-over-weight rule applies to upper-body and trunk work.","+","+**Why it works** — Siff's reasoning is the impulse-momentum statement quoted. Inference (physics): for a body falling from rest through height h, speed at contact is √(2gh), so momentum at contact is m·√(2gh). Momentum therefore rises with the square root of drop height, not in proportion to it: doubling the drop height raises contact momentum by a factor of √2, about 41%, and quadrupling it doubles momentum. Inference (physics): the peak force on landing is not fixed by drop height alone; for the same momentum, a shorter, stiffer arrest gives a higher average force (average net force = change in momentum divided by contact time). That is why the authors' force plate step is needed to find the height that gives a target force."," "," **When to apply** — Progressing depth jumps or rebounds: rank drills by drop height and bodymass rather than counting all \"plyos\" as equal, and raise height before adding external load. Inference: with ForceDecks, a drop jump from a known box gives landing force and contact time for each athlete, which is the measurement the authors' item 1 asks for."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — Momentum at contact is an intensity proxy for impact drills with a fall; it does not grade non-impact or ground-based bounding drills where there is no measured drop. The authors give no numeric scale bands (no \"% of CFmax\" per height). Item 1's \"F = m.a\" sentence does not on its own let you compute landing force from height (see the Inference above). Height also has an upper limit: past the optimum the transition slows (see [[heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact]] and [[claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]]).","+**When NOT to apply** — Momentum at contact is an intensity proxy for impact drills with a fall; it does not grade non-impact or ground-based bounding drills where there is no measured drop. Siff gives no numeric scale bands (no \"% of CFmax\" per height). Item 1's \"F = m.a\" sentence does not on its own let you compute landing force from height (see the Inference above). Height also has an upper limit: past the optimum the transition slows (see [[heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact]] and [[claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]])."," "," **Example** — Inference: a 100 kg athlete stepping off a 0.5 m box lands at about √(2 × 9.81 × 0.5) ≈ 3.1 m/s, about 313 kg·m/s of momentum; from a 1.0 m box about 4.4 m/s and about 443 kg·m/s, roughly 41% more, not double. This calculation is not in the source."," "]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height.md"],"shared":true}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"493e40be-2b69-4af7-a529-109004c78de3","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
