{"parentUuid":"9832487e-878c-4827-81b9-6361713846d6","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a943b4c4c92ae7c73","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffcJreZJCUd8xEUUa3im","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XC8xYihb8Jn2xjvccfg1qK","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; cat /tmp/r2_5a/p266.txt | tail -25; echo \"-----ENTRY-----\"; cat heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement.md","description":"Read p266 text and dynamic correspondence entry"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3158,"cache_read_input_tokens":80263,"output_tokens":421,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":207},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3158},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":421,"cache_read_input_tokens":80263,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3158,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3158,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01XC8xYihb8Jn2xjvccfg1qK":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; cat /tmp/r2_5a/p266.txt | tail -25; echo \"-----ENTRY-----\"; cat heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement.md","description":"Read p266 text and dynamic correspondence entry"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffcJpmCgL3XB5vtsFsZs","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"080d3c19-0fe7-46ca-b6f8-d5f071188f35","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:21:04.888Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"080d3c19-0fe7-46ca-b6f8-d5f071188f35","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"cd7a4cde-2781-465b-b264-9d057dc89d63","agentId":"a943b4c4c92ae7c73","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XC8xYihb8Jn2xjvccfg1qK","type":"tool_result","content":"developing muscular strength, because they can misdirect one’s thinking. Training methods must be based on a \nclear understanding of the processes underlying all sporting movement. \nAt a physiological level, muscular \ncontraction controlled by nervous processes is the source of all voluntary movement, determining, in particular, \nits speed and the working effect as a whole. At a biomechanical level, the force-time curve for human movement \nmay be regarded as the starting point for analysing all types of strength production. Thus, it is vital to gain a \ngood working knowledge of the most important features of both neuromuscular control and movement \nbiomechanics.\nThe significance of physiological processes was discussed in depth in the preceding chapters; therefore, it is \nthe objective of this section to offer further background on the fundamental biomechanical features of training. \nThe biomechanical foundations of the major motor qualities of strength, including maximum strength, explosive \nstrength, acceleration strength and starting strength, were covered in Section 2.2, where the importance of the \nForce-Time and Rate of Force Development curves was stressed. Force-Time, Force-Velocity and other graphs \ndescribing isometric, dynamic and ballistic movements, as well as Hill's classic mathematical relationship between \nmuscular force and velocity of movement (3.3.2) were discussed in Chapter 3. The characteristics o f power \nproduction were covered in Section 4.3.4. It remains the task here to understand the implications for strength \ntraining of the basic laws of dynamics, particularly Newton's 2nd Law of Motion.\nSome Im plications of the Laws of Dynam ics\nIf the Force-Time curve is to be regarded as the graphical starting point for sport biomcchanics, then Newton's \n2nd Law of Motion must logically serve as the corresponding mathematical starting point.\nIn its original form, Newton II stated that Force is proportional to the rate o f change o f momentum o f the \nmass concerned, where momentum (the tendency of a body to keep in motion) = m.v. Thus, if the appropriate \nunits of measurement are used, Newton II may be written in calculus form as:\n5\n255\n-----ENTRY-----\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.1\"\ntopic: misreading dynamic correspondence\ncategory: [dynamic-correspondence, exercise-selection, transfer]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Dynamic Correspondence Means Matching the Sport's Motor Characteristics, Not Copying the Sport Movement Under Load\n\n**What it is** — The authors warn of \"an excessive passion for making the strength training means correspond structurally to the special exercises.\" The misinterpretation of dynamic correspondence \"lies in literally copying the special exercise in training movements, instead of selecting a specific arsenal of means corresponding to the most important motor characteristics of the given sport and controlling their growth by creating conditions under which they will develop steadily.\" In their words, \"it is inappropriate to try to simulate sports specific movements with supplementary training activities,\" because this \"not only does ... not accurately develop the necessary fitness and motor abilities, but it can also alter the neuromuscular programmes which control the motor actions.\" The aim is \"the specific type of fitness and motor characteristics of the sport\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257, verified against the page image). The paragraph that closes 5.1's dynamics discussion, after Fig 5.1, sets this up: the shape of the force-time curve depends on the neuromuscular system's ability to develop force \"with the speed necessary to produce the required motor effect,\" which \"requires specialised means of training,\" and the authors go as far as \"All subsequent training and performance errors are caused by inappropriate neuromuscular programming\" (PDF p. 267-268 / printed p. 256-257).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that a loaded copy of a skill changes the skill's neuromuscular programme while missing the motor characteristic that matters. They give no further mechanism or data on these pages. Inference: the five criteria in 4.3 (amplitude and direction, accentuated region, dynamics of effort, rate and time of force, regime of work) are the tool for picking a \"specific arsenal of means\" by characteristic rather than by look.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: the Principle of Dynamic Correspondence \"governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 103, Explicit, Q1). Inference: this passage is the authors' own statement of that \"not surface resemblance\" rule. Use it when a coach asks for a loaded version of a sport skill (a weighted throwing motion, a banded route stem, a loaded sprint drill that changes the mechanics) and the question is whether it trains the quality or only resembles the skill.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors do not ban all resisted sport movements: in 4.3 they discuss parachute resistance for sprinters (see [[claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence]]), and in 5.2.3 they write that \"good results are achieved by making the special exercise more demanding,\" with water resistance in rowing or very small limb loads enhancing speed-strength \"under conditions that are very close to those of the special exercise\" (PDF p. 277-278 / printed p. 266-267). Inference: the line is whether the load changes the movement's structure; light loads that leave the pattern intact fall outside this warning. The sweeping sentence \"All subsequent training and performance errors are caused by inappropriate neuromuscular programming\" is an assertion without evidence on the page; do not quote it as a finding.\n\n**Example** — Inference: the source gives no named example here. A heavy cable \"simulated\" football block that slows and reshapes the punch would be the literal copy the authors warn against; a heavy push or pull that matches the punch's accentuated region and rate of force, plus fast unloaded punches, would be the \"arsenal of means\" approach.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]]; [[heuristic--train-coupled-hip-flexion-extension-without-heavily-loaded-simulation]]; [[claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence]]; [[principle--specificity-of-training-ten-dimensions]]; [[elite-sports-performance--evans/case-study--dynamic-correspondence-applied-to-wrestling-takedown]]","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"3a8ea8fd-10f6-4dc8-acc7-9274d109a7ec","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:21:05.063Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"developing muscular strength, because they can misdirect one’s thinking. Training methods must be based on a \nclear understanding of the processes underlying all sporting movement. \nAt a physiological level, muscular \ncontraction controlled by nervous processes is the source of all voluntary movement, determining, in particular, \nits speed and the working effect as a whole. At a biomechanical level, the force-time curve for human movement \nmay be regarded as the starting point for analysing all types of strength production. Thus, it is vital to gain a \ngood working knowledge of the most important features of both neuromuscular control and movement \nbiomechanics.\nThe significance of physiological processes was discussed in depth in the preceding chapters; therefore, it is \nthe objective of this section to offer further background on the fundamental biomechanical features of training. \nThe biomechanical foundations of the major motor qualities of strength, including maximum strength, explosive \nstrength, acceleration strength and starting strength, were covered in Section 2.2, where the importance of the \nForce-Time and Rate of Force Development curves was stressed. Force-Time, Force-Velocity and other graphs \ndescribing isometric, dynamic and ballistic movements, as well as Hill's classic mathematical relationship between \nmuscular force and velocity of movement (3.3.2) were discussed in Chapter 3. The characteristics o f power \nproduction were covered in Section 4.3.4. It remains the task here to understand the implications for strength \ntraining of the basic laws of dynamics, particularly Newton's 2nd Law of Motion.\nSome Im plications of the Laws of Dynam ics\nIf the Force-Time curve is to be regarded as the graphical starting point for sport biomcchanics, then Newton's \n2nd Law of Motion must logically serve as the corresponding mathematical starting point.\nIn its original form, Newton II stated that Force is proportional to the rate o f change o f momentum o f the \nmass concerned, where momentum (the tendency of a body to keep in motion) = m.v. Thus, if the appropriate \nunits of measurement are used, Newton II may be written in calculus form as:\n5\n255\n-----ENTRY-----\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.1\"\ntopic: misreading dynamic correspondence\ncategory: [dynamic-correspondence, exercise-selection, transfer]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Dynamic Correspondence Means Matching the Sport's Motor Characteristics, Not Copying the Sport Movement Under Load\n\n**What it is** — The authors warn of \"an excessive passion for making the strength training means correspond structurally to the special exercises.\" The misinterpretation of dynamic correspondence \"lies in literally copying the special exercise in training movements, instead of selecting a specific arsenal of means corresponding to the most important motor characteristics of the given sport and controlling their growth by creating conditions under which they will develop steadily.\" In their words, \"it is inappropriate to try to simulate sports specific movements with supplementary training activities,\" because this \"not only does ... not accurately develop the necessary fitness and motor abilities, but it can also alter the neuromuscular programmes which control the motor actions.\" The aim is \"the specific type of fitness and motor characteristics of the sport\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257, verified against the page image). The paragraph that closes 5.1's dynamics discussion, after Fig 5.1, sets this up: the shape of the force-time curve depends on the neuromuscular system's ability to develop force \"with the speed necessary to produce the required motor effect,\" which \"requires specialised means of training,\" and the authors go as far as \"All subsequent training and performance errors are caused by inappropriate neuromuscular programming\" (PDF p. 267-268 / printed p. 256-257).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that a loaded copy of a skill changes the skill's neuromuscular programme while missing the motor characteristic that matters. They give no further mechanism or data on these pages. Inference: the five criteria in 4.3 (amplitude and direction, accentuated region, dynamics of effort, rate and time of force, regime of work) are the tool for picking a \"specific arsenal of means\" by characteristic rather than by look.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: the Principle of Dynamic Correspondence \"governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 103, Explicit, Q1). Inference: this passage is the authors' own statement of that \"not surface resemblance\" rule. Use it when a coach asks for a loaded version of a sport skill (a weighted throwing motion, a banded route stem, a loaded sprint drill that changes the mechanics) and the question is whether it trains the quality or only resembles the skill.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors do not ban all resisted sport movements: in 4.3 they discuss parachute resistance for sprinters (see [[claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence]]), and in 5.2.3 they write that \"good results are achieved by making the special exercise more demanding,\" with water resistance in rowing or very small limb loads enhancing speed-strength \"under conditions that are very close to those of the special exercise\" (PDF p. 277-278 / printed p. 266-267). Inference: the line is whether the load changes the movement's structure; light loads that leave the pattern intact fall outside this warning. The sweeping sentence \"All subsequent training and performance errors are caused by inappropriate neuromuscular programming\" is an assertion without evidence on the page; do not quote it as a finding.\n\n**Example** — Inference: the source gives no named example here. A heavy cable \"simulated\" football block that slows and reshapes the punch would be the literal copy the authors warn against; a heavy push or pull that matches the punch's accentuated region and rate of force, plus fast unloaded punches, would be the \"arsenal of means\" approach.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]]; [[heuristic--train-coupled-hip-flexion-extension-without-heavily-loaded-simulation]]; [[claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence]]; [[principle--specificity-of-training-ten-dimensions]]; [[elite-sports-performance--evans/case-study--dynamic-correspondence-applied-to-wrestling-takedown]]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"080d3c19-0fe7-46ca-b6f8-d5f071188f35","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
