trypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"5892a4e4-b075-464f-8301-42af43292333","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"cd7a4cde-2781-465b-b264-9d057dc89d63","agentId":"a943b4c4c92ae7c73","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Tq1ofSUucBFbeiE9kKyvee","type":"tool_result","content":"L61: - No sport performs true 1RM testing. Maxes are projected from observation plus the athlete's history. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q10): \"things are ballparked based on other information we're grabbing on the athlete: watching them move in real time, then using historical context to make future programming decisions around projected 1RMs.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**\nL67: - Velocity-based 1RM estimation (linear-displacement transducer, ~10% load increments to 85-90% or a target mean velocity) is preferred over live 1RM attempts to avoid the fatigue and injury risk of true maxing, with published target mean velocities at 1RM (squat 0.3 m/s, bench 0.2 m/s, power hang snatch 1.0 m/s, power hang clean 0.8 m/s). **Legacy (interview 2026-09-28, Q8, Q10)**; was Inferred (\nL73: - Force plates (CMJ testing) were already the department's longitudinal performance-tracking tool well before the 2022-23 wellness-survey experiment, and remained in place after that experiment ended. **Explicit** [2026-02-19-sportsmith-6otb-er.md]\nL76: - The dynamic strength index (DSI: CMJ against isometric mid-thigh pull peak force) chooses between plyometric and max-strength emphasis. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q21): \"we incorporate the countermovement jump and the isometric mid-thigh pull to get a dynamic strength index, to determine whether we need more plyometric training or more max-strength training.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q\nL78: - The isometric seated calf raise is run on the ForceDecks \"Mid-Thigh Pull\" setting: 90-degree ankle/knee position, belt pre-tensioned to a fingertip's gap, 3 maximal 6-second contractions with 15 seconds' rest between reps. **Inferred (protocol)** [protocol-iso-seated-calf-raise.md; revised-force-plate-testing-v3.md]\nL79: - Metric selection for force-plate reporting follows a disciplined framework: pick 1-2 output metrics (the obvious performance result, e.g. jump height), 1-3 driver metrics (what produces the output, e.g. braking/propulsive force), 1-3 strategy metrics (how the athlete achieved it, e.g. phase durations), optional symmetry metrics (L/R differences), and add \"fluffy\" metrics (peak power, average pow\nL96: - Speed is taught as the quality with the shortest ground-contact-time demand (~0.08s male, ~0.10s female at max velocity) and is described as harder to train and more genetically constrained than strength or power, but as the biggest separator between good and great athletes at the elite level. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.11]\nL97: - Issurin's residual training effects (speed fades fastest, 5 ± 3 days; Block Periodization PDF p. 35, as cited in `docs/synthesis/peaking-and-taper-in-season-football.md`) drive speed programming. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q16): \"I believe Issurin. It drives our speed programming in the off-season and what drives sprinting recommendations in season.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q16)** See\nL101: - The Vermeil hierarchy of athletic development (work capacity, then strength, then power, then speed, then sport skill, each level a required foundation for the next) is repeatedly described as the department's programming \"compass.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**; was Inferred (transcript + shared course material) [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.9-10; week1-lecture.md pp.23,26]\nL103: - The Principle of Dynamic Correspondence (Verkhoshansky), matching amplitude/direction of movement, region of force production, dynamics of effort, rate/timing of force production, and regime of muscular work to the sport skill, governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport. **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**; was Inferred (transcript + slides) \nL104: - Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints); single-joint assistance work is kept to a small share of total volume. **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.13; week1-lecture.md p.31]\nL105: - Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work. In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule. **Inferred (transcript + shared slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.20-21; hh\nL107: - Prilepin's chart (total reps and reps-per-set by %1RM) is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment within a training block, used consistently across strength and power teaching. **Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.19; hhpr-5355-class-8-2026.md p.15] Inference: with no true 1RM testing (Q10), any %1RM prescription rests on projected maxes. Ke\nL130: - What Applied Performance actually contributes to RTP is testing and progression, with shared loading decisions, and the involvement has no fixed end point. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q4): \"Testing and progression. I collaborate on loading and progression decisions when needed. It doesn't really end.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q4)** See Tension 14 on \"support\" versus \"doesn't really end\"\nL134: - NordBord hamstring testing, dropped for roster-wide use, is explicitly retained for smaller, targeted return-to-play groups paired with joint-specific isometric testing (ForceFrame-style). **Explicit** [2026-02-20-sportsmith-6otb-nordbord-er.md]\nL141: - Bodyweight-control competency is treated as a marker of injury risk in its own right: an athlete who can bench 400 lb but can't do 10 clean push-ups has \"something out of whack\" that needs fixing regardless of their strength numbers. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.5]\nL181: - In-season programming deliberately waves volume and intensity even when the practice schedule looks repetitive week to week, specifically to avoid training monotony for the athlete. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.21]\nL182: - Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming: incompatible qualities (e.g., maximal strength work and glycolytic-capacity work) are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block — \"you can't ride two horses with one saddle.\" Compatible qualities are trained together; the rest are trained at a retention dose. **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-tr\nL188: - The head coach's system of play and roster identity (e.g., a \"home run hitters\" lineup vs. a \"steal bases\" lineup) is treated as a direct input into whether a team's programming leans toward maximal strength or toward speed/power emphasis. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.16]\nL260:     **Status: RESOLVED 2026-10-02 (Eric).** Keep %1RM and Prilepin prescription, stated explicitly as built on projected maxes (observation plus history, Q10), not tested 1RMs.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"ede4a5dc-42d0-44fa-89d8-8832d9ea8c19","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:21:15.992Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"L61: - No sport performs true 1RM testing. Maxes are projected from observation plus the athlete's history. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q10): \"things are ballparked based on other information we're grabbing on the athlete: watching them move in real time, then using historical context to make future programming decisions around projected 1RMs.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**\nL67: - Velocity-based 1RM estimation (linear-displacement transducer, ~10% load increments to 85-90% or a target mean velocity) is preferred over live 1RM attempts to avoid the fatigue and injury risk of true maxing, with published target mean velocities at 1RM (squat 0.3 m/s, bench 0.2 m/s, power hang snatch 1.0 m/s, power hang clean 0.8 m/s). **Legacy (interview 2026-09-28, Q8, Q10)**; was Inferred (\nL73: - Force plates (CMJ testing) were already the department's longitudinal performance-tracking tool well before the 2022-23 wellness-survey experiment, and remained in place after that experiment ended. **Explicit** [2026-02-19-sportsmith-6otb-er.md]\nL76: - The dynamic strength index (DSI: CMJ against isometric mid-thigh pull peak force) chooses between plyometric and max-strength emphasis. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q21): \"we incorporate the countermovement jump and the isometric mid-thigh pull to get a dynamic strength index, to determine whether we need more plyometric training or more max-strength training.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q\nL78: - The isometric seated calf raise is run on the ForceDecks \"Mid-Thigh Pull\" setting: 90-degree ankle/knee position, belt pre-tensioned to a fingertip's gap, 3 maximal 6-second contractions with 15 seconds' rest between reps. **Inferred (protocol)** [protocol-iso-seated-calf-raise.md; revised-force-plate-testing-v3.md]\nL79: - Metric selection for force-plate reporting follows a disciplined framework: pick 1-2 output metrics (the obvious performance result, e.g. jump height), 1-3 driver metrics (what produces the output, e.g. braking/propulsive force), 1-3 strategy metrics (how the athlete achieved it, e.g. phase durations), optional symmetry metrics (L/R differences), and add \"fluffy\" metrics (peak power, average pow\nL96: - Speed is taught as the quality with the shortest ground-contact-time demand (~0.08s male, ~0.10s female at max velocity) and is described as harder to train and more genetically constrained than strength or power, but as the biggest separator between good and great athletes at the elite level. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.11]\nL97: - Issurin's residual training effects (speed fades fastest, 5 ± 3 days; Block Periodization PDF p. 35, as cited in `docs/synthesis/peaking-and-taper-in-season-football.md`) drive speed programming. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q16): \"I believe Issurin. It drives our speed programming in the off-season and what drives sprinting recommendations in season.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q16)** See\nL101: - The Vermeil hierarchy of athletic development (work capacity, then strength, then power, then speed, then sport skill, each level a required foundation for the next) is repeatedly described as the department's programming \"compass.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**; was Inferred (transcript + shared course material) [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.9-10; week1-lecture.md pp.23,26]\nL103: - The Principle of Dynamic Correspondence (Verkhoshansky), matching amplitude/direction of movement, region of force production, dynamics of effort, rate/timing of force production, and regime of muscular work to the sport skill, governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport. **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**; was Inferred (transcript + slides) \nL104: - Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints); single-joint assistance work is kept to a small share of total volume. **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.13; week1-lecture.md p.31]\nL105: - Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work. In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule. **Inferred (transcript + shared slides)** [0119