{"parentUuid":"310534b7-e2fb-4230-a13f-559c8cb4d284","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a943b4c4c92ae7c73","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffcLPzUmi3FhC45XBga5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FU93mq4NEy22gkLunt4A8u","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; cat claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves.md; echo \"-----P272-----\"; cat /tmp/r2_5a/p272.txt","description":"Read Ivanov entry and page 272"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3561,"cache_read_input_tokens":98132,"output_tokens":715,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":524},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3561},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":715,"cache_read_input_tokens":98132,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3561,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3561,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01FU93mq4NEy22gkLunt4A8u":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; cat claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves.md; echo \"-----P272-----\"; cat /tmp/r2_5a/p272.txt","description":"Read Ivanov entry and page 272"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffcLNLkzL9BsTmkzZ3jC","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"a82c02b4-6238-4877-bfe6-950151f14bf6","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:21:28.338Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"a82c02b4-6238-4877-bfe6-950151f14bf6","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"cd7a4cde-2781-465b-b264-9d057dc89d63","agentId":"a943b4c4c92ae7c73","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FU93mq4NEy22gkLunt4A8u","type":"tool_result","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.1\"\ntopic: concentric eccentric isometric training comparison\ncategory: [specificity, eccentric, isometric]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Ivanov (1966): Isometric Training Raised Isometric Strength Most, Eccentric Training Raised the Squat 1RM Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump\n\n**What it is** — In \"A 3-month experiment comparing the effectiveness of concentric, eccentric and isometric work,\" \"the largest increase in a 1RM squat occurred with eccentric work (average 15 kg), and the smallest with isometric tension (9.2 kg).\" \"Not unexpectedly, the largest increase in isometric strength was obtained with isometric tension (30.2 kg), and the smallest increase with concentric work (14.6 kg).\" \"Vertical jump increased only with the concentric training regime (3.7 cm)\"; it \"decreased by 1.6 cm for the eccentric regime and by 5.4 cm for isometric tension (Ivanov, 1966)\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261, numbers verified against the page image; the text layer misreads 30.2 as \"iJO.2\"). The authors' conclusion: \"this research does not support the effectiveness of eccentric work as much as it corroborates the principle of the neuromuscular specificity of strength, which dictates the method of its development.\" Around it they report that isometric training does not beat dynamic lifting (Salter 1955; Denison et al 1961; Asmussen 1949; Darcus 1955; Rose et al 1951), that dynamic 3 x 5-6 beats \"the corresponding amount of isometric work (Rasch & Morehouse, 1957; Berger, 1962),\" that \"Comparison with explosive isometrics, however, has not yet been done,\" and that comment on eccentric training \"should be guarded\" until \"more reliable results are obtained\" (PDF p. 271-272 / printed p. 260-261).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is \"the neuromuscular specificity of strength\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). They give no further mechanism. Inference: read as each mode training the nervous system for that mode; the book does not say this.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: the dynamic strength index from the CMJ and the isometric mid-thigh pull \"chooses between plyometric and max-strength emphasis\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 76, Explicit, Q21; tagged contested there). Inference: Ivanov's result is a reminder that an isometric test (IMTP) will rise most with isometric work and a jump test most with concentric, fast work, so a change in DSI after a block can reflect the training mode as much as a real shift in the athlete's profile. Read the two tests against what was trained.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Group sizes, subjects, exercises and loads for Ivanov (1966) are not given. Inference: a squat 1RM is decided by its concentric phase, yet eccentric training raised it most, which the authors do not discuss; that is a reason not to over-read the specificity story from one study. The \"explosive isometrics\" comparison was not done, so the isometric result applies to slow isometrics only. The book's other eccentric findings disagree in places; see [[claim--eccentric-vs-concentric-training-adaptation-contradictory-findings]].\n\n**Example** — The authors' own data: isometric training gave the largest isometric strength gain (30.2 kg) and lowered the vertical jump by 5.4 cm (Ivanov 1966; PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--velocity-and-contraction-specificity-asymmetric-transfer]] (Ch 1, the same specificity point from other studies); [[claim--eccentric-vs-concentric-training-adaptation-contradictory-findings]]; [[framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric]]; [[heuristic--isometric-training-execution-protocol-and-advantages-drawbacks]]\n-----P272-----\nSUPERTRAINING\nThe same is the case with static flexibility-isometric routines using 5-10 stretches and 6-second isometric \ncontractions (Denison et al, 1961). Similar conclusions were obtained in other studies (Asmusscn, 1949; Darcus, \n1955; Rose et al, 1951; Berger, 1962). It has been shown that dynamic work executed for 3 sets o f 5 or 6 \nrepetitions is more effective than the corresponding amount of isometric work (Rasch & Morehouse, 1957; \nBerger, 1962). Comparison with explosive isometrics, however, has not yet been done (see 4.2.4).\nThere has been considerable interest in studying the potential of eccentric exercise with weights for \ndeveloping strength (Verkhoshansky, 1961; Semyonov & Chudinov, 1963; Ivanov, 1966; Semyonov, 1968). The \nfirst positive results in this direction were supported by various specialists and served as the basis for their \nrecommendations on using eccentric work to develop strength (Medvedev & Vorobyev, 1967). However, until \nmore reliable results are obtained, comment on the general effectiveness of eccentric training (’negatives’ in \nbodybuilding jargon) should be guarded (see 4.2.5).\nA 3-month experiment comparing the effectiveness of concentric, eccentric and isometric work showed that \nthe largest increase in a IRM squat occurred with eccentric work (average 15 kg), and the smallest with isometric \ntension (9.2 kg). Not unexpectedly, the largest increase in isometric strength was obtained with isometric tension \niJO.2 kg), and the smallest increase with concentric work (14.6 kg). \nVertical jump increased only with the \nconcentric training regime (3.7 cm). The vertical jump heights decreased by 1.6 cm for the eccentric regime and by\n5.4 cm for isometric tension (Ivanov, 1966). Thus, this research does not support the effectiveness of eccentric \nwork as much as it corroborates the principle of the neuromuscular specificity of strength, which dictates the \nmethod of its development.\nSimilar significant increases in maximum strength can be obtained by the repetitive effort and brief maximal \ntension methods, as well as with isometric tension. However, the strength acquired from each of these methods \nhas its own neuromuscular specificity.\nThe repetitive effort method is appropriate in the initial stages of strength development and when strength \nand its speed of production are not important. Repetitive work with moderate weights (50-60% of IRM) and a \nfairly large number of repetitions (8-12) increases muscle mass. Strength increases more rapidly with heavy \nweights(90-95% of IRM) and fewer repetitions (3-6), this being accompanied by a smaller increase in muscle \nmass. The training effect is enhanced by increasing the magnitude of the load and the volume of work.\nThe brief maximal tension method increases maximum strength without appreciably increasing muscle mass \nand simultaneously improves the ability to display strength quickly. It is appropriate to use this method when \nthe repetitive effort method has become ineffective for strength development and when it is necessary to increase \nstrength quickly in a relatively short period with a small volume of work. The brief maximal tension method is \neffective for maintaining the existing level of strength, the general tonus of the neuromuscular system and sporting \nform. The strength training effect is enhanced by increasing the maximal weight, increasing the average weight \nlifted per session and reducing the number of sets and repetitions.\nIsometric tension (with gradual increase in effort) is useful for developing maximum strength without an \naccompanying increase in muscle mass and for enhancing the general tonus of the neuromuscular system. This \nmethod can be used to maintain the existing level of strength development in cases where speed of movement is \noot important, during the preparatory training of athletes and during the early stages of musculoskeletal \nrehabilitation, when movement might be restricted. An increase in the training effect is achieved primarily through \nthe use of maximal tension which is progressively increased by means of heavier loads as muscular strength \nincreases. It should be remembered that isometric tension may be applied slowly or explosively (sec 4.2.4), the \nchoice of method depending on the athlete's condition and goal of training.\n5.2.2 Autoregulating Progressive R esistance Exercise\nAnother variant of the DeLorme system has been used successfully in physiotherapy by Knight (1979). Known \nas the DAPRE (Daily Adjustable Progressive Resistance Exercise) method, it offers a combination of training and \ntesting for the recovering patient. It requires four sets of exercises to be performed per given muscle group, \nbeginning with a set of 10 repetitions with 50% of one's anticipated 6RM. Set 2 consists of 6 repetitions with \n75°o of the anticipated 6RM, while set 3 with the anticipated 6RM is performed to failure. The number of strict, \nfull repetitions with this weight is used to determine the appropriate load for set 4, according to an adjustment \ntable. Finally, the number of repetitions completed with this set is used to calculate the starting weight for the \nfollowing workout.\n261","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"9b702cbb-92ef-4ee5-ace9-86967435aca4","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:21:28.505Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.2.1\"\ntopic: concentric eccentric isometric training comparison\ncategory: [specificity, eccentric, isometric]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Ivanov (1966): Isometric Training Raised Isometric Strength Most, Eccentric Training Raised the Squat 1RM Most, and Only Concentric Training Raised the Vertical Jump\n\n**What it is** — In \"A 3-month experiment comparing the effectiveness of concentric, eccentric and isometric work,\" \"the largest increase in a 1RM squat occurred with eccentric work (average 15 kg), and the smallest with isometric tension (9.2 kg).\" \"Not unexpectedly, the largest increase in isometric strength was obtained with isometric tension (30.2 kg), and the smallest increase with concentric work (14.6 kg).\" \"Vertical jump increased only with the concentric training regime (3.7 cm)\"; it \"decreased by 1.6 cm for the eccentric regime and by 5.4 cm for isometric tension (Ivanov, 1966)\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261, numbers verified against the page image; the text layer misreads 30.2 as \"iJO.2\"). The authors' conclusion: \"this research does not support the effectiveness of eccentric work as much as it corroborates the principle of the neuromuscular specificity of strength, which dictates the method of its development.\" Around it they report that isometric training does not beat dynamic lifting (Salter 1955; Denison et al 1961; Asmussen 1949; Darcus 1955; Rose et al 1951), that dynamic 3 x 5-6 beats \"the corresponding amount of isometric work (Rasch & Morehouse, 1957; Berger, 1962),\" that \"Comparison with explosive isometrics, however, has not yet been done,\" and that comment on eccentric training \"should be guarded\" until \"more reliable results are obtained\" (PDF p. 271-272 / printed p. 260-261).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is \"the neuromuscular specificity of strength\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). They give no further mechanism. Inference: read as each mode training the nervous system for that mode; the book does not say this.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: the dynamic strength index from the CMJ and the isometric mid-thigh pull \"chooses between plyometric and max-strength emphasis\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 76, Explicit, Q21; tagged contested there). Inference: Ivanov's result is a reminder that an isometric test (IMTP) will rise most with isometric work and a jump test most with concentric, fast work, so a change in DSI after a block can reflect the training mode as much as a real shift in the athlete's profile. Read the two tests against what was trained.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Group sizes, subjects, exercises and loads for Ivanov (1966) are not given. Inference: a squat 1RM is decided by its concentric phase, yet eccentric training raised it most, which the authors do not discuss; that is a reason not to over-read the specificity story from one study. The \"explosive isometrics\" comparison was not done, so the isometric result applies to slow isometrics only. The book's other eccentric findings disagree in places; see [[claim--eccentric-vs-concentric-training-adaptation-contradictory-findings]].\n\n**Example** — The authors' own data: isometric training gave the largest isometric strength gain (30.2 kg) and lowered the vertical jump by 5.4 cm (Ivanov 1966; PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--velocity-and-contraction-specificity-asymmetric-transfer]] (Ch 1, the same specificity point from other studies); [[claim--eccentric-vs-concentric-training-adaptation-contradictory-findings]]; [[framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric]]; [[heuristic--isometric-training-execution-protocol-and-advantages-drawbacks]]\n-----P272-----\nSUPERTRAINING\nThe same is the case with static flexibility-isometric routines using 5-10 stretches and 6-second isometric \ncontractions (Denison et al, 1961). Similar conclusions were obtained in other studies (Asmusscn, 1949; Darcus, \n1955; Rose et al, 1951; Berger, 1962). It has been shown that dynamic work executed for 3 sets o f 5 or 6 \nrepetitions is more effective than the corresponding amount of isometric work (Rasch & Morehouse, 1957; \nBerger, 1962). Comparison with explosive isometrics, however, has not yet been done (see 4.2.4).\nThere has been considerable interest in studying the potential of eccentric exercise with weights for \ndeveloping strength (Verkhoshansky, 1961; Semyonov & Chudinov, 1963; Ivanov, 1966; Semyonov, 1968). The \nfirst positive results in this direction were supported by various specialists and served as the basis for their \nrecommendations on using eccentric work to develop strength (Medvedev & Vorobyev, 1967). However, until \nmore reliable results are obtained, comment on the general effectiveness of eccentric training (’negatives’ in \nbodybuilding jargon) should be guarded (see 4.2.5).\nA 3-month experiment comparing the effectiveness of concentric, eccentric and isometric work showed that \nthe largest increase in a IRM squat occurred with eccentric work (average 15 kg), and the smallest with isometric \ntension (9.2 kg). Not unexpectedly, the largest increase in isometric strength was obtained with isometric tension \niJO.2 kg), and the smallest increase with concentric work (14.6 kg). \nVertical jump increased only with the \nconcentric training regime (3.7 cm). The vertical jump heights decreased by 1.6 cm for the eccentric regime and by\n5.4 cm for isometric tension (Ivanov, 1966). Thus, this research does not support the effectiveness of eccentric \nwork as much as it corroborates the principle of the neuromuscular specificity of strength, which dictates the \nmethod of its development.\nSimilar significant increases in maximum strength can be obtained by the repetitive effort and brief maximal \ntension methods, as well as with isometric tension. However, the strength acquired from each of these methods \nhas its own neuromuscular specificity.\nThe repetitive effort method is appropriate in the initial stages of strength development and when strength \nand its speed of production are not important. Repetitive work with moderate weights (50-60% of IRM) and a \nfairly large number of repetitions (8-12) increases muscle mass. Strength increases more rapidly with heavy \nweights(90-95% of IRM) and fewer repetitions (3-6), this being accompanied by a smaller increase in muscle \nmass. The training effect is enhanced by increasing the magnitude of the load and the volume of work.\nThe brief maximal tension method increases maximum strength without appreciably increasing muscle mass \nand simultaneously improves the ability to display strength quickly. It is appropriate to use this method when \nthe repetitive effort method has become ineffective for strength development and when it is necessary to increase \nstrength quickly in a relatively short period with a small volume of work. The brief maximal tension method is \neffective for maintaining the existing level of strength, the general tonus of the neuromuscular system and sporting \nform. The strength training effect is enhanced by increasing the maximal weight, increasing the average weight \nlifted per session and reducing the number of sets and repetitions.\nIsometric tension (with gradual increase in effort) is useful for developing maximum strength without an \naccompanying increase in muscle mass and for enhancing the general tonus of the neuromuscular system. This \nmethod can be used to maintain the existing level of strength development in cases where speed of movement is \noot important, during the preparatory training of athletes and during the early stages of musculoskeletal \nrehabilitation, when movement might be restricted. An increase in the training effect is achieved primarily through \nthe use of maximal tension which is progressively increased by means of heavier loads as muscular strength \nincreases. It should be remembered that isometric tension may be applied slowly or explosively (sec 4.2.4), the \nchoice of method depending on the athlete's condition and goal of training.\n5.2.2 Autoregulating Progressive R esistance Exercise\nAnother variant of the DeLorme system has been used successfully in physiotherapy by Knight (1979). Known \nas the DAPRE (Daily Adjustable Progressive Resistance Exercise) method, it offers a combination of training and \ntesting for the recovering patient. It requires four sets of exercises to be performed per given muscle group, \nbeginning with a set of 10 repetitions with 50% of one's anticipated 6RM. Set 2 consists of 6 repetitions with \n75°o of the anticipated 6RM, while set 3 with the anticipated 6RM is performed to failure. The number of strict, \nfull repetitions with this weight is used to determine the appropriate load for set 4, according to an adjustment \ntable. Finally, the number of repetitions completed with this set is used to calculate the starting weight for the \nfollowing workout.\n261","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a82c02b4-6238-4877-bfe6-950151f14bf6","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
