{"parentUuid":"7debcc7e-0faf-4169-86a8-c9ed0b189864","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a943b4c4c92ae7c73","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffcP4ADehR7jaAM8GpnA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Xgi7b8X7f6aiFh3o1gTuAh","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; for f in principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities.md case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work.md case-study--alternate-leg-bounding-with-thigh-cuffs-for-sprinters.md; do echo \"=-=-= $f\"; sed -n '13,200p' $f; done","description":"Read three GO entries for spot check"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6045,"cache_read_input_tokens":112181,"output_tokens":639,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":386},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6045},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":639,"cache_read_input_tokens":112181,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6045,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6045,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Xgi7b8X7f6aiFh3o1gTuAh":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; for f in principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities.md case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work.md case-study--alternate-leg-bounding-with-thigh-cuffs-for-sprinters.md; do echo \"=-=-= $f\"; sed -n '13,200p' $f; done","description":"Read three GO entries for spot check"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffcP1wWKvmwHJHv5vY5F","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"3f9673f7-062d-4660-bded-6db6ec937437","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:22:03.760Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"3f9673f7-062d-4660-bded-6db6ec937437","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"cd7a4cde-2781-465b-b264-9d057dc89d63","agentId":"a943b4c4c92ae7c73","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Xgi7b8X7f6aiFh3o1gTuAh","type":"tool_result","content":"=-=-= principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities.md\n\n# Speed-Strength, Strength-Endurance and Speed-Endurance Are Independent Qualities, Not By-Products of Strength, Speed and Endurance\n\n**What it is** — The authors state that \"speed-strength, strength-endurance and speed-endurance are not simply derivatives of strength, speed and endurance, but are totally independent qualities.\" Their evidence is that \"an increase in absolute strength does not necessarily enhance any of these three qualities.\" So the combined qualities \"should be placed alongside the latter in a group, each with its own particular means and methods of development (Verkhoshansky, 1963)\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257). The same paragraph frames force and velocity as qualities that, \"at a high level of development are inversely proportional to one another (see Ch 4),\" and says excessive development of both \"is not required\" because they are \"interrelated aspects\" of all motor activity; depending on the movement, \"one of these qualities achieves greater development\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism beyond the observation that absolute strength gains do not carry over automatically. Inference: \"inversely proportional\" here is the authors' loose wording about trained motor qualities, not a statement of Hill's force-velocity equation, and should not be read as a law of mechanics.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: the Vermeil hierarchy (work capacity, strength, power, speed, sport skill, \"each level a required foundation for the next\") is the department's programming \"compass\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 101, Explicit, Q1). Inference: this principle refines that compass rather than contradicting it: strength can be a foundation for power and speed, but the authors say a strength gain does not by itself buy speed-strength or strength-endurance, so each combined quality needs its own block of means. Use it when a strong athlete's jump, sprint or repeat-effort numbers stall while the squat keeps rising.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The claim is \"does not necessarily enhance,\" not \"never enhances.\" Elsewhere the book records that qualities transfer more in the early stages of training ([[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]]), so for novices a general strength gain may still lift the combined qualities. The authors cite one source (Verkhoshansky 1963) and give no data on this page. Inference: do not use the \"inversely proportional\" sentence to argue that strength training slows athletes; the book's own Ch 2-4 material on the strength-speed continuum is the place for that question.\n\n**Example** — Inference: no example is given on this page. A lineman whose back squat rises over an off-season block while his 10-yard split and repeat-sprint times do not change fits the authors' claim that absolute strength gains need not carry into speed-strength or speed-endurance.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--strength-speed-continuum-five-zones]]; [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]]; [[framework--motor-ability-connection-types-taxonomy]]; [[framework--special-strength-methods-classified-by-sport-group-and-strength-type]] (5.2, the method groups that follow from this split)\n=-=-= case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work.md\n\n# Arm Throw Height After Barbell Presses: The After-Effect Experiment Behind Table 5.3 and Fig 5.4\n\n**What it is** — \"The progressive development of speed-strength is the result of combining means for exploiting the after-effect phenomenon of preceding work to increase the effectiveness of subsequent work.\" In an \"exercise modelling\" experiment, \"the working effect of an explosive movement, as measured by the height to which a load was thrown (Fig 5.4), increases by an average of 38-40% after execution of barbell presses for 3 sets of 3 repetitions with 80% of the 1RM (with a rest interval of 10 minutes between the pressing and the throwing),\" with shorter movement time and greater working path, average speed, driving force and power (PDF p. 276 / printed p. 265). Table 5.3, \"Arm movement characteristics for throwing a weight before and after tonic work (Verkhoshansky, 1977)\" (PDF p. 277 / printed p. 266, verified against the page image):\n\n| Characteristic | First effort | Second effort | Difference (%) as printed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Height of throw (cm) | 44.0 | 61.0 | +38.8 |\n| Movement time (s) | 0.266 | 0.250 | -6.8 |\n| Working path (cm) | 62.0 | 65.0 | +4.8 |\n| Average speed (m/s) | 2.330 | 2.600 | +11.7 |\n| Average driving force (kgf) | 5.550 | 7.430 | +33.8 |\n| Power (kg.m/s) | 1.310 | 1.930 | +47.3 |\n\nFig 5.4, \"The after-effect of prior tonic work on the height of a thrown object (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)\" (PDF p. 276 / printed p. 265, verified against the page image), plots height against \"number of the movement\" (0-20): the first-effort curve runs from about 42 to about 49 cm, the second-effort curve from about 50 cm to about 67-69 cm, levelling after about movement 8. The authors conclude: \"Thus, the repetition of the same stimulus evokes a more pronounced reaction by the body and greater development of strength and speed\" (PDF p. 276 / printed p. 265).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors attribute it to the after-effect of the preceding tonic work; the mechanism is the one in their 3.4.2 after-effect material, not restated here.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline (course material, Inferred): training is \"weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 104). Inference: this is the book's concrete upper-body example for pairing a heavy press with a light throw in one session (for example, heavy bench sets, about 10 minutes' rest, then medicine-ball chest passes), a pairing the department could trial and measure with throw distance or velocity before adopting it.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Several numbers do not check out. Inference, recomputed from the table: 44.0 to 61.0 cm is +38.6%, not +38.8; 0.266 to 0.250 s is -6.0%, not -6.8; speed (+11.6) and force (+33.9) are rounding. The working path divided by time reproduces the average speeds (0.62/0.266 = 2.33; 0.65/0.250 = 2.60). The power row is about one tenth of average force times average speed (12.9 and 19.3 kgf.m/s) but not in exact proportion, and the book does not say how power was derived or what its unit scaling is; do not compute power that way from this table. The table is credited to Verkhoshansky (1977) and the figure to Siff & Verkhoshansky (1999); the figure's second-effort heights reach about 67-69 cm while the table gives 61.0, and the book does not say whether the table is a mean or a different trial. Subjects, n, the thrown load and the throwing apparatus are not given. Inference: \"repetition of the same stimulus\" does not describe a press-then-throw design; the conclusion reads as a general after-effect statement rather than one drawn from this table. The book itself records a Western finding where shot-put performance deteriorated after preliminary throws with a heavier shot (Bischke & Morehouse 1950); see [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]].\n\n**Example** — The authors' own case: after 3 x 3 barbell presses at 80% of 1RM and 10 minutes' rest, the throw height rose from 44.0 to 61.0 cm in Table 5.3 (PDF p. 276-277 / printed p. 265-266).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]] (3.4.2, the after-effect rules); [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]]; [[claim--thrown-load-height-decreases-with-load-increases-with-drop-height]]; [[claim--average-or-peak-force-times-velocity-is-not-power]]; [[heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix]]\n=-=-= case-study--alternate-leg-bounding-with-thigh-cuffs-for-sprinters.md\n\n# Alternate-Leg Bounding for Sprinters: Gather the Planted Leg Through Hip Flexion, 50-100 m, With 100-150 g Thigh Cuffs (Verkhoshansky's Practice)\n\n**What it is** — \"In certain cases, power can be increased without using additional loads. For example, with jumps from one leg to the other, the push-off displays values of power which are greater than in running. Therefore, these jumps are excellent for the special strength training of sprinters.\" They must be done \"by not accentuating the push-off of the body (which does not happen in sprinting), but by active gathering-in of the planted leg (i.e. via hip flexion).\" \"These jumps should be executed repetitively over distances of 50-100 meters as fast as possible. The addition of small cuffs weighing from 100-150 grams on each thigh can makes such exercises more effective in experienced athletes (based on Verkhoshansky's experience with top Russian sprinters, such as Zubov)\" (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267, verified against the page image). The passage names Verkhoshansky in the third person, so this account is Siff's report of Verkhoshansky's coaching practice.\n\n**Why it works** — The given reason is that bounding push-offs show more power than running push-offs, so they overload the sprint push-off without external weight. No power values, method of measurement or source are given. Inference: the cue to gather the planted leg rather than push off keeps the drill close to sprint mechanics, which matches the book's dynamic-correspondence argument, though the passage does not say so.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: Issurin's residual effects drive \"our speed programming in the off-season and ... sprinting recommendations in season\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 97, Explicit, Q16). Inference: bounding for 50-100 m is a candidate speed-strength means for skill players in the off-season speed block, with the hip-flexion \"gather\" cue as the coaching point; thigh cuffs belong only to experienced athletes, as the authors say.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — This is one coach's experience reported second-hand, with no data; the \"greater power than running\" claim has no figures. The cuffs are reserved for \"experienced athletes.\" Inference: 50-100 m of maximal bounding is a high-volume plyometric dose for heavy athletes such as linemen, and the passage gives no set or rep count. Bondarchuk's correlation data support leg-to-leg jumps for sprinters, but are a separate source with its own population. Moyer also argues for gram-level limb loads in sprinting, but the library entry on that claim finds his kinetic-energy numbers do not hold, so it does not add support for the cuffs.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own case: Verkhoshansky's top Russian sprinters, Zubov named, bounding with 100-150 g cuffs on each thigh (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267).\n\n**Related** — [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--sprint-jump-exercises-most-transferable-assistance]] (leg-to-leg jumps for 50 m transferred to 100 m results at all levels); [[fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer/claim--wearable-resistance-kinetic-energy-sprint]]; [[heuristic--train-coupled-hip-flexion-extension-without-heavily-loaded-simulation]]; [[heuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-strength-tempo-rest-and-fatigue]]","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"631435b0-e9b7-43f8-9cd1-de0a2bde4cbd","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:22:03.998Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"=-=-= principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities.md\n\n# Speed-Strength, Strength-Endurance and Speed-Endurance Are Independent Qualities, Not By-Products of Strength, Speed and Endurance\n\n**What it is** — The authors state that \"speed-strength, strength-endurance and speed-endurance are not simply derivatives of strength, speed and endurance, but are totally independent qualities.\" Their evidence is that \"an increase in absolute strength does not necessarily enhance any of these three qualities.\" So the combined qualities \"should be placed alongside the latter in a group, each with its own particular means and methods of development (Verkhoshansky, 1963)\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257). The same paragraph frames force and velocity as qualities that, \"at a high level of development are inversely proportional to one another (see Ch 4),\" and says excessive development of both \"is not required\" because they are \"interrelated aspects\" of all motor activity; depending on the movement, \"one of these qualities achieves greater development\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism beyond the observation that absolute strength gains do not carry over automatically. Inference: \"inversely proportional\" here is the authors' loose wording about trained motor qualities, not a statement of Hill's force-velocity equation, and should not be read as a law of mechanics.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: the Vermeil hierarchy (work capacity, strength, power, speed, sport skill, \"each level a required foundation for the next\") is the department's programming \"compass\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 101, Explicit, Q1). Inference: this principle refines that compass rather than contradicting it: strength can be a foundation for power and speed, but the authors say a strength gain does not by itself buy speed-strength or strength-endurance, so each combined quality needs its own block of means. Use it when a strong athlete's jump, sprint or repeat-effort numbers stall while the squat keeps rising.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The claim is \"does not necessarily enhance,\" not \"never enhances.\" Elsewhere the book records that qualities transfer more in the early stages of training ([[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]]), so for novices a general strength gain may still lift the combined qualities. The authors cite one source (Verkhoshansky 1963) and give no data on this page. Inference: do not use the \"inversely proportional\" sentence to argue that strength training slows athletes; the book's own Ch 2-4 material on the strength-speed continuum is the place for that question.\n\n**Example** — Inference: no example is given on this page. A lineman whose back squat rises over an off-season block while his 10-yard split and repeat-sprint times do not change fits the authors' claim that absolute strength gains need not carry into speed-strength or speed-endurance.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--strength-speed-continuum-five-zones]]; [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]]; [[framework--motor-ability-connection-types-taxonomy]]; [[framework--special-strength-methods-classified-by-sport-group-and-strength-type]] (5.2, the method groups that follow from this split)\n=-=-= case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work.md\n\n# Arm Throw Height After Barbell Presses: The After-Effect Experiment Behind Table 5.3 and Fig 5.4\n\n**What it is** — \"The progressive development of speed-strength is the result of combining means for exploiting the after-effect phenomenon of preceding work to increase the effectiveness of subsequent work.\" In an \"exercise modelling\" experiment, \"the working effect of an explosive movement, as measured by the height to which a load was thrown (Fig 5.4), increases by an average of 38-40% after execution of barbell presses for 3 sets of 3 repetitions with 80% of the 1RM (with a rest interval of 10 minutes between the pressing and the throwing),\" with shorter movement time and greater working path, average speed, driving force and power (PDF p. 276 / printed p. 265). Table 5.3, \"Arm movement characteristics for throwing a weight before and after tonic work (Verkhoshansky, 1977)\" (PDF p. 277 / printed p. 266, verified against the page image):\n\n| Characteristic | First effort | Second effort | Difference (%) as printed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Height of throw (cm) | 44.0 | 61.0 | +38.8 |\n| Movement time (s) | 0.266 | 0.250 | -6.8 |\n| Working path (cm) | 62.0 | 65.0 | +4.8 |\n| Average speed (m/s) | 2.330 | 2.600 | +11.7 |\n| Average driving force (kgf) | 5.550 | 7.430 | +33.8 |\n| Power (kg.m/s) | 1.310 | 1.930 | +47.3 |\n\nFig 5.4, \"The after-effect of prior tonic work on the height of a thrown object (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)\" (PDF p. 276 / printed p. 265, verified against the page image), plots height against \"number of the movement\" (0-20): the first-effort curve runs from about 42 to about 49 cm, the second-effort curve from about 50 cm to about 67-69 cm, levelling after about movement 8. The authors conclude: \"Thus, the repetition of the same stimulus evokes a more pronounced reaction by the body and greater development of strength and speed\" (PDF p. 276 / printed p. 265).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors attribute it to the after-effect of the preceding tonic work; the mechanism is the one in their 3.4.2 after-effect material, not restated here.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline (course material, Inferred): training is \"weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 104). Inference: this is the book's concrete upper-body example for pairing a heavy press with a light throw in one session (for example, heavy bench sets, about 10 minutes' rest, then medicine-ball chest passes), a pairing the department could trial and measure with throw distance or velocity before adopting it.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Several numbers do not check out. Inference, recomputed from the table: 44.0 to 61.0 cm is +38.6%, not +38.8; 0.266 to 0.250 s is -6.0%, not -6.8; speed (+11.6) and force (+33.9) are rounding. The working path divided by time reproduces the average speeds (0.62/0.266 = 2.33; 0.65/0.250 = 2.60). The power row is about one tenth of average force times average speed (12.9 and 19.3 kgf.m/s) but not in exact proportion, and the book does not say how power was derived or what its unit scaling is; do not compute power that way from this table. The table is credited to Verkhoshansky (1977) and the figure to Siff & Verkhoshansky (1999); the figure's second-effort heights reach about 67-69 cm while the table gives 61.0, and the book does not say whether the table is a mean or a different trial. Subjects, n, the thrown load and the throwing apparatus are not given. Inference: \"repetition of the same stimulus\" does not describe a press-then-throw design; the conclusion reads as a general after-effect statement rather than one drawn from this table. The book itself records a Western finding where shot-put performance deteriorated after preliminary throws with a heavier shot (Bischke & Morehouse 1950); see [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]].\n\n**Example** — The authors' own case: after 3 x 3 barbell presses at 80% of 1RM and 10 minutes' rest, the throw height rose from 44.0 to 61.0 cm in Table 5.3 (PDF p. 276-277 / printed p. 265-266).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]] (3.4.2, the after-effect rules); [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]]; [[claim--thrown-load-height-decreases-with-load-increases-with-drop-height]]; [[claim--average-or-peak-force-times-velocity-is-not-power]]; [[heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix]]\n=-=-= case-study--alternate-leg-bounding-with-thigh-cuffs-for-sprinters.md\n\n# Alternate-Leg Bounding for Sprinters: Gather the Planted Leg Through Hip Flexion, 50-100 m, With 100-150 g Thigh Cuffs (Verkhoshansky's Practice)\n\n**What it is** — \"In certain cases, power can be increased without using additional loads. For example, with jumps from one leg to the other, the push-off displays values of power which are greater than in running. Therefore, these jumps are excellent for the special strength training of sprinters.\" They must be done \"by not accentuating the push-off of the body (which does not happen in sprinting), but by active gathering-in of the planted leg (i.e. via hip flexion).\" \"These jumps should be executed repetitively over distances of 50-100 meters as fast as possible. The addition of small cuffs weighing from 100-150 grams on each thigh can makes such exercises more effective in experienced athletes (based on Verkhoshansky's experience with top Russian sprinters, such as Zubov)\" (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267, verified against the page image). The passage names Verkhoshansky in the third person, so this account is Siff's report of Verkhoshansky's coaching practice.\n\n**Why it works** — The given reason is that bounding push-offs show more power than running push-offs, so they overload the sprint push-off without external weight. No power values, method of measurement or source are given. Inference: the cue to gather the planted leg rather than push off keeps the drill close to sprint mechanics, which matches the book's dynamic-correspondence argument, though the passage does not say so.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: Issurin's residual effects drive \"our speed programming in the off-season and ... sprinting recommendations in season\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 97, Explicit, Q16). Inference: bounding for 50-100 m is a candidate speed-strength means for skill players in the off-season speed block, with the hip-flexion \"gather\" cue as the coaching point; thigh cuffs belong only to experienced athletes, as the authors say.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — This is one coach's experience reported second-hand, with no data; the \"greater power than running\" claim has no figures. The cuffs are reserved for \"experienced athletes.\" Inference: 50-100 m of maximal bounding is a high-volume plyometric dose for heavy athletes such as linemen, and the passage gives no set or rep count. Bondarchuk's correlation data support leg-to-leg jumps for sprinters, but are a separate source with its own population. Moyer also argues for gram-level limb loads in sprinting, but the library entry on that claim finds his kinetic-energy numbers do not hold, so it does not add support for the cuffs.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own case: Verkhoshansky's top Russian sprinters, Zubov named, bounding with 100-150 g cuffs on each thigh (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267).\n\n**Related** — [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--sprint-jump-exercises-most-transferable-assistance]] (leg-to-leg jumps for 50 m transferred to 100 m results at all levels); [[fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer/claim--wearable-resistance-kinetic-energy-sprint]]; [[heuristic--train-coupled-hip-flexion-extension-without-heavily-loaded-simulation]]; [[heuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-strength-tempo-rest-and-fatigue]]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3f9673f7-062d-4660-bded-6db6ec937437","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
