{"parentUuid":"e878ecbb-5a09-453b-8c90-e5e99dc9f178","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a943b4c4c92ae7c73","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffcQ1gAKPY4etzJdxsgu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VU9quYvscy4hYnc9c5WSia","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; sed -n '13,200p' framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise.md; echo \"=-=-=\"; sed -n '13,200p' heuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-strength-tempo-rest-and-fatigue.md","description":"Read APRE and cyclic entries"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4645,"cache_read_input_tokens":123005,"output_tokens":485,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":264},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4645},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":485,"cache_read_input_tokens":123005,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4645,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4645,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01VU9quYvscy4hYnc9c5WSia":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; sed -n '13,200p' framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise.md; echo \"=-=-=\"; sed -n '13,200p' heuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-strength-tempo-rest-and-fatigue.md","description":"Read APRE and cyclic entries"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffcPxuiynZGhoHjp6Dgv","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"db783258-8a63-4d2e-aa4d-11fcaf67e3f8","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:22:15.968Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"db783258-8a63-4d2e-aa4d-11fcaf67e3f8","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"685d1925-9566-4313-ae7b-41bf0a7b0668","agentId":"a943b4c4c92ae7c73","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VU9quYvscy4hYnc9c5WSia","type":"tool_result","content":"# APRE: Autoregulating Progressive Resistance Exercise (3RM, 6RM and 10RM Routines With a Rep-Based Load Adjustment)\n\n**What it is** — APRE is the authors' expansion of Knight's (1979) DAPRE (\"Daily Adjustable Progressive Resistance Exercise\"), a DeLorme variant \"used successfully in physiotherapy\" that \"offers a combination of training and testing for the recovering patient.\" DAPRE: set 1, 10 reps at 50% of the anticipated 6RM; set 2, 6 reps at 75%; set 3, the anticipated 6RM to failure; the reps in set 3 set the load for set 4 by an adjustment table, and set 4's reps set the next workout's starting weight (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261, verified against the page image). The authors say DAPRE \"needs to be expanded somewhat\" to fit \"the principles of supercompensation and functional fitness,\" because \"daily training of the same muscle groups using the same resistance regime is neither necessary nor recommended.\" APRE uses \"3RM (strength/power), 6RM (strength/hypertrophy) and 10RM (hypertrophy) regimes\" (PDF p. 273 / printed p. 262). Four sets after a warm-up, with \"2-4 minutes of rest (preferably mild active rest with unloaded movements).\" Table 5.1 (PDF p. 273 / printed p. 262, verified against the page image):\n\n| Set | 6RM routine | 10RM routine | 3RM routine |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 0 | warm-up | warm-up | warm-up |\n| 1 | 10 reps / 50% of 6RM | 12 reps / 50% of 10RM | 6 reps / 50% of 3RM |\n| 2 | 6 reps / 75% of 6RM | 10 reps / 75% of 10RM | 3 reps / 75% of 3RM |\n| 3 | reps to failure / 6RM | reps to failure / 10RM | reps to failure / 3RM |\n| 4 | adjusted reps to failure | adjusted reps to failure | adjusted reps to failure |\n\nTable 5.2, set 4 adjustment from set 3 reps (PDF p. 274 / printed p. 263, transcribed from the page image because the text layer scrambles the columns): 6RM routine, 0-2 reps decrease 2.5-5 kg, 3-4 decrease 0-2.5 kg, 5-7 leave the same, 8-12 increase 2.5-5 kg, 13 or more increase 5-7.5 kg. 10RM routine, 4-6 decrease 2.5-5 kg, 7-8 decrease 0-2.5 kg, 9-11 leave the same, 12-16 increase 2.5-5 kg, 17 or more increase 5-7.5 kg. 3RM routine, 1-2 decrease 2.5-5 kg, 3-4 leave the same, 5-6 increase 2.5-5 kg, 7 or more increase 5-10 kg. Set 4 then fixes the next session's RM load. \"Generally, the 6RM routine should be followed for 6-8 weeks, before the 3RM routine is introduced. The 10RM may be introduced at any stage to improve general muscle endurance and hypertrophy.\" As fitness rises, reps \"may be done more rapidly and a brief ballistic action may be used near the end of the movement, in order to recruit the phasic stretch reflex more powerfully\" (PDF p. 274 / printed p. 263).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' rationale is that the athlete's own reps to failure set the load each session, so training and testing are one act, and that rotating the RM regimes avoids training the same muscles \"using the same resistance regime\" daily (PDF p. 273 / printed p. 262). They give no outcome data for APRE itself; DAPRE is called \"successful\" in physiotherapy without figures.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: \"No sport performs true 1RM testing. Maxes are projected from observation plus the athlete's history\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 61, Explicit, Q10), and in return to play Applied Performance contributes \"Testing and progression\" (line 130, Explicit, Q4). Inference: APRE gives a written, rep-based alternative to projected maxes: the working RM is re-set every session from performance, with no 1RM test, which suits both the no-1RM practice and rehab progressions where Athletic Medicine wants a rule for when load goes up. The Legacy VBT protocol (line 67) did the same job with bar speed; APRE does it with reps and needs no device.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The source is inconsistent on the adjustment rule. The DAPRE text says \"If he can perform more than 6 repetitions, the weight is too light and must be increased. Conversely, if fewer than 6 repetitions can be completed strictly, the weight is too heavy,\" with adjustments \"according to a table of guidelines, as given below\" (PDF p. 273 / printed p. 262), but the table below leaves the 6RM load unchanged at 5-7 reps (PDF p. 274 / printed p. 263). The 10RM column has no row for 0-3 reps and the 3RM column none for 0 reps. Inference: the kg steps are absolute, so the same 2.5-5 kg change is a large step on a small-muscle lift and a small one on a squat; and to-failure sets every session add fatigue that the authors do not address for in-season athletes. Isometric and testing uses of APRE are in their own entries.\n\n**Example** — Inference, applying Table 5.2 as printed: an athlete on the 6RM routine gets 9 reps on set 3, so set 4 goes up 2.5-5 kg; if set 4 then yields 6 reps, that load stays as the next session's 6RM.\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--apre-skill-learning-6rm-pyramid-test-and-rm-muscle-testing]]; [[heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol]]; [[claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared]]; [[heuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity]] (names APRE as the isokinetic alternative); [[velocity-based-training-implementation-guide-from-texas-am]] (daily autoregulation by bar velocity)\n=-=-=\n\n# Cyclic Versus Acyclic Speed-Strength: Tempo, Rest and Fatigue Are Handled Differently\n\n**What it is** — The authors separate speed-strength in acyclic and cyclic movements (PDF p. 277-278 / printed p. 266-267, verified against the page images). **Rest**: the interval is \"determined by level of fitness, the level of special endurance to repeatedly produce maximal tension and the degree of strength exerted\"; \"Experiments show that, with an optimal pause of 0.5-1 minute, speed-strength output can be maintained at a high level for a fairly long period without serious deterioration,\" and the decline with \"exhausting, monotonous work can be largely eliminated by creating a favourable emotional state.\" **Cyclic speed-strength** is \"repetitive contractions separated by relaxation phases,\" so \"the movement tempo and the duration of work are of great significance.\" \"The resistance and the movement tempo are inversely proportional\": more resistance lowers tempo and \"promotes the rapid onset of fatigue,\" so the combination of resistance and tempo \"should be selected in each specific case, based on the kinesiological structure of the given exercise.\" \"Speed of movement decreases over a long period of training done at a slow tempo and increases through training at the optimal speed (Korobkov, 1953; Monogarov, 1958).\" The criterion is \"the ability to correctly execute the full movement cycle, including the appropriate phases of tension and muscular relaxation,\" and \"The movement tempo should gradually approach the tempo of the special exercise and even exceed it, while the working time should lengthen (Diachkov, 1961).\" Making the special exercise \"more demanding\" also works: \"water resistance in rowing or very small loads on the limbs of track-and-field athletes and gymnasts.\" **Fatigue**: \"Fatigue must be avoided when one is trying to develop speed-strength for acyclic movements. However, fatigue is a necessary component of training for speed-strength in cyclic movements which require speed-endurance.\" The authors close that precise implementation \"is possible only under specific training conditions so, for the present, one has to rely on empirical methods\" (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons are the inverse resistance-tempo relation and the need to keep relaxation phases in the cycle; they give no further mechanism.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline (course material, Inferred): speed is the quality with the shortest ground-contact demand and is \"harder to train\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 96), and \"Issurin's residual training effects ... drive speed programming\" (line 97, Explicit, Q16). Inference: for a sprint or a repeated football action (cyclic or near-cyclic), the authors' rules say build tempo up to and past game tempo while lengthening the work, accept fatigue only when speed-endurance is the goal, and keep pure speed-strength (acyclic starts, jumps, throws) fresh.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — \"inversely proportional\" is the authors' loose wording for \"more resistance, lower tempo,\" not a measured law. The 0.5-1 minute rest comes from unnamed \"experiments\" with no exercise, load or population given; Inference: for maximal sprints or jumps that rest is short, so treat it as untested and do not apply it to maximal acyclic efforts without checking output stays high. \"Favourable emotional state\" is not defined. The authors themselves say the rules rest on \"empirical methods.\"\n\n**Example** — The authors' own examples of a more demanding special exercise: water resistance in rowing, and very small loads on the limbs of track-and-field athletes and gymnasts (PDF p. 277-278 / printed p. 266-267).\n\n**Related** — [[principle--training-effort-must-match-or-exceed-sport-effort-judged-by-movement-speed]] (4.3.3, the same \"match or exceed\" rule for effort); [[heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix]]; [[claim--three-zone-force-velocity-curve-for-cyclic-locomotion]]; [[case-study--alternate-leg-bounding-with-thigh-cuffs-for-sprinters]]","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"cb3f537a-b4be-4cda-955d-e63bb0eafde2","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:22:16.139Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# APRE: Autoregulating Progressive Resistance Exercise (3RM, 6RM and 10RM Routines With a Rep-Based Load Adjustment)\n\n**What it is** — APRE is the authors' expansion of Knight's (1979) DAPRE (\"Daily Adjustable Progressive Resistance Exercise\"), a DeLorme variant \"used successfully in physiotherapy\" that \"offers a combination of training and testing for the recovering patient.\" DAPRE: set 1, 10 reps at 50% of the anticipated 6RM; set 2, 6 reps at 75%; set 3, the anticipated 6RM to failure; the reps in set 3 set the load for set 4 by an adjustment table, and set 4's reps set the next workout's starting weight (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261, verified against the page image). The authors say DAPRE \"needs to be expanded somewhat\" to fit \"the principles of supercompensation and functional fitness,\" because \"daily training of the same muscle groups using the same resistance regime is neither necessary nor recommended.\" APRE uses \"3RM (strength/power), 6RM (strength/hypertrophy) and 10RM (hypertrophy) regimes\" (PDF p. 273 / printed p. 262). Four sets after a warm-up, with \"2-4 minutes of rest (preferably mild active rest with unloaded movements).\" Table 5.1 (PDF p. 273 / printed p. 262, verified against the page image):\n\n| Set | 6RM routine | 10RM routine | 3RM routine |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 0 | warm-up | warm-up | warm-up |\n| 1 | 10 reps / 50% of 6RM | 12 reps / 50% of 10RM | 6 reps / 50% of 3RM |\n| 2 | 6 reps / 75% of 6RM | 10 reps / 75% of 10RM | 3 reps / 75% of 3RM |\n| 3 | reps to failure / 6RM | reps to failure / 10RM | reps to failure / 3RM |\n| 4 | adjusted reps to failure | adjusted reps to failure | adjusted reps to failure |\n\nTable 5.2, set 4 adjustment from set 3 reps (PDF p. 274 / printed p. 263, transcribed from the page image because the text layer scrambles the columns): 6RM routine, 0-2 reps decrease 2.5-5 kg, 3-4 decrease 0-2.5 kg, 5-7 leave the same, 8-12 increase 2.5-5 kg, 13 or more increase 5-7.5 kg. 10RM routine, 4-6 decrease 2.5-5 kg, 7-8 decrease 0-2.5 kg, 9-11 leave the same, 12-16 increase 2.5-5 kg, 17 or more increase 5-7.5 kg. 3RM routine, 1-2 decrease 2.5-5 kg, 3-4 leave the same, 5-6 increase 2.5-5 kg, 7 or more increase 5-10 kg. Set 4 then fixes the next session's RM load. \"Generally, the 6RM routine should be followed for 6-8 weeks, before the 3RM routine is introduced. The 10RM may be introduced at any stage to improve general muscle endurance and hypertrophy.\" As fitness rises, reps \"may be done more rapidly and a brief ballistic action may be used near the end of the movement, in order to recruit the phasic stretch reflex more powerfully\" (PDF p. 274 / printed p. 263).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' rationale is that the athlete's own reps to failure set the load each session, so training and testing are one act, and that rotating the RM regimes avoids training the same muscles \"using the same resistance regime\" daily (PDF p. 273 / printed p. 262). They give no outcome data for APRE itself; DAPRE is called \"successful\" in physiotherapy without figures.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: \"No sport performs true 1RM testing. Maxes are projected from observation plus the athlete's history\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 61, Explicit, Q10), and in return to play Applied Performance contributes \"Testing and progression\" (line 130, Explicit, Q4). Inference: APRE gives a written, rep-based alternative to projected maxes: the working RM is re-set every session from performance, with no 1RM test, which suits both the no-1RM practice and rehab progressions where Athletic Medicine wants a rule for when load goes up. The Legacy VBT protocol (line 67) did the same job with bar speed; APRE does it with reps and needs no device.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The source is inconsistent on the adjustment rule. The DAPRE text says \"If he can perform more than 6 repetitions, the weight is too light and must be increased. Conversely, if fewer than 6 repetitions can be completed strictly, the weight is too heavy,\" with adjustments \"according to a table of guidelines, as given below\" (PDF p. 273 / printed p. 262), but the table below leaves the 6RM load unchanged at 5-7 reps (PDF p. 274 / printed p. 263). The 10RM column has no row for 0-3 reps and the 3RM column none for 0 reps. Inference: the kg steps are absolute, so the same 2.5-5 kg change is a large step on a small-muscle lift and a small one on a squat; and to-failure sets every session add fatigue that the authors do not address for in-season athletes. Isometric and testing uses of APRE are in their own entries.\n\n**Example** — Inference, applying Table 5.2 as printed: an athlete on the 6RM routine gets 9 reps on set 3, so set 4 goes up 2.5-5 kg; if set 4 then yields 6 reps, that load stays as the next session's 6RM.\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--apre-skill-learning-6rm-pyramid-test-and-rm-muscle-testing]]; [[heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol]]; [[claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared]]; [[heuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity]] (names APRE as the isokinetic alternative); [[velocity-based-training-implementation-guide-from-texas-am]] (daily autoregulation by bar velocity)\n=-=-=\n\n# Cyclic Versus Acyclic Speed-Strength: Tempo, Rest and Fatigue Are Handled Differently\n\n**What it is** — The authors separate speed-strength in acyclic and cyclic movements (PDF p. 277-278 / printed p. 266-267, verified against the page images). **Rest**: the interval is \"determined by level of fitness, the level of special endurance to repeatedly produce maximal tension and the degree of strength exerted\"; \"Experiments show that, with an optimal pause of 0.5-1 minute, speed-strength output can be maintained at a high level for a fairly long period without serious deterioration,\" and the decline with \"exhausting, monotonous work can be largely eliminated by creating a favourable emotional state.\" **Cyclic speed-strength** is \"repetitive contractions separated by relaxation phases,\" so \"the movement tempo and the duration of work are of great significance.\" \"The resistance and the movement tempo are inversely proportional\": more resistance lowers tempo and \"promotes the rapid onset of fatigue,\" so the combination of resistance and tempo \"should be selected in each specific case, based on the kinesiological structure of the given exercise.\" \"Speed of movement decreases over a long period of training done at a slow tempo and increases through training at the optimal speed (Korobkov, 1953; Monogarov, 1958).\" The criterion is \"the ability to correctly execute the full movement cycle, including the appropriate phases of tension and muscular relaxation,\" and \"The movement tempo should gradually approach the tempo of the special exercise and even exceed it, while the working time should lengthen (Diachkov, 1961).\" Making the special exercise \"more demanding\" also works: \"water resistance in rowing or very small loads on the limbs of track-and-field athletes and gymnasts.\" **Fatigue**: \"Fatigue must be avoided when one is trying to develop speed-strength for acyclic movements. However, fatigue is a necessary component of training for speed-strength in cyclic movements which require speed-endurance.\" The authors close that precise implementation \"is possible only under specific training conditions so, for the present, one has to rely on empirical methods\" (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons are the inverse resistance-tempo relation and the need to keep relaxation phases in the cycle; they give no further mechanism.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline (course material, Inferred): speed is the quality with the shortest ground-contact demand and is \"harder to train\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 96), and \"Issurin's residual training effects ... drive speed programming\" (line 97, Explicit, Q16). Inference: for a sprint or a repeated football action (cyclic or near-cyclic), the authors' rules say build tempo up to and past game tempo while lengthening the work, accept fatigue only when speed-endurance is the goal, and keep pure speed-strength (acyclic starts, jumps, throws) fresh.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — \"inversely proportional\" is the authors' loose wording for \"more resistance, lower tempo,\" not a measured law. The 0.5-1 minute rest comes from unnamed \"experiments\" with no exercise, load or population given; Inference: for maximal sprints or jumps that rest is short, so treat it as untested and do not apply it to maximal acyclic efforts without checking output stays high. \"Favourable emotional state\" is not defined. The authors themselves say the rules rest on \"empirical methods.\"\n\n**Example** — The authors' own examples of a more demanding special exercise: water resistance in rowing, and very small loads on the limbs of track-and-field athletes and gymnasts (PDF p. 277-278 / printed p. 266-267).\n\n**Related** — [[principle--training-effort-must-match-or-exceed-sport-effort-judged-by-movement-speed]] (4.3.3, the same \"match or exceed\" rule for effort); [[heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix]]; [[claim--three-zone-force-velocity-curve-for-cyclic-locomotion]]; [[case-study--alternate-leg-bounding-with-thigh-cuffs-for-sprinters]]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"db783258-8a63-4d2e-aa4d-11fcaf67e3f8","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
