trength-stay-close' . | grep -v -e '^./review_' -e '^./fixes_'; echo \"exit $?\"; grep -c '\\[\\[' library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/{claim--newton*,claim--implement-mass*,claim--ivanov*} ; sed -n '13,40p' library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--analytical-synthetic-and-multifaceted-preparation-suit-only-early-training.md","description":"Grep old slugs across staging; read analytical entry"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffcRqYGidQ9DeikTdtWL","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"8de1f2e8-2b52-493f-ac26-0a339d519017","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:22:42.229Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"8de1f2e8-2b52-493f-ac26-0a339d519017","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"685d1925-9566-4313-ae7b-41bf0a7b0668","agentId":"a943b4c4c92ae7c73","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WMHd81bpgxZkLhTcbK47vM","type":"tool_result","content":"review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md\nfixes_supertraining_ch5a_applied.md\nexit 0\nlibrary/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md:2\nlibrary/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves.md:2\nlibrary/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md:2\n\n# Developing Qualities Separately and Then Combining Them (and Cross Training) Suits Only Early or Transitional Stages\n\n**What it is** — The authors describe the first attempts to train the combined qualities as the \"analytical-synthetic method\": \"the qualities should be developed separately with the appropriate means and then they should be integrated into the special exercises\" (analysis breaks a process into components; synthesis combines them into a whole \"different from each of its components\"). Examples: sprints for gymnasts and weightlifters, barbell work for track athletes, and cyclic sports for general endurance (Verkhoshansky 1977). They grant these recommendations are hard to deny, but say they \"are appropriate only for the initial stages of training and it would be a serious error for highly-qualified athletes to use them casually\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257). Multi-faceted preparation as \"the leading training principle\" is \"partly true and only under certain circumstances,\" because it rests on observing single-factor adaptation, not on \"quantitative analysis of the interrelation among all the factors involved.\" Transfer between qualities occurs \"primarily in the initial stages\" (Yakovlev 1968; Zimkin 1965; Zatsiorsky 1965), and with rising performance multi-faceted preparation \"inevitably opposes the law of gradual development\" and \"can hinder the body's specialised adaptation\" (Verkhoshansky 1977). The authors apply this to Western \"cross training\": concurrent use of varied means or similar sports \"to prevent stagnation can be counterproductive and valuable only during particular transitional stages,\" and even then means should be combined by \"the most appropriate sequentiality, simultaneity or scheme of superposition\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257; cross training is taken up again in 5.5).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that transfer between qualities is real but limited to early training, so a programme built on transfer stops working as the athlete specializes. They give no physiological mechanism. Inference: this fits the book's broader specificity argument, where adaptation narrows toward the trained task as qualification rises.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline (course material, Inferred): \"Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming: incompatible qualities ... are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 182). Inference: this heuristic supports that rule for advanced athletes, and adds a time dimension: varied general means are reasonable for freshmen and for transition periods (post-season, return from a layoff), but a senior's main blocks should be organised around the sport's specific demands, with general means placed by sequence rather than mixed in to \"keep it fresh.\"\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not read it as \"general training is useless.\" The authors keep a place for general means in early and transitional stages, and Bompa's long-term model builds multilateral development into the early years ([[periodization--bompa/framework--ltad-sequential-model]]), which agrees on that point. The book also prints a passage in Ch 2 that \"the development of each quality positively influences the development of the others\" (see [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]]), so the book is not uniform on how much qualities help each other; that entry records the tension. The cited studies are 1960s Soviet work with no data given here.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own examples: barbell exercises for track athletes and sprints for weightlifters and gymnasts are fine early, but \"a serious error for highly-qualified athletes to use them casually\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]]; [[principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities]]; [[framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order]] (sequencing of means); [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--training-modality-compatibility-in-a-workout]]; [[periodization--bompa/framework--ltad-sequential-model]]","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"00b6a022-eb4e-480f-b10c-02dae727a00e","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:22:42.495Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md\nfixes_supertraining_ch5a_applied.md\nexit 0\nlibrary/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md:2\nlibrary/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves.md:2\nlibrary/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md:2\n\n# Developing Qualities Separately and Then Combining Them (and Cross Training) Suits Only Early or Transitional Stages\n\n**What it is** — The authors describe the first attempts to train the combined qualities as the \"analytical-synthetic method\": \"the qualities should be developed separately with the appropriate means and then they should be integrated into the special exercises\" (analysis breaks a process into components; synthesis combines them into a whole \"different from each of its components\"). Examples: sprints for gymnasts and weightlifters, barbell work for track athletes, and cyclic sports for general endurance (Verkhoshansky 1977). They grant these recommendations are hard to deny, but say they \"are appropriate only for the initial stages of training and it would be a serious error for highly-qualified athletes to use them casually\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257). Multi-faceted preparation as \"the leading training principle\" is \"partly true and only under certain circumstances,\" because it rests on observing single-factor adaptation, not on \"quantitative analysis of the interrelation among all the factors involved.\" Transfer between qualities occurs \"primarily in the initial stages\" (Yakovlev 1968; Zimkin 1965; Zatsiorsky 1965), and with rising performance multi-faceted preparation \"inevitably opposes the law of gradual development\" and \"can hinder the body's specialised adaptation\" (Verkhoshansky 1977). The authors apply this to Western \"cross training\": concurrent use of varied means or similar sports \"to prevent stagnation can be counterproductive and valuable only during particular transitional stages,\" and even then means should be combined by \"the most appropriate sequentiality, simultaneity or scheme of superposition\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257; cross training is taken up again in 5.5).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that transfer between qualities is real but limited to early training, so a programme built on transfer stops working as the athlete specializes. They give no physiological mechanism. Inference: this fits the book's broader specificity argument, where adaptation narrows toward the trained task as qualification rises.\n\n**When to apply** — Eric's baseline (course material, Inferred): \"Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming: incompatible qualities ... are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 182). Inference: this heuristic supports that rule for advanced athletes, and adds a time dimension: varied general means are reasonable for freshmen and for transition periods (post-season, return from a layoff), but a senior's main blocks should be organised around the sport's specific demands, with general means placed by sequence rather than mixed in to \"keep it fresh.\"\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not read it as \"general training is useless.\" The authors keep a place for general means in early and transitional stages, and Bompa's long-term model builds multilateral development into the early years ([[periodization--bompa/framework--ltad-sequential-model]]), which agrees on that point. The book also prints a passage in Ch 2 that \"the development of each quality positively influences the development of the others\" (see [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]]), so the book is not uniform on how much qualities help each other; that entry records the tension. The cited studies are 1960s Soviet work with no data given here.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own examples: barbell exercises for track athletes and sprints for weightlifters and gymnasts are fine early, but \"a serious error for highly-qualified athletes to use them casually\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]]; [[principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities]]; [[framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order]] (sequencing of means); [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--training-modality-compatibility-in-a-workout]]; [[periodization--bompa/framework--ltad-sequential-model]]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[],"moreFiles":0,"shared":true}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8de1f2e8-2b52-493f-ac26-0a339d519017","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
