{"parentUuid":"96a13fd3-26f4-4356-92d8-18643598e88f","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a943b4c4c92ae7c73","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffcbJQ3cxavNaJMuZDbt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2218,"cache_read_input_tokens":152441,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2218,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":461,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffcbBLQDrh3zg3vtcDwp","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"01c1ab61-195e-4707-a3df-87a93f9ad9d2","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:24:45.245Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"01c1ab61-195e-4707-a3df-87a93f9ad9d2","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a943b4c4c92ae7c73","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffcbJQ3cxavNaJMuZDbt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018HNP7Lf4Gjv23j13x5o5Mx","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch5a_opus_round2.md","content":"# Review round 2: Supertraining Ch 5a (5.1 to 5.2.3), falsification of the round-1 fixes\n\nReviewer: Claude Opus, independent of the writer and of the fixer. Date: 2026-10-03.\nScope: the 19 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch5a.txt`, folder `staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`. Round 1: `staging/review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md`. Fix log: `staging/fixes_supertraining_ch5a_applied.md`.\nSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx = printed + 11, PDF 266-278. Pages rendered to `staging/_renders/r2_5a_p266.png` ... `r2_5a_p278.png` (plus the crop `r2_5a_p272_ivanov.png`). Opened as images: PDF 267, 268, 269, 270, 271, 272 (Ivanov crop), 274, 276, 277, 278. PDF 266, 273 and 275 were checked from the text layer only (quotes, no numbers in dispute).\nBaseline: lines 61, 76, 96, 97, 101, 103, 104, 105, 107, 130, 182, 260 of `docs/method-baseline.md` re-read; every quote in the files I read matches the cited line, including the rewritten line-105 quote and its \"Inferred (transcript + shared slides)\" tag.\n\nCounts: **GO 18, FIX 1, NO-GO 0.**\n\nRound-1 count note: the round-1 header said \"FIX 8\" but its table has 7 FIX rows; item 8 of its fix list is the inbound-link update. The fixer's reading is correct.\n\n## Part 1: the 7 round-1 FIX entries, re-checked in full\n\n| file | verdict | finding (entry vs source, with page) | exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md | GO | All four round-1 problems are gone. Physics checked line by line against PDF 267 / p.256 (image): (1) `proven` scope is the identity only, \"∫F_net dt = mΔv, with Δv the change in velocity, not the book's ΔV\": correct for constant mass. (2) Why it works: integrating F_net = m dv/dt gives the impulse-momentum identity for any curve shape; \"for a jump from a still start, the take-off velocity fixes the net impulse\": correct. (3) When to apply: \"net propulsive impulse (vertical ground reaction force minus body weight, integrated over the propulsive phase, which starts at zero vertical velocity at the bottom of the countermovement)\": correct; net impulse over that phase = m.v_takeoff because v starts at 0. (4) (c): area under a force-time curve is that force's impulse and equals a change in momentum only for the net external force: correct; \"hold the load until fatigue forced them to stop\" is on the page. (5) (d): a projectile under constant gravity changes direction with a constant force, so a direction change needs a net force, not a change in force: correct, and it now counters the book's \"associated with a change in force\" sentence instead of confirming it. (6) (e): the book's integral runs 0 to ΔV and \"The final velocity reached at the end of this phase is ΔV\" (image): quoted correctly; \"m.ΔV equals the change in momentum only when the body starts from rest; in general the impulse of the net external force equals m(v_final - v_initial)\": correct. (a) and (b) unchanged and correct. Fixer disagreement 3 (net-force wording, zero-velocity start, \"ignoring air resistance\") is justified; each tightening makes the physics exact. No new error. | none. Nit (optional): When NOT opens \"Five sentences ... should not be quoted as physics\" but closes \"Do not use (c), (d) or (e) as stated\"; (a) is also not to be quoted, so \"(a), (c), (d) or (e)\" would be consistent. |\n| heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement.md | GO | \"The paragraph that closes 5.1's dynamics discussion, after Fig 5.1\" is now right: the neuromuscular paragraph sits below Fig 5.1 at the foot of PDF 267 and \"All subsequent training and performance errors ...\" opens PDF 268 (both images). \"Inference: this passage is the authors' own statement ...\" is labelled. All quotes re-verified on PDF 268 / p.257 image; the 5.2.3 \"more demanding\" quotes on PDF 277-278. Baseline line 103 quote matches. | none |\n| framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md | GO | (4) now separates the clear clash (90-95% with 3-6 reps vs 5RM \"about 89%\") from the loose one (85-95% = 3-5RM), labelled Inference (fixer disagreement 5 is right: it is reasoning, not the authors'). The logic holds: if 5RM is 89%, five or six reps at 90% or more is impossible. Numbers: \"about 89% and 74%\" PDF 269 (image); \"85-95% of 1RM, i.e. 3-5RM\", \"more than three (Berger, 1962)\", \"5-6 exercises for 6-10 sets of 1-3\" PDF 271 (image); \"90-95% of 1RM\" / \"(3-6)\", \"reducing the number of sets and repetitions\", \"Similar significant increases\" PDF 272 (image). The line-105 fragment is now a full sentence with the \"Eric's baseline:\" prefix and a verbatim quote of line 105. Ivanov link points to the new slug and resolves. | none |\n| claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared.md | GO | \"mostly in rehabilitation or untrained subjects\" is gone; replacement says the book gives no populations and labels the rehabilitation point Inference. Re-verified (images): 89%/74%, DeLorme 50%/75%/10RM (Hettinger, 1961) PDF 269; Zinovieff 1951, McCloy 1954, \"add nothing new\", 154/142, 212.8/186 (Faulkner, 1950), 5.5%, McMorris & Elks 1954, Krusen 25/50/75% vs 5RM/125%/150% PDF 270; Narikashvili 1960 PDF 271. | none |\n| claim--berger-sets-reps-and-frequency-studies-in-novices.md | GO | Traceability sentence now matches the page: conclusion 1's comparator and conclusion 5 trace to Berger 1963 (\"10RM for one set three times per week\"; \"no major difference between ... 2 or 3 training sessions per week\", PDF 270 image); conclusion 6 has no data. Fixer disagreement 4 is justified: conclusion 5 reads \"3 sets of the 10RM\" (PDF 271 image) while the Berger 1963 sentence says \"one set\" (PDF 270 image). The added 1-3 rep sentence is verbatim from PDF 270 (image), placed correctly after Vorobyev 1971 (PDF 270), with Vorobyev 1978 \"1-6\" on PDF 271. All five programmes, six conclusions, the italic novice caveat and the recovery quote re-verified on the images. | none. Nit (optional): \"conclusion 3 traces loosely to ...\" is writer reasoning without an \"Inference:\" prefix, unlike the parallel sentence in the three-methods entry. |\n| claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves.md | GO | New title matches the data (PDF 272 crop image): eccentric 15 kg largest squat 1RM gain, isometric 9.2 smallest; isometric 30.2 kg largest isometric gain, concentric 14.6 smallest; vertical jump +3.7 cm concentric only, -1.6 eccentric, -5.4 isometric. The \"trains the nervous system for that mode\" gloss is now labelled Inference with \"the book does not say this\". File renamed; body has no review-history wording. | none |\n| claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md | FIX | Title, When to apply and the Bondarchuk framing are fixed and correct (Bondarchuk sentence confirmed verbatim in the linked vault entry `heuristic--implement-weight-dosing-and-throwing-boom-warning`, line 23). Fixer disagreements 1 and 2 are justified by the page: Egstrom transfer is of \"precision\", and shot transfer \"apparently is possible only by alternating\" (PDF 276-277 images), so \"Can Run One Way\" is the right strength; water polo 2 kg beat 4 kg and 4 kg \"is detrimental to technique\" (PDF 276 image), and both balls are far above a match ball. **New error introduced by the round-1 wording and copied by the fixer:** When NOT says \"only the shot and baseball data fit it\" (a stay-close-to-competition-mass rule). The page gives the baseball masses: \"light (57 grams) and a heavy (184 gm) baseball ... a regulation ball is about 148 gm\" (PDF 276 / p.265, image). The heavy ball is +24% of regulation, the light ball is -61%, which is as far from competition mass as the counterexamples the same sentence lists; and Egstrom measured precision transfer, not the effect of closeness. These masses are on the page, so this is not outside-book knowledge. | In When NOT, replace \"only the shot and baseball data fit it, and the water polo result\" with \"only the shot data fit it (250 g vs 500 g differences); in the baseball study the heavy ball (184 g) is close to the regulation 148 g but the light ball (57 g) is far below it, and that study measured precision transfer, not closeness; the water polo result\". Keep the rest of the sentence. |\n\n## Part 2: spot checks of round-1 GO entries (6 of 11)\n\n| file | verdict | checked |\n|---|---|---|\n| principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities.md | GO | \"totally independent qualities\", \"does not necessarily enhance any of these three qualities\", \"(Verkhoshansky, 1963)\", \"inversely proportional to one another (see Ch 4)\", \"one of these qualities achieves greater development\" at PDF 268 / p.257 (image); baseline line 101 quote; Example labelled Inference. |\n| heuristic--analytical-synthetic-and-multifaceted-preparation-suit-only-early-training.md | GO | analytical-synthetic definition, Verkhoshansky 1977 examples, \"appropriate only for the initial stages ... use them casually\", \"partly true and only under certain circumstances\", \"quantitative analysis of the interrelation among all the factors involved\", Yakovlev 1968 / Zimkin 1965 / Zatsiorsky 1965, \"inevitably opposes the law of gradual development\", cross training \"(see 5.5)\", \"sequentiality, simultaneity or scheme of superposition\" at PDF 268 / p.257 (image); baseline line 182. |\n| framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise.md | GO | Table 5.2 every cell against PDF 274 / p.263 (image): 6RM 0-2 / 3-4 / 5-7 / 8-12 / 13+; 10RM 4-6 / 7-8 / 9-11 / 12-16 / 17+; 3RM 1-2 / 3-4 / 5-6 / 7+ (incr 5-10 kg); \"6-8 weeks\", ballistic-end quote. Table 5.1 cells, APRE regime labels, 2-4 min rest and the \"more than 6 ... must be increased\" contradiction at PDF 273 (text layer). Inference example (9 reps, +2.5-5 kg; 6 reps on set 4 holds) is consistent with the table. |\n| case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work.md | GO | \"38-40%\", 3 x 3 at 80% 1RM, 10 min rest, Fig 5.4 caption \"(Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)\" and curves (first effort about 42 to 49 cm, second about 50 to 67-69 cm, flattening after about movement 8) at PDF 276 (image); Table 5.3 all 18 cells and \"(Verkhoshansky, 1977)\" at PDF 277 (image). Recomputed: +38.6%, -6.0%, +4.8%, +11.6%, +33.9%, +47.3%; 0.62/0.266 = 2.33, 0.65/0.250 = 2.60; F x v = 12.9 and 19.3. All as the entry states. |\n| heuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-strength-tempo-rest-and-fatigue.md | GO | rest-interval determinants, \"0.5-1 minute\", \"favourable emotional state\", relaxation phases, \"inversely proportional\", Korobkov 1953 / Monogarov 1958, Diachkov 1961 \"approach the tempo ... and even exceed it\" at PDF 277 (image); water resistance and limb loads at PDF 277-278; fatigue rule and \"empirical methods\" at PDF 278 (image); baseline lines 96, 97. |\n| case-study--alternate-leg-bounding-with-thigh-cuffs-for-sprinters.md | GO | power greater than running, hip-flexion gathering cue, 50-100 m, 100-150 g cuffs \"can makes\", \"experienced athletes\", Zubov, third-person Verkhoshansky at PDF 278 / p.267 (image); baseline line 97. |\n\nAlso re-checked because the fixer edited it: `heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix.md`, GO. The added two-lever passage is verbatim from PDF 278 (image), both quoted bullets correct, and the arm-throw link fits the \"after-effect\" lever (PDF 276 introduces that experiment with the same phrase). Nit (optional): \"The section closes on two levers\" is loose, since the fatigue paragraph follows the bullets on PDF 278; \"The section's summary names two levers\" would be exact.\n\nNot re-checked this round (round-1 GO, untouched by the fixer): `framework--special-strength-methods-classified-by-sport-group-and-strength-type`, `claim--moderate-and-mixed-movement-tempo-for-repetition-strength-gains`, `heuristic--apre-skill-learning-6rm-pyramid-test-and-rm-muscle-testing`, `heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol`, `framework--two-groups-of-speed-strength-movements-and-their-tension-types`. They passed the mechanical checks below. In passing, the PDF 269 image confirms the four sport categories and three principles in the special-strength framework, and the PDF 271 image confirms the Lelikov 22.6 ± 0.6 / 16.3 ± 0.5 kg and Vasiliev 20/40/60/80% figures in the tempo entry.\n\n## Part 3: links and old slugs\n\n- Every `[[wikilink]]` in all 19 files resolves to a file in `staging/library` or `/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library` (scripted check, bare slug and book/slug forms).\n- Old slugs `claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves` and `claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass`: no match in `staging/library`, the vault library, or any staging scope file. The only staging hits are the round-1 review and the fix log, which is expected. Both new files exist; the scope file lists them.\n\n## Part 4: format, all 19 files\n\nScripted check: each of the six vault-form headers (`**What it is** —` through `**Related** —`) appears exactly once in every file; no em dash outside those headers (titles included); no `[[` without its `]]` on the same line; no review-history wording (searched for \"prior version\", \"previous version\", \"round\", \"corrected from\", \"renamed\", \"NO-GO finding\", \"fixed per\", \"earlier draft\"). All 19 pass.\n\n## Missing\n\nNothing new. Both round-1 gaps are now filled (1-3 reps sentence in Berger; two-lever summary in the loading entry).\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** One required fix:\n\n1. `claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md`, When NOT: replace \"only the shot and baseball data fit it\" as given above (the 57 g light baseball is -61% of the 148 g regulation ball on PDF 276; Egstrom measured precision).\n\nThe other 18 entries can be promoted as they stand. The single `proven` entry keeps its identity-only scope; both `contested` entries keep both sides on the cited pages. Optional nits (non-blocking): the Newton closing list should include (a); the Berger traceability sentence could carry \"Inference:\"; the loading entry's \"closes on two levers\" could read \"summary names two levers\".\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2218,"cache_read_input_tokens":152441,"output_tokens":5999,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":9},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2218},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":5999,"cache_read_input_tokens":152441,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2218,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2218,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_018HNP7Lf4Gjv23j13x5o5Mx":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch5a_opus_round2.md","content":"# Review round 2: Supertraining Ch 5a (5.1 to 5.2.3), falsification of the round-1 fixes\n\nReviewer: Claude Opus, independent of the writer and of the fixer. Date: 2026-10-03.\nScope: the 19 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch5a.txt`, folder `staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`. Round 1: `staging/review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md`. Fix log: `staging/fixes_supertraining_ch5a_applied.md`.\nSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx = printed + 11, PDF 266-278. Pages rendered to `staging/_renders/r2_5a_p266.png` ... `r2_5a_p278.png` (plus the crop `r2_5a_p272_ivanov.png`). Opened as images: PDF 267, 268, 269, 270, 271, 272 (Ivanov crop), 274, 276, 277, 278. PDF 266, 273 and 275 were checked from the text layer only (quotes, no numbers in dispute).\nBaseline: lines 61, 76, 96, 97, 101, 103, 104, 105, 107, 130, 182, 260 of `docs/method-baseline.md` re-read; every quote in the files I read matches the cited line, including the rewritten line-105 quote and its \"Inferred (transcript + shared slides)\" tag.\n\nCounts: **GO 18, FIX 1, NO-GO 0.**\n\nRound-1 count note: the round-1 header said \"FIX 8\" but its table has 7 FIX rows; item 8 of its fix list is the inbound-link update. The fixer's reading is correct.\n\n## Part 1: the 7 round-1 FIX entries, re-checked in full\n\n| file | verdict | finding (entry vs source, with page) | exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md | GO | All four round-1 problems are gone. Physics checked line by line against PDF 267 / p.256 (image): (1) `proven` scope is the identity only, \"∫F_net dt = mΔv, with Δv the change in velocity, not the book's ΔV\": correct for constant mass. (2) Why it works: integrating F_net = m dv/dt gives the impulse-momentum identity for any curve shape; \"for a jump from a still start, the take-off velocity fixes the net impulse\": correct. (3) When to apply: \"net propulsive impulse (vertical ground reaction force minus body weight, integrated over the propulsive phase, which starts at zero vertical velocity at the bottom of the countermovement)\": correct; net impulse over that phase = m.v_takeoff because v starts at 0. (4) (c): area under a force-time curve is that force's impulse and equals a change in momentum only for the net external force: correct; \"hold the load until fatigue forced them to stop\" is on the page. (5) (d): a projectile under constant gravity changes direction with a constant force, so a direction change needs a net force, not a change in force: correct, and it now counters the book's \"associated with a change in force\" sentence instead of confirming it. (6) (e): the book's integral runs 0 to ΔV and \"The final velocity reached at the end of this phase is ΔV\" (image): quoted correctly; \"m.ΔV equals the change in momentum only when the body starts from rest; in general the impulse of the net external force equals m(v_final - v_initial)\": correct. (a) and (b) unchanged and correct. Fixer disagreement 3 (net-force wording, zero-velocity start, \"ignoring air resistance\") is justified; each tightening makes the physics exact. No new error. | none. Nit (optional): When NOT opens \"Five sentences ... should not be quoted as physics\" but closes \"Do not use (c), (d) or (e) as stated\"; (a) is also not to be quoted, so \"(a), (c), (d) or (e)\" would be consistent. |\n| heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement.md | GO | \"The paragraph that closes 5.1's dynamics discussion, after Fig 5.1\" is now right: the neuromuscular paragraph sits below Fig 5.1 at the foot of PDF 267 and \"All subsequent training and performance errors ...\" opens PDF 268 (both images). \"Inference: this passage is the authors' own statement ...\" is labelled. All quotes re-verified on PDF 268 / p.257 image; the 5.2.3 \"more demanding\" quotes on PDF 277-278. Baseline line 103 quote matches. | none |\n| framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md | GO | (4) now separates the clear clash (90-95% with 3-6 reps vs 5RM \"about 89%\") from the loose one (85-95% = 3-5RM), labelled Inference (fixer disagreement 5 is right: it is reasoning, not the authors'). The logic holds: if 5RM is 89%, five or six reps at 90% or more is impossible. Numbers: \"about 89% and 74%\" PDF 269 (image); \"85-95% of 1RM, i.e. 3-5RM\", \"more than three (Berger, 1962)\", \"5-6 exercises for 6-10 sets of 1-3\" PDF 271 (image); \"90-95% of 1RM\" / \"(3-6)\", \"reducing the number of sets and repetitions\", \"Similar significant increases\" PDF 272 (image). The line-105 fragment is now a full sentence with the \"Eric's baseline:\" prefix and a verbatim quote of line 105. Ivanov link points to the new slug and resolves. | none |\n| claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared.md | GO | \"mostly in rehabilitation or untrained subjects\" is gone; replacement says the book gives no populations and labels the rehabilitation point Inference. Re-verified (images): 89%/74%, DeLorme 50%/75%/10RM (Hettinger, 1961) PDF 269; Zinovieff 1951, McCloy 1954, \"add nothing new\", 154/142, 212.8/186 (Faulkner, 1950), 5.5%, McMorris & Elks 1954, Krusen 25/50/75% vs 5RM/125%/150% PDF 270; Narikashvili 1960 PDF 271. | none |\n| claim--berger-sets-reps-and-frequency-studies-in-novices.md | GO | Traceability sentence now matches the page: conclusion 1's comparator and conclusion 5 trace to Berger 1963 (\"10RM for one set three times per week\"; \"no major difference between ... 2 or 3 training sessions per week\", PDF 270 image); conclusion 6 has no data. Fixer disagreement 4 is justified: conclusion 5 reads \"3 sets of the 10RM\" (PDF 271 image) while the Berger 1963 sentence says \"one set\" (PDF 270 image). The added 1-3 rep sentence is verbatim from PDF 270 (image), placed correctly after Vorobyev 1971 (PDF 270), with Vorobyev 1978 \"1-6\" on PDF 271. All five programmes, six conclusions, the italic novice caveat and the recovery quote re-verified on the images. | none. Nit (optional): \"conclusion 3 traces loosely to ...\" is writer reasoning without an \"Inference:\" prefix, unlike the parallel sentence in the three-methods entry. |\n| claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves.md | GO | New title matches the data (PDF 272 crop image): eccentric 15 kg largest squat 1RM gain, isometric 9.2 smallest; isometric 30.2 kg largest isometric gain, concentric 14.6 smallest; vertical jump +3.7 cm concentric only, -1.6 eccentric, -5.4 isometric. The \"trains the nervous system for that mode\" gloss is now labelled Inference with \"the book does not say this\". File renamed; body has no review-history wording. | none |\n| claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md | FIX | Title, When to apply and the Bondarchuk framing are fixed and correct (Bondarchuk sentence confirmed verbatim in the linked vault entry `heuristic--implement-weight-dosing-and-throwing-boom-warning`, line 23). Fixer disagreements 1 and 2 are justified by the page: Egstrom transfer is of \"precision\", and shot transfer \"apparently is possible only by alternating\" (PDF 276-277 images), so \"Can Run One Way\" is the right strength; water polo 2 kg beat 4 kg and 4 kg \"is detrimental to technique\" (PDF 276 image), and both balls are far above a match ball. **New error introduced by the round-1 wording and copied by the fixer:** When NOT says \"only the shot and baseball data fit it\" (a stay-close-to-competition-mass rule). The page gives the baseball masses: \"light (57 grams) and a heavy (184 gm) baseball ... a regulation ball is about 148 gm\" (PDF 276 / p.265, image). The heavy ball is +24% of regulation, the light ball is -61%, which is as far from competition mass as the counterexamples the same sentence lists; and Egstrom measured precision transfer, not the effect of closeness. These masses are on the page, so this is not outside-book knowledge. | In When NOT, replace \"only the shot and baseball data fit it, and the water polo result\" with \"only the shot data fit it (250 g vs 500 g differences); in the baseball study the heavy ball (184 g) is close to the regulation 148 g but the light ball (57 g) is far below it, and that study measured precision transfer, not closeness; the water polo result\". Keep the rest of the sentence. |\n\n## Part 2: spot checks of round-1 GO entries (6 of 11)\n\n| file | verdict | checked |\n|---|---|---|\n| principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities.md | GO | \"totally independent qualities\", \"does not necessarily enhance any of these three qualities\", \"(Verkhoshansky, 1963)\", \"inversely proportional to one another (see Ch 4)\", \"one of these qualities achieves greater development\" at PDF 268 / p.257 (image); baseline line 101 quote; Example labelled Inference. |\n| heuristic--analytical-synthetic-and-multifaceted-preparation-suit-only-early-training.md | GO | analytical-synthetic definition, Verkhoshansky 1977 examples, \"appropriate only for the initial stages ... use them casually\", \"partly true and only under certain circumstances\", \"quantitative analysis of the interrelation among all the factors involved\", Yakovlev 1968 / Zimkin 1965 / Zatsiorsky 1965, \"inevitably opposes the law of gradual development\", cross training \"(see 5.5)\", \"sequentiality, simultaneity or scheme of superposition\" at PDF 268 / p.257 (image); baseline line 182. |\n| framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise.md | GO | Table 5.2 every cell against PDF 274 / p.263 (image): 6RM 0-2 / 3-4 / 5-7 / 8-12 / 13+; 10RM 4-6 / 7-8 / 9-11 / 12-16 / 17+; 3RM 1-2 / 3-4 / 5-6 / 7+ (incr 5-10 kg); \"6-8 weeks\", ballistic-end quote. Table 5.1 cells, APRE regime labels, 2-4 min rest and the \"more than 6 ... must be increased\" contradiction at PDF 273 (text layer). Inference example (9 reps, +2.5-5 kg; 6 reps on set 4 holds) is consistent with the table. |\n| case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work.md | GO | \"38-40%\", 3 x 3 at 80% 1RM, 10 min rest, Fig 5.4 caption \"(Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)\" and curves (first effort about 42 to 49 cm, second about 50 to 67-69 cm, flattening after about movement 8) at PDF 276 (image); Table 5.3 all 18 cells and \"(Verkhoshansky, 1977)\" at PDF 277 (image). Recomputed: +38.6%, -6.0%, +4.8%, +11.6%, +33.9%, +47.3%; 0.62/0.266 = 2.33, 0.65/0.250 = 2.60; F x v = 12.9 and 19.3. All as the entry states. |\n| heuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-strength-tempo-rest-and-fatigue.md | GO | rest-interval determinants, \"0.5-1 minute\", \"favourable emotional state\", relaxation phases, \"inversely proportional\", Korobkov 1953 / Monogarov 1958, Diachkov 1961 \"approach the tempo ... and even exceed it\" at PDF 277 (image); water resistance and limb loads at PDF 277-278; fatigue rule and \"empirical methods\" at PDF 278 (image); baseline lines 96, 97. |\n| case-study--alternate-leg-bounding-with-thigh-cuffs-for-sprinters.md | GO | power greater than running, hip-flexion gathering cue, 50-100 m, 100-150 g cuffs \"can makes\", \"experienced athletes\", Zubov, third-person Verkhoshansky at PDF 278 / p.267 (image); baseline line 97. |\n\nAlso re-checked because the fixer edited it: `heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix.md`, GO. The added two-lever passage is verbatim from PDF 278 (image), both quoted bullets correct, and the arm-throw link fits the \"after-effect\" lever (PDF 276 introduces that experiment with the same phrase). Nit (optional): \"The section closes on two levers\" is loose, since the fatigue paragraph follows the bullets on PDF 278; \"The section's summary names two levers\" would be exact.\n\nNot re-checked this round (round-1 GO, untouched by the fixer): `framework--special-strength-methods-classified-by-sport-group-and-strength-type`, `claim--moderate-and-mixed-movement-tempo-for-repetition-strength-gains`, `heuristic--apre-skill-learning-6rm-pyramid-test-and-rm-muscle-testing`, `heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol`, `framework--two-groups-of-speed-strength-movements-and-their-tension-types`. They passed the mechanical checks below. In passing, the PDF 269 image confirms the four sport categories and three principles in the special-strength framework, and the PDF 271 image confirms the Lelikov 22.6 ± 0.6 / 16.3 ± 0.5 kg and Vasiliev 20/40/60/80% figures in the tempo entry.\n\n## Part 3: links and old slugs\n\n- Every `[[wikilink]]` in all 19 files resolves to a file in `staging/library` or `/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library` (scripted check, bare slug and book/slug forms).\n- Old slugs `claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves` and `claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass`: no match in `staging/library`, the vault library, or any staging scope file. The only staging hits are the round-1 review and the fix log, which is expected. Both new files exist; the scope file lists them.\n\n## Part 4: format, all 19 files\n\nScripted check: each of the six vault-form headers (`**What it is** —` through `**Related** —`) appears exactly once in every file; no em dash outside those headers (titles included); no `[[` without its `]]` on the same line; no review-history wording (searched for \"prior version\", \"previous version\", \"round\", \"corrected from\", \"renamed\", \"NO-GO finding\", \"fixed per\", \"earlier draft\"). All 19 pass.\n\n## Missing\n\nNothing new. Both round-1 gaps are now filled (1-3 reps sentence in Berger; two-lever summary in the loading entry).\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** One required fix:\n\n1. `claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md`, When NOT: replace \"only the shot and baseball data fit it\" as given above (the 57 g light baseball is -61% of the 148 g regulation ball on PDF 276; Egstrom measured precision).\n\nThe other 18 entries can be promoted as they stand. The single `proven` entry keeps its identity-only scope; both `contested` entries keep both sides on the cited pages. Optional nits (non-blocking): the Newton closing list should include (a); the Berger traceability sentence could carry \"Inference:\"; the loading entry's \"closes on two levers\" could read \"summary names two levers\".\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffcbBLQDrh3zg3vtcDwp","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"c5d025b4-9cea-4a8e-8d96-976cc8cf59ea","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:25:31.325Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"c5d025b4-9cea-4a8e-8d96-976cc8cf59ea","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"685d1925-9566-4313-ae7b-41bf0a7b0668","agentId":"a943b4c4c92ae7c73","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018HNP7Lf4Gjv23j13x5o5Mx","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch5a_opus_round2.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"cceaa9db-4d35-46b5-af3f-59aa049f0f32","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:25:31.425Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch5a_opus_round2.md","content":"# Review round 2: Supertraining Ch 5a (5.1 to 5.2.3), falsification of the round-1 fixes\n\nReviewer: Claude Opus, independent of the writer and of the fixer. Date: 2026-10-03.\nScope: the 19 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch5a.txt`, folder `staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`. Round 1: `staging/review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md`. Fix log: `staging/fixes_supertraining_ch5a_applied.md`.\nSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx = printed + 11, PDF 266-278. Pages rendered to `staging/_renders/r2_5a_p266.png` ... `r2_5a_p278.png` (plus the crop `r2_5a_p272_ivanov.png`). Opened as images: PDF 267, 268, 269, 270, 271, 272 (Ivanov crop), 274, 276, 277, 278. PDF 266, 273 and 275 were checked from the text layer only (quotes, no numbers in dispute).\nBaseline: lines 61, 76, 96, 97, 101, 103, 104, 105, 107, 130, 182, 260 of `docs/method-baseline.md` re-read; every quote in the files I read matches the cited line, including the rewritten line-105 quote and its \"Inferred (transcript + shared slides)\" tag.\n\nCounts: **GO 18, FIX 1, NO-GO 0.**\n\nRound-1 count note: the round-1 header said \"FIX 8\" but its table has 7 FIX rows; item 8 of its fix list is the inbound-link update. The fixer's reading is correct.\n\n## Part 1: the 7 round-1 FIX entries, re-checked in full\n\n| file | verdict | finding (entry vs source, with page) | exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve.md | GO | All four round-1 problems are gone. Physics checked line by line against PDF 267 / p.256 (image): (1) `proven` scope is the identity only, \"∫F_net dt = mΔv, with Δv the change in velocity, not the book's ΔV\": correct for constant mass. (2) Why it works: integrating F_net = m dv/dt gives the impulse-momentum identity for any curve shape; \"for a jump from a still start, the take-off velocity fixes the net impulse\": correct. (3) When to apply: \"net propulsive impulse (vertical ground reaction force minus body weight, integrated over the propulsive phase, which starts at zero vertical velocity at the bottom of the countermovement)\": correct; net impulse over that phase = m.v_takeoff because v starts at 0. (4) (c): area under a force-time curve is that force's impulse and equals a change in momentum only for the net external force: correct; \"hold the load until fatigue forced them to stop\" is on the page. (5) (d): a projectile under constant gravity changes direction with a constant force, so a direction change needs a net force, not a change in force: correct, and it now counters the book's \"associated with a change in force\" sentence instead of confirming it. (6) (e): the book's integral runs 0 to ΔV and \"The final velocity reached at the end of this phase is ΔV\" (image): quoted correctly; \"m.ΔV equals the change in momentum only when the body starts from rest; in general the impulse of the net external force equals m(v_final - v_initial)\": correct. (a) and (b) unchanged and correct. Fixer disagreement 3 (net-force wording, zero-velocity start, \"ignoring air resistance\") is justified; each tightening makes the physics exact. No new error. | none. Nit (optional): When NOT opens \"Five sentences ... should not be quoted as physics\" but closes \"Do not use (c), (d) or (e) as stated\"; (a) is also not to be quoted, so \"(a), (c), (d) or (e)\" would be consistent. |\n| heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement.md | GO | \"The paragraph that closes 5.1's dynamics discussion, after Fig 5.1\" is now right: the neuromuscular paragraph sits below Fig 5.1 at the foot of PDF 267 and \"All subsequent training and performance errors ...\" opens PDF 268 (both images). \"Inference: this passage is the authors' own statement ...\" is labelled. All quotes re-verified on PDF 268 / p.257 image; the 5.2.3 \"more demanding\" quotes on PDF 277-278. Baseline line 103 quote matches. | none |\n| framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md | GO | (4) now separates the clear clash (90-95% with 3-6 reps vs 5RM \"about 89%\") from the loose one (85-95% = 3-5RM), labelled Inference (fixer disagreement 5 is right: it is reasoning, not the authors'). The logic holds: if 5RM is 89%, five or six reps at 90% or more is impossible. Numbers: \"about 89% and 74%\" PDF 269 (image); \"85-95% of 1RM, i.e. 3-5RM\", \"more than three (Berger, 1962)\", \"5-6 exercises for 6-10 sets of 1-3\" PDF 271 (image); \"90-95% of 1RM\" / \"(3-6)\", \"reducing the number of sets and repetitions\", \"Similar significant increases\" PDF 272 (image). The line-105 fragment is now a full sentence with the \"Eric's baseline:\" prefix and a verbatim quote of line 105. Ivanov link points to the new slug and resolves. | none |\n| claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared.md | GO | \"mostly in rehabilitation or untrained subjects\" is gone; replacement says the book gives no populations and labels the rehabilitation point Inference. Re-verified (images): 89%/74%, DeLorme 50%/75%/10RM (Hettinger, 1961) PDF 269; Zinovieff 1951, McCloy 1954, \"add nothing new\", 154/142, 212.8/186 (Faulkner, 1950), 5.5%, McMorris & Elks 1954, Krusen 25/50/75% vs 5RM/125%/150% PDF 270; Narikashvili 1960 PDF 271. | none |\n| claim--berger-sets-reps-and-frequency-studies-in-novices.md | GO | Traceability sentence now matches the page: conclusion 1's comparator and conclusion 5 trace to Berger 1963 (\"10RM for one set three times per week\"; \"no major difference between ... 2 or 3 training sessions per week\", PDF 270 image); conclusion 6 has no data. Fixer disagreement 4 is justified: conclusion 5 reads \"3 sets of the 10RM\" (PDF 271 image) while the Berger 1963 sentence says \"one set\" (PDF 270 image). The added 1-3 rep sentence is verbatim from PDF 270 (image), placed correctly after Vorobyev 1971 (PDF 270), with Vorobyev 1978 \"1-6\" on PDF 271. All five programmes, six conclusions, the italic novice caveat and the recovery quote re-verified on the images. | none. Nit (optional): \"conclusion 3 traces loosely to ...\" is writer reasoning without an \"Inference:\" prefix, unlike the parallel sentence in the three-methods entry. |\n| claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves.md | GO | New title matches the data (PDF 272 crop image): eccentric 15 kg largest squat 1RM gain, isometric 9.2 smallest; isometric 30.2 kg largest isometric gain, concentric 14.6 smallest; vertical jump +3.7 cm concentric only, -1.6 eccentric, -5.4 isometric. The \"trains the nervous system for that mode\" gloss is now labelled Inference with \"the book does not say this\". File renamed; body has no review-history wording. | none |\n| claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md | FIX | Title, When to apply and the Bondarchuk framing are fixed and correct (Bondarchuk sentence confirmed verbatim in the linked vault entry `heuristic--implement-weight-dosing-and-throwing-boom-warning`, line 23). Fixer disagreements 1 and 2 are justified by the page: Egstrom transfer is of \"precision\", and shot transfer \"apparently is possible only by alternating\" (PDF 276-277 images), so \"Can Run One Way\" is the right strength; water polo 2 kg beat 4 kg and 4 kg \"is detrimental to technique\" (PDF 276 image), and both balls are far above a match ball. **New error introduced by the round-1 wording and copied by the fixer:** When NOT says \"only the shot and baseball data fit it\" (a stay-close-to-competition-mass rule). The page gives the baseball masses: \"light (57 grams) and a heavy (184 gm) baseball ... a regulation ball is about 148 gm\" (PDF 276 / p.265, image). The heavy ball is +24% of regulation, the light ball is -61%, which is as far from competition mass as the counterexamples the same sentence lists; and Egstrom measured precision transfer, not the effect of closeness. These masses are on the page, so this is not outside-book knowledge. | In When NOT, replace \"only the shot and baseball data fit it, and the water polo result\" with \"only the shot data fit it (250 g vs 500 g differences); in the baseball study the heavy ball (184 g) is close to the regulation 148 g but the light ball (57 g) is far below it, and that study measured precision transfer, not closeness; the water polo result\". Keep the rest of the sentence. |\n\n## Part 2: spot checks of round-1 GO entries (6 of 11)\n\n| file | verdict | checked |\n|---|---|---|\n| principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities.md | GO | \"totally independent qualities\", \"does not necessarily enhance any of these three qualities\", \"(Verkhoshansky, 1963)\", \"inversely proportional to one another (see Ch 4)\", \"one of these qualities achieves greater development\" at PDF 268 / p.257 (image); baseline line 101 quote; Example labelled Inference. |\n| heuristic--analytical-synthetic-and-multifaceted-preparation-suit-only-early-training.md | GO | analytical-synthetic definition, Verkhoshansky 1977 examples, \"appropriate only for the initial stages ... use them casually\", \"partly true and only under certain circumstances\", \"quantitative analysis of the interrelation among all the factors involved\", Yakovlev 1968 / Zimkin 1965 / Zatsiorsky 1965, \"inevitably opposes the law of gradual development\", cross training \"(see 5.5)\", \"sequentiality, simultaneity or scheme of superposition\" at PDF 268 / p.257 (image); baseline line 182. |\n| framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise.md | GO | Table 5.2 every cell against PDF 274 / p.263 (image): 6RM 0-2 / 3-4 / 5-7 / 8-12 / 13+; 10RM 4-6 / 7-8 / 9-11 / 12-16 / 17+; 3RM 1-2 / 3-4 / 5-6 / 7+ (incr 5-10 kg); \"6-8 weeks\", ballistic-end quote. Table 5.1 cells, APRE regime labels, 2-4 min rest and the \"more than 6 ... must be increased\" contradiction at PDF 273 (text layer). Inference example (9 reps, +2.5-5 kg; 6 reps on set 4 holds) is consistent with the table. |\n| case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work.md | GO | \"38-40%\", 3 x 3 at 80% 1RM, 10 min rest, Fig 5.4 caption \"(Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)\" and curves (first effort about 42 to 49 cm, second about 50 to 67-69 cm, flattening after about movement 8) at PDF 276 (image); Table 5.3 all 18 cells and \"(Verkhoshansky, 1977)\" at PDF 277 (image). Recomputed: +38.6%, -6.0%, +4.8%, +11.6%, +33.9%, +47.3%; 0.62/0.266 = 2.33, 0.65/0.250 = 2.60; F x v = 12.9 and 19.3. All as the entry states. |\n| heuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-strength-tempo-rest-and-fatigue.md | GO | rest-interval determinants, \"0.5-1 minute\", \"favourable emotional state\", relaxation phases, \"inversely proportional\", Korobkov 1953 / Monogarov 1958, Diachkov 1961 \"approach the tempo ... and even exceed it\" at PDF 277 (image); water resistance and limb loads at PDF 277-278; fatigue rule and \"empirical methods\" at PDF 278 (image); baseline lines 96, 97. |\n| case-study--alternate-leg-bounding-with-thigh-cuffs-for-sprinters.md | GO | power greater than running, hip-flexion gathering cue, 50-100 m, 100-150 g cuffs \"can makes\", \"experienced athletes\", Zubov, third-person Verkhoshansky at PDF 278 / p.267 (image); baseline line 97. |\n\nAlso re-checked because the fixer edited it: `heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix.md`, GO. The added two-lever passage is verbatim from PDF 278 (image), both quoted bullets correct, and the arm-throw link fits the \"after-effect\" lever (PDF 276 introduces that experiment with the same phrase). Nit (optional): \"The section closes on two levers\" is loose, since the fatigue paragraph follows the bullets on PDF 278; \"The section's summary names two levers\" would be exact.\n\nNot re-checked this round (round-1 GO, untouched by the fixer): `framework--special-strength-methods-classified-by-sport-group-and-strength-type`, `claim--moderate-and-mixed-movement-tempo-for-repetition-strength-gains`, `heuristic--apre-skill-learning-6rm-pyramid-test-and-rm-muscle-testing`, `heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol`, `framework--two-groups-of-speed-strength-movements-and-their-tension-types`. They passed the mechanical checks below. In passing, the PDF 269 image confirms the four sport categories and three principles in the special-strength framework, and the PDF 271 image confirms the Lelikov 22.6 ± 0.6 / 16.3 ± 0.5 kg and Vasiliev 20/40/60/80% figures in the tempo entry.\n\n## Part 3: links and old slugs\n\n- Every `[[wikilink]]` in all 19 files resolves to a file in `staging/library` or `/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library` (scripted check, bare slug and book/slug forms).\n- Old slugs `claim--ivanov-1966-training-mode-decides-which-strength-test-improves` and `claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-stay-close-to-competition-mass`: no match in `staging/library`, the vault library, or any staging scope file. The only staging hits are the round-1 review and the fix log, which is expected. Both new files exist; the scope file lists them.\n\n## Part 4: format, all 19 files\n\nScripted check: each of the six vault-form headers (`**What it is** —` through `**Related** —`) appears exactly once in every file; no em dash outside those headers (titles included); no `[[` without its `]]` on the same line; no review-history wording (searched for \"prior version\", \"previous version\", \"round\", \"corrected from\", \"renamed\", \"NO-GO finding\", \"fixed per\", \"earlier draft\"). All 19 pass.\n\n## Missing\n\nNothing new. Both round-1 gaps are now filled (1-3 reps sentence in Berger; two-lever summary in the loading entry).\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** One required fix:\n\n1. `claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete.md`, When NOT: replace \"only the shot and baseball data fit it\" as given above (the 57 g light baseball is -61% of the 148 g regulation ball on PDF 276; Egstrom measured precision).\n\nThe other 18 entries can be promoted as they stand. The single `proven` entry keeps its identity-only scope; both `contested` entries keep both sides on the cited pages. Optional nits (non-blocking): the Newton closing list should include (a); the Berger traceability sentence could carry \"Inference:\"; the loading entry's \"closes on two levers\" could read \"summary names two levers\".\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c5d025b4-9cea-4a8e-8d96-976cc8cf59ea","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
